= Quantum chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Electron density
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{title2=$n(\mathbf r)$}

The electron density describes the expected number of <electrons> per unit <volume> at position $\mathbf r$. For a system with a fixed number $N$ of <electrons>, $\int n(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r=N$. A <Lewis structure> allocates electron counts without specifying this spatial density.

= Electron configuration
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

An <electron configuration> records the occupation of <electron shells> and <electron subshells> by <electrons>. For neutral <sodium> in its <ground state>, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6\,3s^1$ gives $2+2+6+1=11$ <electrons>; adding the occupations within each <electron shell> gives $(2,8,1)$. A superscript counts <electrons>, rather than multiplying the preceding labels. Removing the outer <electron> gives the <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ with $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6$, or $(2,8)$. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electron configurations of atoms and ions]{ref}.

= Electron shell
{parent=Electron configuration}
{title2=$n$}
{wiki}

An <electron shell> collects the allowed electronic <quantum states> sharing a <principal quantum number> $n$. Its occupancy is the number of <electrons> assigned to those states in an <electron configuration>. An <electron shell> is not a material surface or a prescribed orbit.

= Electron subshell
{parent=Electron shell}

An <electron subshell> groups the allowed electronic <quantum states> within an <electron shell> by their <orbital angular momentum> quantum number $\ell$. Labels $s$ and $p$ mean $\ell=0$ and $\ell=1$, respectively; their capacities are two and six <electrons>. The leading number in $2p$ identifies the <electron shell>, while $p$ identifies the <electron subshell>.

= Atomic orbital
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\phi_{n\ell m}(\mathbf r)$}

An atomic orbital is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> used to describe an <electron>'s state in a <chemical atom>. For a one-electron <hydrogen atom>, orbitals can be exact <energy eigenstates>; descriptions of many-electron atoms instead use an orbital approximation. A normalized atomic orbital has $\int|\phi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$, and the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\phi|^2$. It is a function extending through space, not a prescribed classical orbit. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-3-development-of-quantum-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 6.3: atomic orbitals and the wavefunction description]{ref}

The <linear combination of atomic orbitals> combines these single-centre functions to approximate a <molecular orbital>. The <orbital overlap> measures their <inner product>, and their relative <quantum phase> controls the <interference> shown in <molecular orbital theory>.

= Molecular orbital theory
{parent=Quantum chemistry}
{wiki}

<Molecular orbital theory> describes the electronic arrangement of a <molecule> using <molecular orbitals>: one-<electron> spatial <wavefunctions> extending over the combined <atomic nuclei>. Filling <bonding molecular orbitals> can stabilize a <chemical bond>; filling <antibonding molecular orbitals> opposes that stabilization. A <molecular orbital> is a building block of a many-<electron> description, rather than the entire many-<electron> <wavefunction>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf[Robert W. Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 1–4: molecular orbitals and the LCAO approximation]{ref}.

First make the <interference> visible with an exactly normalized one-dimensional toy. Replace the three-dimensional $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom> by two <Gaussian functions> centred at $x=\pm a$, with <dimensionless> $x$ and $a>0$:
$$
\phi_A(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x+a)^2/2},\qquad
\phi_B(x)=\pi^{-1/4}e^{-(x-a)^2/2},\qquad
S=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\phi_A\phi_B\,dx=e^{-a^2}.
$$
The normalized sum and difference are $\psi_\pm=(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)/\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}$, following the same <linear combination of atomic orbitals> algebra. At $a=1$, the <orbital overlap> is $S=e^{-1}\approx0.367879$. At the midpoint, $\phi_A(0)=\phi_B(0)\approx0.455581$, giving
$$
\psi_+(0)\approx0.550879,\qquad |\psi_+(0)|^2\approx0.303468,\qquad
\psi_-(0)=|\psi_-(0)|^2=0.
$$
Both <probability densities> have total <integral> one: <interference> redistributes <probability>, rather than creating an extra <electron>. These are toy <wavefunctions>, not actual <hydrogen atom> orbitals or predictions of <molecular hydrogen> <energies>.

\Image[molecular-orbital-interference.png]
{title=Normalized one-dimensional <Gaussian function> toy: signed <probability amplitudes> add or cancel before their squares give <probability densities>}
{description=Centres at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ give <orbital overlap> $S=e^{-1}$. The difference has a zero at the midpoint; its negative lobe does not carry positive <electric charge>. All density curves shown are normalized on the line, rather than in three-dimensional space.}

Now fill the two $1s$-derived <molecular orbitals> in the minimal model. The lower <bonding molecular orbital> is labelled $\sigma_g$; the upper <antibonding molecular orbital> is $\sigma_u^*$. The <Pauli exclusion principle> permits at most two <electrons> in each spatial <molecular orbital>, with opposite <spin> projections along a chosen axis. For the lowest <electron configuration> in this model, fill $\sigma_g$ first. The subscripts $g$ and $u$ denote <even> and <odd> behaviour under inversion through the midpoint; the star marks an <antibonding molecular orbital>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 8.4: occupancy and bond order]{ref} and https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–3: bonding, antibonding, and symmetry labels]{ref}.

With $N_b$ <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> and $N_a$ in <antibonding molecular orbitals>, the model's <bond order> is $b=(N_b-N_a)/2$. The three elementary counts are:
* <Molecular hydrogen>, $\mathrm H_2$: two <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2$ and $b=(2-0)/2=1$.
* The <hydrogen> molecular <ion> $\mathrm H_2^+$: one <electron> gives $(\sigma_g)^1$ and $b=(1-0)/2=1/2$.
* Two neutral <helium> <chemical atoms>, $\mathrm{He}_2$: four <electrons> give $(\sigma_g)^2(\sigma_u^*)^2$ and $b=(2-2)/2=0$ in this minimal $1s$ model.

\Image[molecular-orbital-occupancy.png]
{title=Schematic <molecular orbital> occupancy for $\mathrm H_2$, $\mathrm H_2^+$, and $\mathrm{He}_2$: <bond orders> one, one half, and zero}
{description=Each horizontal line is one spatial <molecular orbital>; paired arrows represent opposite <spin> projections. Vertical positions show ordering only, with arbitrary gaps rather than measured <energies> or a common scale across species.}

The zero <bond order> says this minimal model gives no net <covalent bond> for $\mathrm{He}_2$. It does not rule out every bound pair of <helium> <chemical atoms>: a very weak <van der Waals force> can bind a neutral <helium> dimer, which has been observed experimentally. See https://www.atom.uni-frankfurt.de/publications/files/ZellerPNAS-2016.pdf[Zeller et al., Imaging the He2 quantum halo state using a free electron laser, PNAS 113, 14651–14655 (2016), p. 14651: van der Waals binding]{ref} and https://scholarworks.gvsu.edu/chm_articles/6/[Luo et al., The weakest bond: Experimental observation of helium dimer, Journal of Chemical Physics 98, 3564–3567 (1993)]{ref}. The <bond order> count does not include that weak binding mechanism.

The useful surprise is that sharing alone does not determine a <chemical bond>: the relative <quantum phase> and the occupancy matter together. The same two shapes explain why adding an <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> increases the model's <bond order>, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> decreases it. <Molecular orbital theory> also accounts for the unpaired <electrons> and <paramagnetism> of <molecular oxygen>, which its all-paired <Lewis structure> misses. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Chemistry 2e, 8.4: the molecular orbital description of oxygen]{ref}.

= Molecular orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$\psi(\mathbf r)$}
{wiki}

A <molecular orbital> is a one-<electron> spatial <wavefunction> for the combined <atomic nuclei> of a <molecule>. For a normalized <molecular orbital>, the <Born rule> gives the position <probability density> $|\psi(\mathbf r)|^2$, with $\int_{\mathbb R^3}|\psi|^2\,d^3\mathbf r=1$. The <molecular orbital> is the amplitude, rather than its squared <modulus> or a classical trajectory.

In the independent-orbital approximation, a doubly occupied <molecular orbital> contributes $2|\psi|^2$ to the <electron> number density, whose <integral> is two. An empty <molecular orbital> contributes zero, even though its shape is still part of the model. The <molecular hydrogen> example therefore uses one occupied spatial <molecular orbital> for two <electrons>, distinguished by their <spins>. A negative real <probability amplitude> means a relative <quantum phase> of $\pi$, not positive <electric charge>; multiplying the whole <wavefunction> by $-1$ changes only its <global phase>.

= Bonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_g$}
{wiki}

= Bonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying a <bonding molecular orbital> favours a <chemical bond>. For the two equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the sum $\sigma_g=\sigma_+$ has constructive <interference> between the <protons>. The resulting <electron> density in that region helps stabilize the combined system. Its lower <energy> in this model follows from the <quantum Hamiltonian>, not from the plus sign alone; see https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2]{ref}.

For example, $(\sigma_g)^2$ in <molecular hydrogen> supplies $N_b=2$, hence <bond order> one when $N_a=0$. The sum in the <molecular orbital theory> toy has midpoint <probability density> $0.303468$, whereas the difference has zero there. The normalization factors must be included in this comparison.

= Antibonding orbital
{parent=Molecular orbital}
{title2=$\sigma_u^*$}
{wiki}

= Antibonding molecular orbital
{synonym}

Occupying an <antibonding molecular orbital> opposes a <chemical bond>. For equivalent $1s$ <atomic orbitals> of the <hydrogen atom>, the difference $\sigma_u^*=\sigma_-$ vanishes on the plane halfway between the <protons>: every point there is equally far from both centres, so the amplitudes cancel. This nodal plane removes the contribution of this <molecular orbital> to <electron> density on that plane; it does not make an entire finite region empty. The $1s$-derived <antibonding molecular orbital> lies above its <bonding molecular orbital> partner in <energy>. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/e9f9413400be87f6a0a512e757d52e3c_MIT5_61F17_lec25.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 25, pp. 1–2]{ref}.

In the $\mathrm{He}_2$ minimal model, $(\sigma_u^*)^2$ adds $N_a=2$ and cancels the <bond order> contribution of $(\sigma_g)^2$. Opposite signs on the two lobes describe <probability amplitudes>, while both lobes have nonnegative <probability density> after squaring. The star is an orbital label, not an extra <electron> or an <electric charge>.

= Linear combination of atomic orbitals
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{wiki}

= LCAO
{c}
{synonym}

The <LCAO> approximation builds a <molecular orbital> as a <linear combination> of single-centre <atomic orbitals>. For two equivalent normalized real $1s$ functions $\phi_A$ and $\phi_B$, choose their signs positive and define their <orbital overlap> $S$. At nonzero separation, $0<S<1$, and the normalized combinations are
$$
\sigma_\pm(\mathbf r)=\frac{\phi_A(\mathbf r)\pm\phi_B(\mathbf r)}{\sqrt{2(1\pm S)}},\qquad
S=\int_{\mathbb R^3}\phi_A(\mathbf r)\phi_B(\mathbf r)\,d^3\mathbf r.
$$
Here $\sigma_+=\sigma_g$ and $\sigma_-=\sigma_u^*$. Equal coefficients use the equivalence of the two centres; a general <LCAO> need not have equal coefficients. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/ef9da1132ff6233d732a1d6115099467_MIT5_61F17_lec24.pdf[Field, MIT 5.61, lecture 24, pp. 3–6: LCAO coefficients and overlap integrals]{ref}.

For the <Gaussian function> toy's $a=1$, the two denominators are $\sqrt{2(1+S)}\approx1.654013$ and $\sqrt{2(1-S)}\approx1.124385$. To check <wavefunction normalization>, expand the square: $\int(\phi_A\pm\phi_B)^2=1+1\pm2S$. Dividing by $2(1\pm S)$ gives one. The local <probability density> is
$$
|\sigma_\pm|^2=\frac{\phi_A^2+\phi_B^2\pm2\phi_A\phi_B}{2(1\pm S)}.
$$
The cross term is the <interference>; adding the separate densities first would lose it. These formulas normalize the chosen <linear combination> exactly, while the restricted choice of <atomic orbitals> remains an approximation to an actual <molecular orbital>.

= Orbital overlap
{parent=Linear combination of atomic orbitals}
{title2=$S$}

The <orbital overlap> of normalized real <atomic orbitals> is their <inner product> $S=\int\phi_A\phi_B$. It is a <dimensionless> measure of their common amplitude, not an <electron> count. For the <Gaussian function> toy, increasing $a$ from $1$ to $2$ reduces $S$ from $e^{-1}\approx0.367879$ to $e^{-4}\approx0.018316$. As $S$ tends to zero, both <LCAO> normalization denominators tend to $\sqrt2$.

= Bond order
{parent=Molecular orbital theory}
{title2=$b=(N_b-N_a)/2$}
{wiki}

In the elementary <molecular orbital theory> count, <bond order> is half the excess of <electrons> in <bonding molecular orbitals> over those in <antibonding molecular orbitals>. Thus adding one <electron> to a <bonding molecular orbital> raises $b$ by $1/2$, while adding one to an <antibonding molecular orbital> lowers it by $1/2$. The configurations drawn above give $1$, $1/2$, and $0$. This count summarizes net <covalent bond> occupancy; it is neither a measured <energy> nor a universal test for every kind of binding. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Chemistry 2e, 8.4: molecular orbital bond order]{ref}.

= Chemical bond
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <chemical bond> associates <chemical atoms> or groups of <chemical atoms> into a sufficiently stable structure to treat it as a distinct entity. The concept covers connections within <molecules> and in extended solids; see the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/CT07009[IUPAC Gold Book, chemical bond]{ref}. It is useful because composition alone does not specify how the <chemical atoms> are connected, and those connections help determine a <chemical substance>'s properties and possible <chemical reactions>.

In a <covalent bond>, <electrons> are shared between <chemical atoms>. Two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> can form a <molecular hydrogen> <molecule>, with its <electrons> interacting with both <atomic nuclei>. In the usual description of this diatomic <molecule>, the <potential energy> as a function of nuclear separation has a minimum: moving the nuclei too close or too far raises the <energy>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.2 Covalent Bonding>.

Separating bonded <chemical atoms> requires <energy>; forming the corresponding <chemical bonds> releases <energy>. Whether an overall <chemical reaction> releases <energy> depends on both the <chemical bonds> broken and those formed. The lines in the <stoichiometry> diagram indicate connections schematically, rather than literal rods or a sequence of microscopic events.

= Electronegativity
{parent=Chemical bond}
{title2=$\chi$}
{wiki}

= Electronegative
{synonym}

<Electronegativity> describes a <chemical atom>'s tendency to attract <electron density> towards itself in a <chemical bond>. It helps answer a useful question: when two different <chemical atoms> share <electrons>, which end of the <chemical bond> tends to be richer in <electron density>? For <hydrogen chloride>, the answer is <chlorine>: the <covalent bond> is a <polar covalent bond>, conventionally written $\mathrm H^{\delta+}-\mathrm{Cl}^{\delta-}$. The symbols indicate <partial atomic charges>, not isolated <ions> carrying whole elementary <electric charges>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.2, “Pure vs. Polar Covalent Bonds”, HCl example]

<Electronegativity> is a comparison scheme, not the number of <electrons> on a <chemical atom>. A <partial atomic charge> depends on how the <electron density> is partitioned; <formal charge> instead follows an integer allocation in a <Lewis structure>. Neither is numerically equal to <electronegativity>. Nor is <electronegativity> identical to <electron affinity>, which concerns adding an <electron> to an isolated species. Different <electronegativity> scales use different measured inputs and conventions; compare numbers only after specifying the scale.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/electronegativity>{ref}

For a concrete comparison, the Royal Society of Chemistry lists <Pauling electronegativities> $\chi_{\rm P}(\mathrm H)=2.20$ and $\chi_{\rm P}(\mathrm{Cl})=3.16$. Their difference is $0.96$, so the <electronegativity> rule predicts <bond polarization> towards <chlorine> in <hydrogen chloride>. An isolated <molecular hydrogen> <molecule> has identical ends and zero <electronegativity> difference. These examples explain a direction of unequal sharing, not a quantitative <partial atomic charge>.<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Hydrogen — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Chlorine — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}

\Image[electronegativity-scales-hydrogen-chlorine.png]
{id=electronegativity-scales-hydrogen-chlorine}
{title=<Pauling electronegativity> and <Mulliken electronegativity> for <hydrogen> and <chlorine>: separate scales, separate units; the <Mulliken electronegativity> values are calculated from the stated ground-state inputs}

A larger <electronegativity> difference often suggests stronger <bond polarization>, but there is no universal numerical boundary where a <covalent bond> suddenly becomes <ionic bonding>. Tables of cutoffs are classification aids with exceptions. <Hydrogen fluoride>, for example, is described by a <polar covalent bond> even when its <electronegativity> difference exceeds a textbook's nominal ionic threshold. The actual <electron density>, physical setting, and <chemical substance>'s structure matter.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.2, “Electronegativity and Bond Type”, general-guide warning and HF exception]

= Pauling electronegativity
{c}
{parent=Electronegativity}
{title2=$\chi_{\rm P}$}

= Pauling scale
{c}
{synonym}

<Pauling electronegativity> is a relative, <dimensionless> scale motivated by <bond-dissociation energies>. The interesting observation is that a <chemical bond> between unlike <chemical atoms> can be stronger than a simple baseline inferred from the corresponding like-atom <chemical bonds>. An empirical excess <energy> can then be associated with an <electronegativity> difference. The 1932 paper <The Nature of the Chemical Bond. IV. The Energy of Single Bonds and the Relative Electronegativity of Atoms> built such a map; its original coordinates used <hydrogen> as zero, so those coordinates should not be mistaken for the modern tabulated <Pauling electronegativities>.<The Nature of the Chemical Bond. IV. The Energy of Single Bonds and the Relative Electronegativity of Atoms by Linus Pauling>[pp. 3576–3578, equation (1), Table III: H 0.00, Cl 0.94]

One arithmetic-baseline version of the <Pauling scale> relation is
$$
\Delta_D=D_{AB}-\frac{D_{AA}+D_{BB}}{2},\qquad
\left|\chi_{\rm P}(A)-\chi_{\rm P}(B)\right|\approx\sqrt{\frac{\Delta_D}{1\,\mathrm{eV}}}.
$$
Here $D_{AB}$, $D_{AA}$ and $D_{BB}$ are consistently defined <bond-dissociation energies> per <molecule>, and the <arithmetic mean> is the chosen baseline. With molar <energies> instead, write $\sqrt{\Delta_D/(96.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}})}$, approximately $0.102\sqrt{\Delta_D/(1\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}})}$. The denominator makes the result <dimensionless>; the factor $0.102$ is not a unit-independent magic constant. This is an empirical relation with a specified baseline, not a definition valid for every <chemical bond>.<Energy Electronegativity and Chemical Bonding by Stepan S. Batsanov>[§1 equation (1), coefficients for eV, kcal/mol and kJ/mol; §2.2, dependence on other bond properties]

For an explicitly synthetic example, suppose $D_{AA}=D_{BB}=300\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and $D_{AB}=396.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ with the same temperature and dissociation convention. Then $\Delta_D=96.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ and the model gives an <electronegativity> difference of $1$. Replacing $D_{AB}$ by $300\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ gives zero; a negative excess would make this real square-root model inapplicable, not produce a negative <electronegativity> difference. These invented inputs illustrate the formula and are not measurements of named <chemical substances>.

Only differences appear, so adding the same constant to every <Pauling electronegativity> leaves this relation unchanged. A reference value must fix the origin. The <bond-dissociation energies> alone also do not tell which endpoint attracts <electron density> more strongly, because interchanging $A$ and $B$ leaves the formula unchanged. Chemical comparisons fix that direction. Thus <Pauling electronegativity> is useful as a compact ordering, while the underlying measured <energies> and approximations remain essential.

= Bond-dissociation energy
{parent=Pauling electronegativity}
{wiki}
{title2=$D$}

A <bond-dissociation energy> specifies the <energy> needed for a stated dissociation into separated fragments. An <energy> from the lowest vibrational state, an <energy> from the bottom of a <potential energy> well, and a finite-temperature molar dissociation <enthalpy> require different conventions; do not silently mix them in an <electronegativity> formula.

= Mulliken electronegativity
{c}
{parent=Electronegativity}
{title2=$\chi_{\rm M}$}

= Mulliken scale
{c}
{synonym}

<Mulliken electronegativity> uses both the cost of removing an <electron> and the <energy> released by accepting an <electron>. For specified isolated-atom states, the common ground-state convention is
$$
\chi_{\rm M}=\frac{I+A_{\rm ea}}{2},\qquad
I=E(X^+)-E(X),\qquad A_{\rm ea}=E(X)-E(X^-).
$$
The free <electron>'s <energy> is the zero reference. Here $I$ is the <ionization energy> and $A_{\rm ea}$ is the <electron affinity>, positive for exothermic attachment. If attachment is instead tabulated as the signed <energy> change $\Delta E_{\rm attach}=E(X^-)-E(X)$, the same expression is $(I-\Delta E_{\rm attach})/2$. Stating the sign convention prevents adding the wrong quantities. The 1934 original treated suitable valence states, which are not automatically the ground states used in this example.<A New Electroaffinity Scale; Together with Data on Valence States and on Valence Ionization Potentials and Electron Affinities by Robert S. Mulliken>{ref}

<Mulliken electronegativity> has <energy> units before any additional rescaling. With $I$ and $A_{\rm ea}$ in <electronvolts> per <chemical atom>, $\chi_{\rm M}$ is in <electronvolts>; with both in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, it is a molar <energy>. For <hydrogen>, the RSC ground-state inputs are $I=1312.05$ and $A_{\rm ea}=72.769\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, giving $\chi_{\rm M}=692.4095\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\approx7.176\,\mathrm{eV}$ per <chemical atom>. For <chlorine>, $I=1251.186$ and $A_{\rm ea}=348.575\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ give $799.8805\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}\approx8.290\,\mathrm{eV}$. These are calculated ground-state <Mulliken electronegativities>, not the RSC's tabulated <Pauling electronegativities>.<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Hydrogen — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}<Periodic Table by Royal Society of Chemistry/Chlorine — Element information, properties and uses>{ref}

Both schemes place <chlorine> above <hydrogen> here, but their numerical differences cannot be subtracted from one another or equated. Dividing a molar <Mulliken electronegativity> by an <energy> conversion factor changes units; it does not transform it into a <Pauling electronegativity>. A further empirical mapping would require its own calibration and stated domain.

= Electron affinity
{parent=Mulliken electronegativity}
{wiki}
{title2=$A_{\rm ea}$}

<Electron affinity> specifies the <energy> associated with attaching an <electron> to an isolated species in stated conditions. This article uses positive <electron affinity> for <energy> released on attachment, as in the RSC atomic tables; a signed attachment <energy> uses the opposite convention.

= Electronvolt
{parent=Mulliken electronegativity}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{eV}$}

An <electronvolt> is $1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm J$. One <electronvolt> per particle corresponds to approximately $96.485\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.<Fundamental Physical Constants — Complete Listing by National Institute of Standards and Technology and others>{ref}

= Dipole evidence for bond polarization
{parent=Electronegativity}

A permanent <electric dipole moment> gives evidence of separated positive and negative <electric charge> in an overall neutral <molecule>. This is a more direct probe of <chemical polarity> than an <electronegativity> table. For <hydrogen chloride>, NIST lists about $1.109$ <debye> for the lowest vibrational state and $1.093$ <debye> for the equilibrium value. They describe different averaging conventions, not two contradictory measurements. The NIST WebBook traces the vibrational value $1.1085$ <debye> to molecular-beam electric-resonance measurements in 1970.<HCl (Hydrogen chloride): experimental data by National Institute of Standards and Technology>{ref}<Hydrogen chloride: Constants of diatomic molecules by Klaus P. Huber and others>{ref}

How does <molecular beam electric resonance> reveal an <electric dipole moment>? A source and apertures form a beam of <molecules> in <vacuum>. A first nonuniform <electric field>, A, selects <quantum states> through their different <Stark effects>. In a central region, C, electrodes supply a nearly uniform <electric field> and applied radiation drives transitions at selected <frequencies>. A second nonuniform <electric field>, B, analyzes the outgoing <quantum states>; a detector measures the rate of arriving <molecules>. In a transmission-loss arrangement, resonant radiation transfers some <molecules> to a <quantum state> rejected by B, so the measured arrival rate dips. The observable is a change in detected <molecules> as the radiation <frequency> is scanned, rather than light absorption itself. This general apparatus description is documented in a later experimental thesis; it is not a reconstruction of the exact 1970 HCl instrument.<High Resolution Molecular Beam Study of Alkali Cyanides by Johannes Jacobus van Vaals>[§2, printed pp. 11–14: fields A, C and B, detected flux, parallel plates, radiation and detector]

\Image[bond-dipole-beam-resonance.png]
{id=bond-dipole-beam-resonance}
{title=<Molecular beam electric resonance>: schematic state selection and a synthetic <Stark effect> signal from a <rigid rotor> model; neither apparatus dimensions nor signal curves are measured HCl data}

The inference comes from how transition <frequencies> change with the known <electric field>. The interaction $-\boldsymbol\mu\cdot\mathbf E$ couples the <electric dipole moment> to the <electric field>; fitting the <quantum mechanics> of the relevant rotational states extracts its magnitude. A free rotating <molecule> need not show a first-order shift proportional to $|\mathbf E|$: the weak-field <Stark effect> can be quadratic. The diagram uses a simplified <rigid rotor>, with $B$ an <energy> constant, to illustrate this behavior. It omits the fine details of actual spectra.

Useful controls include scans with the radiation off to measure the baseline arrival rate, repeated scans at several calibrated <electric fields>, and agreement between independently assigned transitions. These are methodological checks, not claims about undocumented steps of the 1970 experiment. Field calibration and state assignment limit the inferred <electric dipole moment>. The published 1970 abstract confirms analysis of HCl and DCl beam-resonance spectra and combination with <infrared spectroscopy> intensities to obtain a distance-dependent <electric dipole moment>; its full apparatus and calibration record were unavailable here.<Dipole Moment and Hyperfine Parameters of H35Cl and D35Cl by E. W. Kaiser>[pp. 1686–1703, authored abstract; full text unavailable]

Even an accurately measured <electric dipole moment> is not an absolute <partial atomic charge>. Replacing a <hydrogen chloride> <molecule> by two point <electric charges> $\pm q$ separated by <bond length> $r$ gives $|\mu|=|q|r$. Using $1.109$ <debye> and $r=1.27455\times10^{-10}\,\mathrm m$ gives $|q|\approx0.181$ times the elementary <electric charge>. This is an effective two-point model, combining a vibrationally averaged <electric dipole moment> with an equilibrium <bond length>, not a unique partition of the actual <electron density> or a measured “18.1 percent ionic bond”.<Hydrogen chloride: Constants of diatomic molecules by Klaus P. Huber and others>{ref}

For a <molecule> with several <chemical bonds>, <molecular geometry> can cancel opposing contributions to the total <electric dipole moment>. <Carbon dioxide> illustrates why an overall <nonpolar molecule> need not have unpolarized individual <chemical bonds>. An <electronegativity> comparison helps anticipate local <bond polarization>; the measured molecular <electric dipole moment> tests the entire <electric charge> distribution.

= Debye
{disambiguate=unit}
{parent=Dipole evidence for bond polarization}
{wiki=Debye}
{title2=$\mathrm D$}

= Debye
{synonym}

The <debye> is a unit of <electric dipole moment>: $1\,\mathrm D\approx3.33564\times10^{-30}\,\mathrm{C\,m}$.<Triatomic Spectral Database by National Institute of Standards and Technology/5. Special Units, Fundamental Constants, and Useful Conversion Factors>{ref}

= Molecular beam electric resonance
{parent=Dipole evidence for bond polarization}

= Rigid rotor
{parent=Molecular beam electric resonance}
{wiki}

A <rigid rotor> models rotational motion with fixed internal separations. Its use in the synthetic <Stark effect> figure omits vibrational motion and additional interactions in real <molecules>.

= Bond polarization
{parent=Electronegativity}

<Bond polarization> is unequal sharing of <electron density> across a <chemical bond>. An <electronegativity> difference is a heuristic for its direction and tendency, not a unique <partial atomic charge>.

= Polar covalent bond
{parent=Bond polarization}

A <polar covalent bond> shares <electrons> unequally, as in <hydrogen chloride>.

= Hydrogen chloride
{parent=Polar covalent bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{HCl}$}

= Hydrogen fluoride
{parent=Polar covalent bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{HF}$}

= Ionic bonding
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

<Ionic bonding> binds oppositely charged <ions> through attraction governed by <electrostatics>. In ordinary solid <sodium chloride>, each <cation> interacts with many <anions>, and also with other <cations>: attraction and repulsion together contribute to the binding of an extended <ionic crystal>. The <chemical formula> $\mathrm{NaCl}$ specifies equal numbers of the two kinds of <ions>; it does not divide the <solid> into isolated two-atom <molecules>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.1, “The Formation of Ionic Compounds”, Figure 7.3]

This distinction explains a striking combination of properties: ordinary dry <sodium chloride> conducts <electric current> poorly, while its molten form or a sufficiently concentrated <chemical solution> in <water> conducts well. The <ions> already exist in the <solid>; their ability to move through the sample changes. A <conductivity experiment on salt solutions> tests this transport picture, rather than simply asserting that charged constituents must make every material conductive.

\Image[ionic-crystal-pair-and-binding-energy.png]
{id=image-ionic-crystal-pair-and-binding-energy}
{title=An isolated <ion pair>, a plane of an extended <ionic crystal>, and a synthetic <potential energy> model with a stable spacing}
{description=Original schematic geometry and calculated model curves, not a measured <electron density>, <diffraction> pattern, or experimental <lattice energy>. Four nearest neighbours are visible in the plane of the <rock salt structure>; two more lie perpendicular to it. Disk sizes are arbitrary. The right-hand curves use the explicitly stipulated <dimensionless> model below.}

= Salt
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{wiki}

= Salt
{synonym}

= Ionic compound
{synonym}

A <salt> is an assembly of <cations> and <anions> with zero total <electric charge>. Its constituents can be single <chemical atoms> or groups joined internally by <covalent bonds>. Thus <ionic bonding> and <covalent bonds> can occur in the same <chemical substance>, at different structural levels.<Chemistry 2e>[§2.6, polyatomic ions and ionic compounds]

= Stoichiometry of ionic compounds
{parent=Salt (chemistry)}
{title2=$\sum_i\nu_i z_i=0$}

For a neutral <formula unit>, let $\nu_i$ count <ions> of type $i$ with <electric charge> $z_i e$, where $e$ is the <elementary charge>. The <stoichiometry> must satisfy
$$
\sum_i\nu_i z_i=0.
$$
For $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$, the smallest positive counts are $1:1$. For $\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$, they are $1:2$, giving <calcium chloride> $\mathrm{CaCl_2}$: $1(2)+2(-1)=0$. The formula counts <ions>, not the number of nearest neighbours. A <counterion> is the oppositely charged partner needed to balance a charged species; its identity can change <solubility> and other properties without changing the identity of that species.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.1, neutral ionic formulas and charge balance; §2.6, ionic compound examples]

= Calcium chloride
{parent=Stoichiometry of ionic compounds}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CaCl_2}$}

= Ionic crystal
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{wiki}

An <ionic crystal> repeats an arrangement of <ions> in three dimensions. A <unit cell> describes that repetition, whereas a <formula unit> describes composition. The two accounting devices answer different questions. <Coulomb's law> is not restricted to an arbitrarily drawn line connecting one <cation> to one <anion>: every <ion> contributes to the <electric field> experienced by the others.

= Rock salt structure
{parent=Ionic crystal}

The <rock salt structure> of ordinary <sodium chloride> has a <coordination number> of six for each kind of <ion>: six nearest neighbours of the opposite sign surround each <ion> along three perpendicular directions. Its conventional cubic <unit cell> contains four <formula units>; sharing corner, face, and edge sites between adjacent cells must be included in that count. Sixfold local coordination is compatible with $1:1$ overall <stoichiometry>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.1, Figure 7.3; §10.6, “The Structures of Ionic Crystals”, NaCl unit-cell accounting]

The checkerboard plane in the diagram is a slice through this three-dimensional arrangement. It leaves out the two nearest neighbours above and below the plane. Repeating a complete even-by-even patch gives equal numbers of signs; the displayed odd-by-odd crop has a boundary imbalance and is not a complete neutral <ionic crystal> or a <unit cell>. Other <salts> need not have the <rock salt structure> or the same <coordination number>.

= Coordination number
{parent=Ionic crystal}
{title2=$\mathrm{CN}$}

In this structural use, the <coordination number> counts nearest neighbouring constituents. State which neighbours are counted and which <ionic crystal> structure is being described; it is not an <ion>'s <electric charge> number.

= Ion pair
{parent=Ionic bonding}

An <ion pair> is an associated <cation> and <anion>. An isolated <ion pair> lacks the extended neighbourhood of an <ionic crystal>. In a <chemical solution>, <ion pairs> can coexist with more widely separated <ions>; their abundance depends on conditions. A neutral gas-phase <sodium chloride> <molecule> can be treated as a discrete entity, with its own <quantum mechanics> and dissociation products. That use of <molecule> does not describe the constituents of ordinary solid <sodium chloride>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§8–3, distinction between vaporized NaCl pairs and separated gaseous ions]<Conductivity of Solutions by Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations>{ref}

= Electron transfer and ionic character
{parent=Ionic bonding}

The bookkeeping steps $\mathrm{Na}\to\mathrm{Na}^++e^-$ and $\mathrm{Cl}+e^-\to\mathrm{Cl}^-$ conserve <electric charge> and describe the <electron> inventories of separated species. They do not establish a chronological mechanism in which a labeled <electron> jumps between two pre-existing <chemical atoms> and becomes a classical object owned exclusively by one of them. In a bound material, <quantum mechanics> determines the <electron density> and the total <energy>.

<Ionic bonding> and a <covalent bond> are useful limiting descriptions with a continuum between them. <Electronegativity> helps anticipate unequal sharing of <electron density>, but a numerical cutoff does not measure a universal fraction of <ionic bonding>. A <partial atomic charge> depends on how the <electron density> is partitioned; integer <formal charges> are a bookkeeping convention. Using $\pm e$ in a point-charge model therefore does not claim that every partition of the actual <electron density> assigns exactly those values.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§30–2, “Chemical bonds in crystals”: gradations between covalent and ionic bonding]<Chemistry 2e>[§7.2, “Electronegativity and Bond Type”: general guide and exceptions]

= Electrostatic model of ionic binding
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{title2=$U_{ij}=z_i z_j e^2/(4\pi\epsilon_0r_{ij})$}

For two point <electric charges> in <vacuum>, <Coulomb's law> gives the interaction <potential energy>
$$
U_{ij}(r_{ij})=\frac{z_i z_j e^2}{4\pi\epsilon_0 r_{ij}},
$$
with zero at infinite separation. Here $r_{ij}$ is the distance between them and $\epsilon_0$ is the <vacuum permittivity>. Opposite signs give negative <potential energy> and attraction; equal signs give positive <potential energy> and repulsion. For a finite collection of distinct point <electric charges>, sum over $i<j$ to count each pair once. A bulk <ionic crystal> requires the extended lattice sum, including both signs, rather than multiplying one attractive pair by the <coordination number>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§8–1, equations (8.1)–(8.3); §8–3, many-neighbour lattice sum]

At short distances, overlapping <electron density> makes the simple point-charge picture inadequate. <Quantum mechanics>, including the <Pauli exclusion principle>, matters to the steep repulsive contribution. Pure attraction proportional to $-1/r$ would collapse as $r\to0$; it cannot set an equilibrium spacing on its own. A useful illustrative model is
$$
U(r)=-\frac{A}{r}+\frac{B}{r^n},\qquad A>0,\quad B>0,\quad n>1.
$$
Here $r$ scales an assumed fixed arrangement, $A$ packages its attractive contribution, and $B/r^n$ represents short-range repulsion. The constants have units <energy> times length and <energy> times length to the power $n$, respectively. This is a model, not a fitted description of a named <salt>.<The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume II by Richard Feynman and others>[§8–3, close-distance repulsion and limits of a purely electrostatic lattice calculation]

Differentiating gives $r_0=(nB/A)^{1/(n-1)}$ and $U''(r_0)=(n-1)A/r_0^3>0$, so the stationary spacing is a stable minimum. For the diagram's stipulated $n=9$, put $x=r/r_0$ and $E_0=A(1-1/n)/r_0>0$. Then
$$
\frac{U(r)}{E_0}=\frac{-9/x+1/x^9}{8},\qquad U(r_0)=-E_0.
$$
The <dimensionless> curves show how competing interactions select a spacing. Their <energy> scale and exponent are synthetic; neither is a measured <lattice energy> or a <melting> prediction.

= Lattice energy
{parent=Ionic bonding}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta H_{\rm latt,diss}$}

A <lattice energy> needs a reaction, a sign convention, and a stated amount. Using the lattice-dissociation <enthalpy> convention for <sodium chloride>,
$$
\mathrm{NaCl}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Na}^+(g)+\mathrm{Cl}^-(g),\qquad
\Delta H_{\rm latt,diss}>0.
$$
The reverse lattice-formation <enthalpy> is negative with the same magnitude when initial and final states match. <Chemistry 2e> gives $769\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ for this separation, per <mole> of <sodium chloride> <formula units>. This is about $7.97$ <electronvolts> per <formula unit>, not per individual <ion>. It concerns separated gaseous <ions>, rather than neutral <chemical atoms>, molten <salt>, or hydrated <ions> in <water>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.5, “Ionic Bond Strength and Lattice Energy”, positive convention, reverse convention and NaCl value]

Also distinguish an <internal energy> change from an <enthalpy> change: $H=U+pV$. A static point-charge calculation omits motion and thermal contributions. Reversing a reaction reverses its <energy> change; replacing <internal energy> with <enthalpy> additionally changes the defined quantity. The <Born-Haber cycle> infers a lattice-formation <enthalpy> by combining other <enthalpies>, <ionization energies>, and <electron affinities>. These imagined stages are a thermodynamic accounting path, not a claim about the actual sequence of a <chemical reaction>.<Chemistry 2e>[§7.5, “The Born-Haber Cycle”, Figure 7.13 and Table 7.4]

= Born-Haber cycle
{c}
{parent=Lattice energy}

A <Born-Haber cycle> uses conservation of <energy> around a closed thermodynamic path to relate formation of an <ionic crystal> from the <chemical elements> to preparation of separated gaseous <ions> and their assembly. Match the <electron affinity> sign convention to the stated attachment step.

= Physical properties of salts
{parent=Ionic bonding}

Many <ionic crystals> have high <melting> <temperatures> compared with small molecular <solids>, because reorganizing their strongly interacting <ions> costs <energy>. But <melting> leaves a dense <liquid> with interactions; it does not perform the complete separation defining a <lattice energy>. At fixed pressure, the transition occurs when the <Gibbs free energies> of the <solid> and <liquid> match. The relevant <enthalpy> and <entropy> differences both matter, so <lattice energy> alone is not a universal ordering of <melting> <temperatures>. Strong <covalent bonds> in extended networks can also give high <melting> <temperatures>.<Chemistry 2e>[§10.5, “Ionic Solids” and “Covalent Network Solid”; §16.4, equations defining Gibbs free energy and its change]

= Solubility
{parent=Physical properties of salts}
{wiki}

<Solubility> specifies the amount that can remain dissolved at <chemical equilibrium> under stated conditions; it is different from how quickly a sample dissolves. Separating <ions> from an <ionic crystal> costs <energy>, but <solvation> establishes new interactions with the <solvent>. In <water>, <hydration of ions> couples charged <ions> to polar <water> <molecules>. The net <enthalpy> of dissolution can have either sign; <entropy> and <concentration> also enter the <Gibbs free energy> balance. Consequently, being a <salt> does not guarantee high <solubility> in <water> or any other <solvent>.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.1, “The Formation of Solutions”, Figure 11.4; §11.3, definition of solubility; §16.4, free energy]

Low <solubility> must also be distinguished from weak <electrolyte> behavior. A small amount of an <ionic compound> can dissolve essentially completely as <ions>, giving few charge carriers because little material dissolved. Calling that a <weak electrolyte> solely because its <chemical solution> conducts poorly would conflate two different questions.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.2, “Ionic Electrolytes”: sparingly soluble compounds can be strong electrolytes]

= Solvation
{parent=Solubility}
{wiki}

<Solvation> concerns interactions and reorganization when a species is surrounded by <solvent> constituents. In <water>, this is <hydration of ions>; it changes the environment of an <ion> without erasing its net <electric charge>.

= Hydration of ions
{parent=Solvation}

<Hydration of ions> is <solvation> by <water>. The polar <water> <molecules> orient preferentially around <cations> and <anions>, though the surrounding arrangement fluctuates rather than forming a permanently fixed cage.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.2, Figure 11.7 and “Ionic Electrolytes”]

= Conductivity experiment on salt solutions
{parent=Physical properties of salts}

How can a clear <chemical solution> reveal otherwise invisible mobile <ions>? The documented <Conductivity of Solutions by Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations> demonstration uses metal <electrodes> enclosed in tubing, an electrical supply, and a lamp in the circuit. The protocol specifies about $150\,\mathrm{mL}$ samples in labeled $250\,\mathrm{mL}$ beakers and a separate deionized-<water> rinse station. The recorded readout is lamp brightness after the <electrodes> enter each sample; the simplified variant tests <water> before and after adding <sodium chloride>. These are apparatus and procedure specifications, not new measurements reported here.<Conductivity of Solutions by Harvard Natural Sciences Lecture Demonstrations>{ref}

\Image[salt-solution-conductivity-controls.png]
{id=image-salt-solution-conductivity-controls}
{title=<Electrical conductivity> comparisons with a lamp tester, and the opposite motions of <cations> and <anions> in an <electric field>}
{description=Original circuit schematic and qualitative model outcomes, not photographs or recorded brightness data. A dark lamp means a signal below this tester's visible threshold, not identically zero <electrical conductivity>. Arrows show <drift velocities> during one half-cycle of an alternating supply; both signs contribute to the same <conventional current>. <Water> <molecules> and <hydration of ions> are omitted from the microscopic panel.}

The useful comparison is <water>, a <sucrose> <chemical solution>, and a <sodium chloride> <chemical solution> with the same probe geometry. A documented university account reports no visible light with <water>, a glowing lamp with dissolved <sodium chloride>, and a weak filament glow with its <sucrose> sample. The diagram summarizes the latter as little or no visible light; the precise detection threshold depends on the tester. <Sucrose> is a control for the idea that any dissolved material must conduct: its dispersed neutral <molecules> do not provide the abundant mobile <ions> supplied by the <salt>. This contrast is a documented teaching observation, not a numerical calibration of lamp brightness.<Chemistry 2e>[§11.1, sucrose dissolves as molecules; §11.2, Figure 11.6 and ionic versus nonionic charge carriers]<Conductivity of solutions by the migration of ions>{ref}

Rinsing the <electrodes> between samples controls carryover of dissolved <ions>, and the <water> blank checks the solvent background. Repeating the blank after the <salt> sample would test whether that rinse was effective. Additional methodological controls are fixed immersed area, electrode separation, supply setting, <temperature>, and comparison at stated <concentrations>. These additional controls are proposed checks, not undocumented claims about the source demonstration.

The inference is that mobile <electric charge> carriers complete the circuit. In the <chemical solution>, an <electric field> drives positive <ions> along the field and negative <ions> against it; multiplying each <drift velocity> by its <electric charge> makes both contributions to <conventional current> point the same way. With an alternating supply the direction reverses each half-cycle. The external wires instead carry <electric current> through <electrons>. The lamp alone identifies neither the species nor an exact <electrical conductivity>.

Important limits remain. <Water> has a small intrinsic population of <hydronium> and <hydroxide>, so a dark lamp does not prove absence of <ions>. Changes in probe geometry affect measured <electrical conductance>; <electrolysis>, electrode interfaces, contamination, and heating can also affect a real circuit. The comparison supports a transport model but does not independently measure a <coordination number>, a partitioned <partial atomic charge>, or a <lattice energy>.

Nor does poor conduction by ordinary dry <sodium chloride> establish that all <ionic crystals> are electrical insulators. <Crystallographic defects> can allow transport, and <solid electrolytes> can have substantial ionic <electrical conductivity> without <melting>. The authored abstract of <A lithium superionic conductor by Noriaki Kamaya and others> reports room-<temperature> ionic conduction in a <solid electrolyte>; the full experimental article was not accessible here.<A lithium superionic conductor by Noriaki Kamaya and others>{ref}

= Crystallographic defect
{parent=Physical properties of salts}
{wiki}

A <crystallographic defect> is a departure from ideal repeated structure. In an <ionic crystal>, a missing <ion> can provide an available site for another <ion> to move into; movement still requires overcoming an <activation energy>. This is why an ideal immobile-site model is a limit rather than a universal law for <solid> <electrical conductivity>.<Chemistry 2e>[§10.5, “Crystal Defects”: vacancies and other imperfections]

= Solid electrolyte
{parent=Physical properties of salts}

A <solid electrolyte> carries <electric current> through mobile <ions> while remaining a <solid>. The distinction between ionic and electronic <electrical conductivity> matters: different charge carriers serve different roles in <electrochemistry>.

= Bond length
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$r$}

A <bond length> describes the separation of the <atomic nuclei> of two bonded <chemical atoms>. Distinguish an equilibrium geometry from an instantaneous or experimentally averaged separation. Methods of determining <molecular geometry> can use different averaging conventions, so reported <bond lengths> need their measurement context. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/BT07003[IUPAC Gold Book: bond length]{ref}. Changing a <bond length> is one possible <reaction coordinate>.

= Partial atomic charge
{parent=Chemical bond}

A partial atomic charge assigns an <electric charge> to a <chemical atom> by partitioning the actual <electron density>. The result depends on the partitioning convention and need not be an integer. It differs from <formal charge>, which divides bonding <electrons> equally in a specified <Lewis structure>.

= Electron pair
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

An <electron pair> consists of two <electrons> with opposite <spins> sharing the same spatial part of their <quantum state>. The <Lewis structure> distinction between a <bond pair> and a <lone pair> records whether that pair is assigned to a connection between <chemical atoms> or to one <chemical atom>.

= Hydrogen bond
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}
{title2=$X-\mathrm H\cdots Y$}

A <hydrogen bond> is an attractive association involving a <hydrogen> attached to another <chemical atom>, and an accepting atom or group. In the IUPAC 2011 definition, the atom $X$ in $X-\mathrm H$ is more <electronegative> than <hydrogen>, and evidence of bond formation is required. The familiar strong examples use O–H or N–H groups, but restricting the concept to only nitrogen, oxygen and fluorine misses weaker cases. A short distance alone is not the entire definition.https://publications.iupac.org/pac/pdf/2011/pdf/8308x1637.pdf[Arunan et al., Definition of the hydrogen bond, IUPAC Recommendations 2011]{ref}

\Image[water-hydrogen-bond-roles.png]
{title=Two water molecules play different hydrogen-bond roles while retaining their own covalent O–H bonds}

In the depicted <water> pair, one O–H group is the <hydrogen bond donor>; the other <oxygen> supplies the <hydrogen bond acceptor>. The solid O–H line denotes a <covalent bond> within a <water> <molecule>; the dotted H···O line denotes the <hydrogen bond> between them. One <water> <molecule> can play both roles in a larger arrangement because it has O–H groups and <lone pairs>.

This makes <hydrogen bonds> useful for understanding organization beyond a single <molecular formula>. They connect <water> <molecules> and contribute to <protein> folding and recognition between <molecules>. The same types of <chemical atoms> can support different associations depending on which donor and acceptor sites are exposed and how they are oriented. Alberts and colleagues discuss these noncovalent interactions in https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Molecular Biology of the Cell, the chemical components of a cell]{ref}.

<Hydrogen bonds> are often directional, with an approximately straight donor–H–acceptor arrangement favourable in familiar cases; strength and preferred geometry depend on the system. The IUPAC definition uses converging experimental or theoretical evidence rather than one universal distance threshold. The author-hosted https://ipc.iisc.ac.in/~ea/iupac/iupac/IUPAC_finalreport_Nov_2009TG.pdf[2009 task-group draft, §§3.1–3.3]{ref} explains the definition and typical criteria leading to the recommendation.

= Hydrogen bond donor
{parent=Hydrogen bond}
{title2=$X-\mathrm H$}

A <hydrogen bond donor> is the $X-\mathrm H$ group supplying the attached <hydrogen> in a <hydrogen bond> $X-\mathrm H\cdots Y$. The donor atom $X$, or the whole <molecule> containing it, may also be called the donor. The terminology does not imply transfer of a free <proton>: in the ordinary <water> pair, the O–H <covalent bond> remains. Familiar donors include O–H groups in <water> and N–H groups in <ammonia>. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Andrew Glasfeld, Intermolecular Attraction, pp. 12–13: donor and acceptor roles]{ref}.

\Image[hydrogen-bond-donor-acceptor-site-map.png]
{title=<Hydrogen bond donor> and <hydrogen bond acceptor> roles depend on attached <hydrogen>, <protonation>, and <chemical resonance>}
{description=Toy site map for ordinary O–H and N–H associations, not measured geometry or an exhaustive list of <hydrogen bonds>. $R$ and $R'$ denote groups attached through <carbon>. Coloured labels identify possible sites, rather than count the number of simultaneous contacts.}

The attached <hydrogen> is essential to this role. At the <nitrogen> site, an unprotonated <amine> $R_3\mathrm N$ can be a <hydrogen bond acceptor>, but has no N–H <hydrogen bond donor>. <Protonation> gives $R_3\mathrm{NH}^+$: the new N–H group can donate, while that <nitrogen> no longer has the accepting <lone pair>. This is a change of chemical form, not a permanent label attached to a <chemical element>. Here $R$ denotes a group attached through <carbon>; the statement concerns the <nitrogen> site and does not rule out weaker C–H associations elsewhere. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-3-basicity-of-amines[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 24.3: amine protonation]{ref} and https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: protonated amines as donors]{ref}.

An <amide> with an N–H <covalent bond> can donate even though its <nitrogen> is ordinarily not an accepting site. For example, in $R-\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)-\mathrm{NH}-R'$, N–H is a <hydrogen bond donor> and the <carbonyl group>'s <oxygen> is a <hydrogen bond acceptor>. Replacing the remaining N–H by another group attached through <carbon> removes this N–H donor without removing the accepting <oxygen>. The analogous division of roles in a <peptide bond> helps explain <protein> structure. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: amide roles and biological interactions]{ref}. The useful question is therefore which site supplies the <hydrogen>, rather than whether the whole substance is simply “hydrogen bonding”.

= Hydrogen bond acceptor
{parent=Hydrogen bond}

A <hydrogen bond acceptor> supplies the electron-rich atom or group $Y$ interacting with the attached <hydrogen> of a <hydrogen bond donor> in $X-\mathrm H\cdots Y$. An <oxygen> <lone pair> in <water> is the familiar example. Acceptance describes the association, not necessarily acquisition of a <proton>. A single <molecule>, such as <water> or <ammonia>, can offer both <hydrogen bond donor> and <hydrogen bond acceptor> sites. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: water and nitrogen-containing accepting sites]{ref}.

A <lone pair> drawn in a <Lewis structure> is a useful clue, not a universal test. At an ordinary neutral <amide> <nitrogen>, the nominal <lone pair> participates in <chemical resonance> with the <carbonyl group>, so this <nitrogen> is ordinarily excluded from lists of <hydrogen bond acceptors>; the <carbonyl group>'s <oxygen> remains an accepting site. <Protonation> changes the test again: $\mathrm{NH}_4^+$ has four N–H <covalent bonds> and no <nitrogen> <lone pair>, whereas <ammonia> has three and one. These distinctions follow the actual chemical form, and accepting ability also depends on the partner and whether it can approach the site. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-3-basicity-of-amines[McMurry: amide resonance and protonation]{ref} and https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld: limitations of nitrogen accepting sites]{ref}.

Conversely, an accepting group need not supply an atom-centred <lone pair>. The following <phenol>–<benzene> experiment probes an O–H donor interacting with the ring's distributed $\pi$ <electron density>.

\Image[hydrogen-bond-phenol-infrared-controls.png]
{title=<Infrared spectroscopy> distinguishes free and associated <phenol>: schematic experimental logic, no plotted or simulated spectra}
{description=Original toy layout of the comparisons reported by Saggu, Levinson and Boxer (2011). The connector identifies an O–H donor and a ring accepting region; it does not specify a measured <bond length> or the calculated contact geometry.}

Saggu, Levinson and Boxer (2011) used <infrared spectroscopy> to compare $200\,\mathrm{mmol\,L^{-1}}$ <phenol> in different <solvents>. The O–H stretching peak was at $3611\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in $\mathrm{CCl}_4$ and $3555.9\,\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ in <benzene>. To distinguish association from a general <solvent> effect, they diluted the ring-containing <chemical solutions> with $\mathrm{CCl}_4$: two peaks appeared, with the free peak nearly fixed while the associated peak depended on the partner. Replacing the O–H <hydrogen> with <deuterium> reproduced the ordering of shifts for different partners. Thus the perturbed O–H group identifies the <hydrogen bond donor>; the ring supplies the <hydrogen bond acceptor>. A peak shift alone does not specify a unique <molecular geometry>. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3202663/[Saggu, Levinson and Boxer, Direct measurements of electric fields in weak OH···π hydrogen bonds, JACS 133 (2011), Figure 1, Table 1 and dilution controls]{ref}

The roles make recognition between <molecules> intelligible: exposed <hydrogen bond donors> need compatible <hydrogen bond acceptors> in reachable positions. A count of sites is a starting description, not a prediction of binding strength. In <water>, forming a contact between two partners can also replace their existing <hydrogen bonds> to <water>; the <Gibbs free energy> of association concerns the whole change. See https://people.reed.edu/~glasfeld/Chem391/notes/ChIMF_2018.pdf[Glasfeld, pp. 15–16: competition with solvent interactions]{ref}.

= Carbonyl group
{parent=Chemical bond}
{title2=$\mathrm{C=O}$}

A <carbonyl group> contains a <carbon>–<oxygen> double <covalent bond>. It occurs in <aldehydes> and <amides>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/12-7-interpreting-infrared-spectra[McMurry: carbonyl-containing groups]{ref}

= Covalent bond
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

A <covalent bond> joins <chemical atoms> through shared <electrons>. In the familiar two-centre description, one <bond pair> contributes to a connection between two <atomic nuclei>. Sharing means that the <electrons> belong to the combined arrangement and interact with both <atomic nuclei>; it does not mean that identifiable dots shuttle along drawn paths. The https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01384[IUPAC Gold Book, covalent bond]{ref} emphasizes the concentration of <electrons> between <atomic nuclei> and the resulting attraction and characteristic separation. The pair picture is especially useful for simple <molecules>, but is not a universal description of every <covalent bond>.

A <Lewis structure> turns this picture into practical bookkeeping. Count the <valence electrons>, indicate which <chemical atoms> are connected, and allocate two <electrons> to each line. A <bond pair> is shared; a <lone pair> is assigned to one <chemical atom> without connecting it to another. A double line represents two <bond pairs>. Dots and lines record allocations, rather than positions, trajectories, or a measured three-dimensional arrangement. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, Lewis symbols and structures]{ref}

For <molecular hydrogen>, $\mathrm H_2$, each neutral <hydrogen> <chemical atom> supplies one <valence electron>. Thus $1+1=2$ <valence electrons> make one <bond pair>, represented by $\mathrm{H-H}$, with no <lone pairs>. Each <hydrogen> counts the same shared pair when checking its two-electron arrangement. The <molecule> still contains two <electrons>, not four: counting a pair around both endpoints does not create extra <electrons>.

For <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, neutral <oxygen> supplies six <valence electrons> and the two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> supply one each: $6+1+1=8$. Two O–H connections consume four <electrons> as two <bond pairs>. The remaining $8-4=4$ <electrons> form two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>. Around <oxygen>, the two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs> count as eight <electrons>; each <hydrogen> counts two. The whole <water> <molecule> has ten <electrons>, because two additional <oxygen> <electrons> are omitted from this <valence electron> drawing.

For <ammonia>, $\mathrm{NH}_3$, the total is $5+3\times1=8$ <valence electrons>: five from <nitrogen> and three from <hydrogen>. Three N–H connections use six <electrons> as three <bond pairs>, leaving two <electrons> as one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>. Thus <water> and <ammonia> have the same total of eight <valence electrons> but different allocations: two plus two pairs versus three plus one. This small calculation already distinguishes their connectivity without a detailed <quantum mechanics> calculation.

\Image[lewis-electron-pair-counts.png]
{title=<Lewis structures> count <bond pairs> and <lone pairs> in <molecular hydrogen>, <water>, <ammonia>, and <molecular oxygen>; schematic layouts do not specify molecular shapes}

For <molecular oxygen>, $\mathrm O_2$, $6+6=12$ <valence electrons> give the conventional $\mathrm{O=O}$ <Lewis structure>. Its two <bond pairs> use four <electrons>, leaving eight as four <lone pairs>, two on each <oxygen>. Each <oxygen> consequently counts eight <electrons> locally. However, actual <molecular oxygen> has two unpaired <electrons> and exhibits <paramagnetism>: it is attracted into a stronger <magnetic field>. The all-paired <Lewis structure> cannot capture this observation. <Molecular orbital theory> describes the unpaired <electrons>; the successful counting diagram alone is insufficient. https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/8-4-molecular-orbital-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, molecular orbital theory]{ref}

Why does sharing produce a stable separation? For <molecular hydrogen>, the electronic arrangement responds as the <atomic nuclei> approach. Their interactions can lower the <energy>, but pushing them too close raises it. A curve of <potential energy> against separation therefore has a <local minimum>, rather than falling forever. Attraction and repulsion balance there. This is a statement about the combined system, not a claim that stationary <electrons> sit between otherwise motionless <atomic nuclei>. <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.2 Covalent Bonding>{ref}

The following <Morse potential> makes those features calculable. Set $x=r/r_e$, where $r$ is separation and $r_e$ its equilibrium value, and let $D$ be the well depth. With the self-chosen parameter $a=2$, define the <dimensionless quantities>
$$
u(x)=\frac{V(r)}{D}=\left(1-e^{-2(x-1)}\right)^2-1,
\qquad f(x)=\frac{F(r)r_e}{D}=-\frac{du}{dx}=4\left(e^{-4(x-1)}-e^{-2(x-1)}\right).
$$
Here $F=-dV/dr$ is the net separating <force>, obtained from the <derivative> of the <potential energy>. Positive $f$ pushes the <chemical atoms> apart; negative $f$ draws them together. These are illustrative curves with $r_e=D=1$ in chosen units, not measured <molecular hydrogen> data or fitted parameters.

\Image[covalent-bond-energy-model.png]
{title=Illustrative <dimensionless> <Morse potential> and its <force>: a stable <local minimum>, short-distance repulsion, and separation toward zero <potential energy>}

At $x=1$, $u=-1$ and $f=0$. The <second derivative> is $u''(1)=8>0$, confirming a <local minimum>. At $x=0.5$, $u=(1-e)^2-1\approx1.9525$; at $x=1.5$, $u=(1-e^{-1})^2-1\approx-0.6004$. As $x$ increases without bound, $u$ approaches zero from below. Thus moving from the well bottom to separated <chemical atoms> requires an increase of $D$ in this model's <potential energy>. An actual <quantum mechanics> dissociation threshold also depends on the initial motion of the <atomic nuclei>, so the well depth alone is not every measured dissociation <energy>.

The <force> panel separates $f$ into a positive term $4e^{-4(x-1)}$ and a negative term $-4e^{-2(x-1)}$. At $x=1$ they are $+4$ and $-4$, cancelling exactly; nearer separations favour the positive term. These are algebraic components of the chosen <Morse potential>, not separately measured microscopic <forces>. Their usefulness is to show how a balance arises from the slope of one <energy> curve.

The beauty of the pair picture is its economy: a few marks summarize connectivity and constrain what a proposed <molecule> can contain. Its historical anchor is Gilbert N. Lewis's 1916 paper https://doi.org/10.1021/ja02261a002[The Atom and the Molecule, Journal of the American Chemical Society 38, 762–785]{ref}, also available as an https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[archived original paper]{ref}. <Lewis structures> remain useful because they answer counting questions quickly; <quantum mechanics> supplies the richer account when <energy>, shape, or unpaired <electrons> matter.

= Phosphodiester bond
{parent=Covalent bond}
{wiki}

A <phosphodiester bond> connects two groups through a <phosphate> diester. In a <nucleic acid> backbone, the bridging <phosphate> is connected through <oxygen> to the $3'$ <carbon> position of one <sugar> and the $5'$ <carbon> position of another. These <covalent bonds> connect successive <nucleotides> along a strand; they are distinct from the <hydrogen bonds> between paired <nucleobases>.

= Disulfide bond
{parent=Covalent bond}
{title2=$\mathrm{S-S}$}

A <disulfide bond> is the single <covalent bond> between the two adjacent <sulfur> <chemical atoms> in a <disulfide>, conventionally written $\mathrm{R-S-S-R'}$. Here $R$ and $R'$ stand for the attached organic groups; they may belong to the same <molecule> or to initially separate <molecules>. A <disulfide bond> is a chemical bridge, rather than a <hydrogen bond> or a mere contact between nearby groups. Two <thiols>, $\mathrm{R-SH}$ and $\mathrm{R'-SH}$, can supply its two <sulfur> endpoints. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/18-7-thiols-and-sulfides[John McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 18.7: thiol oxidation and disulfides]{ref}

The <stoichiometry> of forming a <disulfide bond> is especially revealing. Written as an <oxidation> <half-reaction>, it is
$$
\mathrm{R-SH+R'-SH\longrightarrow R-S-S-R'+2H^++2e^-}.
$$
The reverse <reduction> <half-reaction> consumes two <electrons> and two <hydrogen ions> to restore two <thiols>. Both sides contain two <sulfur> <chemical atoms>; the two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> have moved into the two <hydrogen ions>, and the net <electric charge> remains zero because $+2-2=0$. Thus one <disulfide bond> corresponds to two <thiol> groups and two transferred <electrons>. These <half-reactions> describe <redox> bookkeeping, not a complete standalone <chemical reaction>: an accompanying <reactant> must accept or supply the <electrons>, and <pH> affects the actual <protonation> of the groups. <Mercaptoethanol> can promote <reduction> through exchange of <disulfide bonds>, but adequate excess and appropriate conditions are needed; adding it does not by itself prove complete <reduction>. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3766385/[Jakob R. Winther and Colin Thorpe, Quantification of Thiols and Disulfides (2014), section 3: reducing reagents and completeness]{ref}

In a <protein disulfide bond>, the endpoints are normally the <amino acid side chains> of two <cysteine> <amino acid residues>. An intrachain <protein disulfide bond> closes an intramolecular bridge within one <polypeptide chain>; an interchain <protein disulfide bond> connects different <polypeptide chains>, and can arise by intermolecular crosslinking of initially separate <molecules>. Forming or reducing these bridges changes <covalent bond> connectivity and can change <protein structure>, while preserving the order of <amino acid residues> and the <peptide bonds> in the <protein backbone>. A complete chemical account of <protein primary structure> includes crosslinks as well as sequence, so preservation of sequence does not imply preservation of every <covalent bond>. Mature <insulin> has two interchain <protein disulfide bonds> and one intrachain <protein disulfide bond>. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14744022/[Seung-Gu Chang, Ki-Doo Choi, Seung-Hwan Jang and Hang-Cheol Shin, Role of disulfide bonds in the structure and activity of human insulin (2003)]{ref}

\Image[disulfide-bonds-redox-and-bridges.png]
{title=<Disulfide bond> <redox> bookkeeping and schematic intrachain and interchain <protein disulfide bonds>}
{description=Original connectivity cartoons, not measured <protein structures>. Blue and green traces preserve their numbered <amino acid residues>; amber S-S links connect <amino acid side chains> without replacing a <peptide bond>. The cartoons are invented examples, not <insulin> coordinates.}

A bridge is useful because it constrains which parts of a <polypeptide chain> can move apart without breaking a <covalent bond>. Its placement therefore matters as well as its presence. The <ribonuclease A refolding experiment> provides the existing experimental example: breaking and reforming four <protein disulfide bonds> can accompany loss and recovery of function. Counting four bridges alone cannot tell whether their endpoints have the native pairing. This is an attractive connection between simple chemical counting and the richer problem of <protein folding>.

How can an experiment reveal an initially hidden <disulfide bond>? <Ellman's reagent>, also called <DTNB>, measures free <thiols> by producing a yellow absorbing species. Its reactive <disulfide bond> undergoes exchange with the <conjugate base> $\mathrm{R-S^-}$ of a <thiol>, producing a mixed <disulfide> attached to $R$ and one released 2-nitro-5-thiobenzoate species, abbreviated TNB. At the assay <pH>, the strongly colored form is the dianion $\mathrm{TNB^{2-}}$: one negative charge is on <sulfur> and the other is on the <carboxylic acid>-derived $\mathrm{COO^-}$ group. With excess <DTNB> and complete reaction, one released TNB reports one reacted <thiol>. George L. Ellman's 1959 paper introduced the tissue assay; it was subsequently reexamined quantitatively. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13650640/[George L. Ellman, Tissue sulfhydryl groups (1959)]{ref} https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0011216_Ellmans_Reag_UG.pdf[Pierce Biotechnology, Ellman's Reagent instructions, introduction and reaction diagram]{ref}

The modern apparatus can be simple: tubes, pipettes, a <buffer solution>, <cysteine> standards, <DTNB>, and an instrument that measures <absorbance> through a cuvette at <wavelength> $412\,\mathrm{nm}$. The Pierce protocol uses $50\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ of a $4\,\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}$ <DTNB> stock, $2.50\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <buffer solution>, and $250\,\mathrm{\mu L}$ of sample. Its <buffer solution> is $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ sodium <phosphate> at <pH> 8.0 with a metal-binding additive. After 15 minutes at room <temperature>, measure <absorbance> against a <buffer solution> plus <DTNB> blank; run known <cysteine> <concentrations> in parallel. The observable is increased yellow color and increased blank-corrected <absorbance> as more accessible <thiols> react, rather than direct imaging of <disulfide bonds>. These are published protocol conditions, not measurements performed for this article. https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0011216_Ellmans_Reag_UG.pdf[Pierce Biotechnology, Ellman's Reagent instructions, page 2: standard and cuvette procedures]{ref}

For a before/after test, split a purified <protein> sample into matched aliquots. Measure accessible free <thiols> in one aliquot; subject the other to complete <reduction> of its <disulfide bonds>, remove the reducing <reagent>, then measure the resulting <thiols> with fresh <DTNB>. Process the unreduced aliquot through a matching cleanup. Normalize to recovered <protein> and known <dilution>, since cleanup can change both. To estimate all reducible <protein disulfide bonds>, expose buried groups using matching <protein denaturation> conditions in both aliquots and calibrate in that same <chemical solution>; a native-accessibility measurement answers a different question. A larger corrected reading after <reduction> supports conversion of previously unreactive groups into detectable <thiols>. Attribution to <disulfide bonds> requires excluding other reducible <sulfur> modifications. This is a proposed comparison based on the detection principles, not a reconstruction of Ellman's original tissue measurements. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3766385/[Winther and Thorpe (2014), sections 1.2 and 4.1: indirect disulfide detection and assay limitations]{ref}

\Image[disulfide-bonds-ellman-workflow.png]
{title=Matched <Ellman's reagent> assay workflow and an explicitly hypothetical <disulfide bond> count}
{description=Original apparatus and workflow schematic. The displayed <absorbances> are calculated illustrations for two free <thiols> and two <disulfide bonds> per model <protein> at equal final <protein> <concentration>; they are not measured data or a calibration curve. Reducing <reagent> removal and matched accessibility are prerequisites for interpreting the difference.}

Under the <Beer-Lambert law>, the blank-corrected <absorbance> is $A=\varepsilon lc$, where $l$ is the cuvette path length, $c$ is the released TNB <concentration>, and $\varepsilon$ is its molar absorption coefficient. Riddles, Blakeley and Zerner measured $\varepsilon_{412}=14150\,\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,cm^{-1}}$ in dilute aqueous salt <chemical solutions>, confirming the value independently using <cysteine>. It must not be applied indiscriminately to other <solvents> or <protein denaturation> mixtures. https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/0003269779907929[Peter W. Riddles, Robert L. Blakeley and Burt Zerner, Ellman's reagent: a reexamination (1979)]{ref} For aliquots compared on the same final volume and <protein> <concentration> basis, the ideal estimate is
$$
c_{\mathrm{S-S}}=\frac{c_{\mathrm{SH,after}}-c_{\mathrm{SH,before}}}{2}
=\frac{A_{\mathrm{after}}-A_{\mathrm{before}}}{2\varepsilon l},
\qquad
\bar n_{\mathrm{S-S}}=\frac{c_{\mathrm{S-S}}}{c_{\mathrm{protein}}}.
$$
The factor of two follows from the <reduction> <stoichiometry>; $\bar n_{\mathrm{S-S}}$ is the number of <disulfide bonds> per <protein> <molecule>, or an average for a mixed population. If the aliquots have different <dilutions> or recoveries, convert each reading to a common sample basis before subtracting.

As a hypothetical calculation, choose $c_{\mathrm{protein}}=10\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ in each final cuvette, two free <thiols> and two <disulfide bonds> per <protein> <molecule>, and $l=1\,\mathrm{cm}$. Before <reduction>, $c_{\mathrm{SH}}=20\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ gives $A=0.283$; complete <reduction> adds four <thiols> per <protein> <molecule>, so $c_{\mathrm{SH}}=60\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ gives $A=0.849$. The difference $0.566$ gives $c_{\mathrm{S-S}}=20\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $\bar n_{\mathrm{S-S}}=2$. The published tube volumes imply a <dilution> factor $2.80/0.250=11.2$; reversing that <dilution> multiplies both <thiol> and <protein> <concentrations> by 11.2 and leaves the bridge count per <protein> <molecule> unchanged.

Controls determine whether that arithmetic has chemical meaning. Use fresh <cysteine> standards and a processed blank without <protein> to detect color generated by residual reducing <reagent>. Even a reducing <reagent> containing no <thiols> can interfere: Han and Han showed that tris(2-carboxyethyl)phosphine, abbreviated TCEP, reduces <DTNB> to two TNB equivalents per TCEP. https://doi.org/10.1006/abio.1994.1290[J. C. Han and G. Y. Han, A Procedure for Quantitative Determination of Tris(2-Carboxyethyl)phosphine (1994)]{ref} In the hypothetical model, $5\,\mathrm{\mu mol\,L^{-1}}$ residual TCEP in only the reduced cuvette would add $A=0.1415$ and falsely add $0.5$ bridge per <protein> <molecule>. A stable endpoint and agreement after a further <reduction> treatment help check completion. A sample without <DTNB> checks its own color; cloudy samples can also attenuate light without producing TNB. Match <pH>, <temperature>, accessibility and calibration <chemical solution>, and minimize delay that permits reoxidation. If two already-free but buried <thiols> become accessible only in the reduced aliquot, they mimic one new <disulfide bond> per <protein> <molecule>. Finally, bulk <absorbance> supplies a count under these assumptions: it does not locate the <cysteine> endpoints, distinguish intrachain from interchain bridges, or establish the native pairing of a <protein disulfide bond>.

= Lewis structure
{parent=Covalent bond}
{c}
{wiki}

= Lewis formula
{c}
{synonym}

= Lewis dot structure
{c}
{synonym}

Before calculating electronic <energy>, ask a simpler question: where can the available <valence electrons> go? A <Lewis structure> makes that budget visible, and <nitrate> shows why a correct budget can still require several <resonance structures>.

A <Lewis structure> records which <chemical atoms> are connected in a <molecule> or <ion> and how its <valence electrons> are allocated. One line represents one <bond pair>, two lines two <bond pairs>, and three lines three <bond pairs>; two dots beside a <chemical atom> represent one <lone pair>. The marks are a counting language, rather than measured <electron> positions. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/L03513[IUPAC Gold Book, Lewis formula]{ref}.

For <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, the budget is $6+2\times1=8$ <valence electrons>. Two O–H lines use four <electrons>; the other four form two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>. For <ammonia>, $\mathrm{NH}_3$, $5+3\times1=8$ <valence electrons> give three N–H lines and one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>. Both <molecules> contain four <electron pairs>, but their allocations differ. Each shared <bond pair> is counted once in the total budget, even though both endpoints count it when checking the <octet rule>.

\Image[lewis-water-ammonia.png]
{title=Eight <valence electrons> in each <molecule>: <water> has two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs>; <ammonia> has three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair>}
{description=Each blue line represents two <electrons>; each orange dot represents one <electron>. The <oxygen> and <nitrogen> each count eight <electrons> locally, while each <hydrogen> counts two. These are schematic <Lewis structures>, not predictions of molecular shape or <electron> trajectories.}

A practical construction method is:
* Add the neutral <chemical atoms>' <valence electron> counts. Add one <electron> for each unit of negative <electric charge>, or subtract one for each unit of positive <electric charge>.
* Choose candidate connections and put one <bond pair> on each. <Hydrogen> normally has one connection in these simple <Lewis structures>.
* Place remaining <electrons> as <lone pairs>, checking the <octet rule>. If an endpoint has eight <electrons> but the central <chemical atom> lacks them, try converting an endpoint <lone pair> into an additional <bond pair>, keeping the total budget fixed.
* Compute every <formal charge> and check its sum against the net <electric charge>. Compare reasonable candidates, including <chemical resonance> when several allocations share the same connections.

For <carbon dioxide>, $4+2\times6=16$ <valence electrons>. Starting with two single C–O connections leaves twelve <electrons>, enough for three <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>, but only four <electrons> around <carbon>. Converting one <lone pair> from each <oxygen> into a second <bond pair> gives $\mathrm{O=C=O}$. Eight <electrons> are in the four <bond pairs>, and eight are in four <lone pairs>, two on each <oxygen>. <Carbon> has no <lone pair>; all three <chemical atoms> satisfy the <octet rule>, and all three <formal charges> vanish.

For the <nitrate ion>, $\mathrm{NO}_3^-$, the <valence electron> budget is
$$
5+3\times6+1=24.
$$
<Nitrogen> contributes five, the three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> contribute eighteen, and the negative <electric charge> adds one <electron>. Start with <nitrogen> connected to each <oxygen> by one <bond pair>: those three lines use six <electrons>. Put the remaining eighteen <electrons> into three <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>. Each <oxygen> now satisfies the <octet rule>, but <nitrogen> counts only six <electrons>. Reassign one <oxygen> <lone pair> as a second <bond pair> on its N–O connection. This changes the allocation without adding an <electron>; it applies the construction procedure in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.3: drawing Lewis structures]{ref}.

The resulting <nitrate> <Lewis structure> has one N=O connection and two N–O connections. Its four <bond pairs> use eight <electrons>, and its eight <lone pairs> use sixteen: $8+16=24$. The doubly connected <oxygen> has two <lone pairs>; each singly connected <oxygen> has three; <nitrogen> has none. All four <chemical atoms> satisfy the <octet rule>. Any of the three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> can supply the additional <bond pair>, giving the three equivalent <resonance structures> below.

\Image[nitrate-resonance-contributors.png]
{title=Three equivalent <nitrate> <resonance structures>, each with 24 <valence electrons>}
{description=The same labelled <chemical atoms> occupy the same positions in all three <Lewis structures>. Each line counts two <electrons>; each dot counts one. Each drawing has four <bond pairs>, eight <lone pairs>, <formal charge> $+1$ on <nitrogen>, $-1$ on each singly connected <oxygen>, and zero on the doubly connected <oxygen>. The brackets give the net <electric charge>; the arrows indicate <chemical resonance>, not a <chemical reaction> or elapsed time.}

Why not draw three N=O connections to make the <ion> look symmetric? With two <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>, that drawing still spends $12+12=24$ <electrons> and has the right total <formal charge>, but places twelve <electrons> around <nitrogen>. It fails the <octet rule> for this second-period <chemical element>. Three equivalent <resonance structures> express the symmetry without that invalid local count.

The economy is beautiful: a handful of dots and lines rules out many impossible counts and identifies <lone pairs> that can participate in <acid-base chemistry>. A <Lewis structure> helps choose rational candidate connections before a detailed <quantum chemistry> calculation. It does not specify a complete many-<electron> <wavefunction>, actual <electron density>, or molecular geometry. Adding the separate <VSEPR model>, described in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, 7.6: molecular structure and polarity]{ref}, predicts bent <water> and pyramidal <ammonia>; the layouts above alone make no such prediction. <Molecular orbital theory> supplies a richer electronic description, including the unpaired <electrons> of <molecular oxygen> that an all-paired <Lewis structure> misses.

In 1916 Gilbert N. Lewis proposed two-dot notation for the shared <electrons> of a <chemical bond> and fuller dot formulae for the outer <electrons>, explicitly relating a shared pair to the existing single-line notation. See https://doi.org/10.1021/ja02261a002[Lewis, The Atom and the Molecule, JACS 38, 762–785 (1916), pp. 777–778]{ref}, available as an https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[archived original paper]{ref}; the relevant passage is also readable in a https://www.chemteam.info/Chem-History/Lewis-1916/Lewis-1916.html[transcription of the original paper]{ref}. The modern <quantum mechanics> interpretation should be distinguished from his historical model of fixed outer <electrons>.

= Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki=VSEPR_theory}

= VSEPR model
{c}
{synonym}

= VSEPR theory
{c}
{synonym}

The <VSEPR model> predicts approximate <molecular geometry> by arranging the <electron domains> around a central <chemical atom> to reduce their mutual repulsion. Start with a <Lewis structure>, count the domains, and then distinguish the resulting <electron pair geometry> from the positions of the <chemical atoms> themselves. A <lone pair> counts as a domain even though it is not another <chemical atom>.https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/3-091-introduction-to-solid-state-chemistry-fall-2018/g9v8zj6VObw_transcript.pdf[MIT 3.091, lecture 11: Lewis structures, domains and molecular shapes]{ref}

Three worked counts have the same total of eight <valence electrons>. In <methane>, <carbon>'s four plus the contributions of four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> give $4+4=8$: four <bond pairs>, no <lone pairs>, and <tetrahedral molecular geometry>. In <ammonia>, <nitrogen>'s five plus three from <hydrogen> give $5+3=8$: three <bond pairs> and one <lone pair>, producing <trigonal pyramidal molecular geometry>. In <water>, <oxygen>'s six plus two from <hydrogen> give $6+2=8$: two <bond pairs> and two <lone pairs>, producing <bent molecular geometry>. All three have four <electron domains>; their <molecular geometries> differ.

\Image[vsepr-four-domain-shapes.png]
{title=Four electron domains give three molecular shapes as lone pairs replace bonded neighbours}

The regular tetrahedral directions can be represented by the <unit vectors>
$$
\frac1{\sqrt3}(1,1,1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(1,-1,-1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(-1,1,-1),\quad
\frac1{\sqrt3}(-1,-1,1).
$$
Each pair has <dot product> $-1/3$, so the ideal <bond angle> is $\theta=\arccos(-1/3)\approx109.47^\circ$. This is a geometric calculation, rather than a prediction that every four-domain <molecule> has that exact <bond angle>. <Lone pairs> and different kinds of <chemical bonds> distort the ideal arrangement; <water>'s H–O–H angle is about $104.5^\circ$.https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, 7.6: ideal geometry and water's bond angle]{ref}

The model is attractive because small-number bookkeeping produces a spatial prediction without first solving for every <molecular orbital>. Shape then helps explain <chemical polarity> and which <hydrogen bond donor> or <hydrogen bond acceptor> sites are accessible. It is a qualitative model, not a full calculation of the electronic <energy> or a universal solution for every <chemical compound>. Gillespie and Nyholm's 1957 review https://doi.org/10.1039/QR9571100339[Inorganic stereochemistry, Quarterly Reviews 11, 339–380]{ref} is a historical anchor for its development.

= Electron domain
{parent=Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory}

An <electron domain> is a region of <electron density> counted around a central <chemical atom> in the <VSEPR model>. One <lone pair> counts as one domain; a bond to one neighbour counts as one domain even when its <bond order> is two or three. Domain count is therefore different from simply counting <electron pairs>.

= Electron pair geometry
{parent=Valence shell electron pair repulsion theory}

<Electron pair geometry> describes the arrangement of all <electron domains> in the <VSEPR model>, including <lone pairs>. <Molecular geometry> instead describes the arrangement of <chemical atoms>. Four domains have a tetrahedral ideal arrangement, but keeping only the bonded neighbours gives the different <molecular geometries> of <methane>, <ammonia> and <water>.

= Bond pair
{parent=Lewis structure}

= Bonding pair
{synonym}

A <bond pair> is an <electron pair> allocated to a connection between two <chemical atoms> in a <Lewis structure>. A single line stands for one <bond pair>, or two <electrons>. A double connection contains two <bond pairs>, or four <electrons>; a triple connection contains three <bond pairs>, or six <electrons>. A <bond pair> is shared bookkeeping for the combined arrangement, rather than two distinguishable <electrons> each permanently owned by one endpoint.

In <water>, two <bond pairs> use $2\times2=4$ of the eight <valence electrons>. In <ammonia>, three <bond pairs> use $3\times2=6$ of the eight <valence electrons>. In $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each C=O connection contains two <bond pairs>, so there are four <bond pairs> in the entire <carbon dioxide> <molecule>. Counting connections and counting <bond pairs> therefore give different answers for multiple connections.

The same <bond pair> is treated differently for different questions: both endpoints count its two <electrons> for the <octet rule>, whereas each endpoint receives just one <electron> for <formal charge>. Neither convention asserts equal physical <electron density> at the endpoints. For electronic shapes, occupancy, and <energy>, see <molecular orbital theory> rather than treating a <bond pair> line as an entire <wavefunction>.

= Lone pair
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

A <lone pair> is an <electron pair> assigned to one <chemical atom> without a connecting line to another <chemical atom> in a <Lewis structure>. It is usually shown as two dots. <Water> has two <lone pairs> on <oxygen>, accounting for four <valence electrons>; <ammonia> has one <lone pair> on <nitrogen>, accounting for two. In $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each <oxygen> has two <lone pairs>, and <carbon> has none.

A <lone pair> can be reactive. In <protonation> of <ammonia>, the <nitrogen> <lone pair> becomes a new <bond pair> to a <proton>:
$$
\mathrm{NH}_3+\mathrm H^+\longrightarrow\mathrm{NH}_4^+.
$$
The <proton> supplies no <electron>, so the eight <valence electrons> are retained: three <bond pairs> plus one <lone pair> become four <bond pairs>. The resulting <nitrogen> has <formal charge> $5-0-8/2=+1$; each <hydrogen> has <formal charge> zero. This is why identifying an available <lone pair> helps explain the <chemical base> behaviour of <ammonia>, rather than merely decorating its formula.

Lewis already used the <ammonia> to $\mathrm{NH}_4^+$ example in https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[The Atom and the Molecule (1916), pp. 777–778]{ref}. In modern terms the dots identify an allocation of <electrons>, not two stationary particles or an isolated patch unaffected by the rest of the <molecule>. A <molecular orbital> description can spread electronic amplitude over several <chemical atoms> while a localized <Lewis structure> remains useful for <acid-base chemistry>.

= Formal charge
{parent=Lewis structure}
{title2=$\mathrm{FC}=v-n-b/2$}
{wiki}

The <formal charge> of a <chemical atom> in a <Lewis structure> assigns it all of its nonbonding <electrons> and half of the <electrons> in its connections:
$$
\mathrm{FC}=v-n-\frac b2.
$$
Here $v$ is the neutral <chemical atom>'s <valence electron> count, $n$ is the number of nonbonding <electrons> assigned to it, and $b$ is the number of bonding <electrons> incident on it. Count <electrons>, not <electron pairs>: two <lone pairs> mean $n=4$, and two double connections mean $b=8$. The number $\mathrm{FC}$ gives <electric charge> in units of the positive <elementary charge>. Equal splitting is a bookkeeping convention, regardless of the actual <electron density>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/08169[IUPAC Gold Book, formal charge]{ref}.

\Image[lewis-carbon-dioxide-formal-charge.png]
{title=Sixteen <valence electrons> in two <carbon dioxide> <Lewis structures>: $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ has zero <formal charges>, while $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$ has separated <formal charges>}
{description=The first <Lewis structure> has two <lone pairs> on each <oxygen>. The second has three <lone pairs> on the singly connected <oxygen>, one on the triply connected <oxygen>, and none on <carbon>. Both use four <bond pairs> and four <lone pairs>; both satisfy the <octet rule>. Their contributions to <chemical resonance> are unequal, and the drawn <formal charges> are not <partial atomic charges>.}

For $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, each <oxygen> has $v=6$, $n=4$, $b=4$, giving <formal charge> $6-4-4/2=0$. <Carbon> has $v=4$, $n=0$, $b=8$, giving <formal charge> $4-0-8/2=0$. For $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$, the left <oxygen> has <formal charge> $6-6-2/2=-1$, <carbon> still has <formal charge> zero, and the right <oxygen> has <formal charge> $6-2-6/2=+1$. The net <electric charge> is zero in either drawing.

The <Lewis structure> with no separated <formal charges> is the dominant contributor in this elementary comparison; the charge-separated drawing is a less important contributor, rather than an equally dominant alternative. This is a qualitative selection rule, not a calculation of <energy> or numerical <chemical resonance> weights. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.4: comparing carbon dioxide structures]{ref}. A zero <formal charge> does not imply zero <partial atomic charge>: the latter depends on partitioning the actual <electron density>, which a <Lewis structure> does not supply.

As a short consistency derivation, summing $n+b/2$ over all <chemical atoms> counts every drawn <electron> once: a nonbonding <electron> is assigned once, and a bonding <electron> contributes half at each of its two endpoints. Thus
$$
\sum_i\mathrm{FC}_i=\sum_i v_i-N_{\mathrm{valence}},
$$
where $N_{\mathrm{valence}}$ is the total drawn <valence electron> count. The right side is the net <electric charge> in units of <elementary charge>. This catches a wrong budget even when individual <octet rule> checks look plausible.

= Resonance
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

= Chemical resonance
{synonym}

A single <nitrate> <Lewis structure> singles out one N=O connection, yet the three N–O <chemical bonds> of the isolated symmetric <ion> are equivalent. <Chemical resonance> reconciles that symmetry with the integer <electron pair> allocations of a <Lewis structure>. The equal <chemical bond> lengths in <nitrate> are discussed in https://www.open.edu/openlearn/mod/oucontent/view.php?id=87848&section=_unit5.2[The Open University, Discovering chemistry: resonance structures]{ref}.

<Chemical resonance> uses several <Lewis structures> with the same <chemical atom> connections and total <electric charge> to describe electronic structure that one allocation cannot fully express. The <chemical atoms> stay in place; the positions of <bond pairs>, <lone pairs>, and <formal charges> differ between drawings. A two-headed arrow $\leftrightarrow$ connects the drawings and is distinct from a <chemical equilibrium> arrow $\rightleftharpoons$. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/08205[IUPAC Gold Book, resonance form]{ref}.

In each <nitrate> <resonance structure>, use the existing <formal charge> convention $\mathrm{FC}=v-n-b/2$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{FC}(\mathrm N)&=5-0-8/2=+1,\\
\mathrm{FC}(\text{singly connected O})&=6-6-2/2=-1,\\
\mathrm{FC}(\text{doubly connected O})&=6-4-4/2=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Here $v$ counts neutral-<chemical atom> <valence electrons>, $n$ counts nonbonding <electrons>, and $b$ counts incident bonding <electrons>. The sum is $+1-1-1+0=-1$, agreeing with the <nitrate ion>'s net <electric charge>. The <formal charge> rules and their distinction from actual atomic <electric charge> are given in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 7.4: formal charges and resonance]{ref}.

Keep one labelled <oxygen> fixed while reading the three <nitrate> <resonance structures>. Its connection is double in one drawing and single in two. Equal weighting within this three-contributor <Lewis structure> model gives the average <bond order>
$$
\overline b_{\mathrm{N-O}}=\frac{2+1+1}{3}=\frac43.
$$
The same calculation applies to all three N–O connections. Averaging the drawn <formal charges> similarly gives $-2/3$ per <oxygen>, while <nitrogen> retains $+1$; $+1+3(-2/3)=-1$. These fractions summarize the chosen contributors. They are not a measurement of <partial atomic charge>, a literal fraction of an <electron> sitting on an <oxygen>, or time spent in one drawing.

\Image[nitrate-delocalized-bonding.png]
{title=Equivalent N–O <chemical bonds> in symmetric <nitrate>: the equal-contributor <bond order> count is $4/3$}
{description=All three connections have the same graphical treatment. A solid line with a dashed companion is a schematic indication of shared multiple-<chemical bond> character, not a plot of <electron density> or a physical fractional line. The labels $4/3$ record an average within three octet <Lewis structures>; no universal, directly measured <bond order> of exactly $1.333\ldots$ is asserted.}

The <resonance hybrid> has one spatially continuous <electron density> extending over the <ion>. None of its N–O <chemical bonds> is permanently selected as the double connection of one contributor. The dashed marks above are symbols; <electrons> do not travel along them or make the marks float between <chemical atoms>. For isolated symmetric <nitrate>, symmetry makes the three N–O <chemical bonds> equivalent; an environment that distinguishes the <oxygen> sites can remove that equivalence. The fraction $4/3$ alone predicts neither a <chemical bond> length nor a dissociation <energy>. The general relation between multiple <chemical bonds>, lengths, and strengths is discussed in <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.5 Strengths of Ionic and Covalent Bonds>; its tabulated averages are not a nitrate-specific calibration of $4/3$.

<Ozone> provides a useful two-contributor comparison. Its $3\times6=18$ <valence electrons> give three <bond pairs> and six <lone pairs>. One <resonance structure> has a double O–O connection on the left and a single one on the right; the other reverses these allocations while retaining the same <chemical atom> positions and connections. The central <oxygen> has one <lone pair> and <formal charge> $+1$; the singly connected terminal <oxygen> has three <lone pairs> and <formal charge> $-1$; the doubly connected terminal <oxygen> has two <lone pairs> and <formal charge> zero. Thus $6+12=18$ <electrons> and zero net <electric charge> are conserved. The equal-contributor <bond order> count is $(2+1)/2=3/2$ for either connection. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-111sc-principles-of-chemical-science-fall-2014/3549685d450773a066a716832634203f_MIT5_111F14_Lec10.pdf[MIT 5.111, lecture 10, pp. 4–5: ozone resonance structures]{ref}.

For <carbon dioxide>, $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ and $\mathrm{O^-{-}C\equiv O^+}$ share sixteen <valence electrons> and the same O–C–O connections. The mirror drawing $\mathrm{O^+\equiv C{-}O^-}$ is also possible. The two charge-separated drawings are equivalent to one another for an isolated symmetric <carbon dioxide> <molecule>, but neither is equally dominant with $\mathrm{O=C=O}$ in the elementary <formal charge> comparison.

<Chemical resonance> does not mean that a <molecule> rapidly flips between drawings, nor that separate <molecules> at <chemical equilibrium> carry the individual drawings. The actual <electron density> belongs to one combined electronic description. In a <quantum mechanics> treatment, contributors enter a <linear combination> of many-<electron> <wavefunctions>; a <molecular orbital> treatment can describe the extended electronic arrangement through <molecular orbitals>. The drawings alone provide neither a measured density nor numerical weights. Their usefulness is to expose the limitations of one localized allocation without pretending that every algebraically allowed <Lewis structure> contributes equally.

= Resonance structure
{parent=Resonance (chemistry)}

= Resonance form
{synonym}

= Resonance contributor
{synonym}

A <resonance structure> is one <Lewis structure> contributing to a <chemical resonance> description. The contributors retain the same <atomic nuclei> in the same positions, the same connections, and the same total <valence electron> count and <electric charge>; allocations of <bond pairs>, <lone pairs>, and <formal charges> differ. They are alternative representations of one electronic structure, not distinct substances with different atomic arrangements. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Chemistry 2e, 7.4: resonance forms]{ref}.

= Resonance hybrid
{parent=Resonance (chemistry)}

A <resonance hybrid> is the combined electronic description expressed using several <resonance structures>. The word does not describe a mixture of separate <molecules> or <ions>: a <nitrate ion> has one <electron density>, rather than three species in <chemical equilibrium>. Equal weighting is appropriate for the equivalent <nitrate> contributors in the elementary model; the unequal <carbon dioxide> contributors illustrate why it is not a general rule. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-4-formal-charges-and-resonance[Chemistry 2e, 7.4: resonance hybrids do not fluctuate between forms]{ref}.

= Octet rule
{parent=Lewis structure}
{wiki}

The <octet rule> is a useful empirical guide: many <Lewis structures> of <chemical compounds> containing <carbon>, <nitrogen>, and <oxygen> give each of those <chemical atoms> eight <electrons> when nonbonding <electrons> and all incident bonding <electrons> are counted. <Hydrogen> instead normally counts two <electrons>. These are local counts, not independent budgets: a shared <bond pair> counts at both endpoints but only once in the <molecule>'s <valence electron> total.

In <water>, <oxygen> counts $4+4=8$ <electrons>; in <ammonia>, <nitrogen> counts $2+6=8$; in $\mathrm{O=C=O}$, <carbon> counts $0+8=8$ and each <oxygen> counts $4+4=8$. The charge-separated <carbon dioxide> drawing also satisfies the <octet rule>, so the <octet rule> alone cannot select the dominant contributor. <Formal charge> provides another check.

The <octet rule> is a heuristic rather than a theorem about every <chemical bond>. Odd <valence electron> totals cannot be partitioned entirely into <electron pairs>, and some valid <Lewis structures> have fewer or more than eight <electrons> around a <chemical atom>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-3-lewis-symbols-and-structures[Chemistry 2e, 7.3: exceptions to the octet rule]{ref}. The capacity of the $s$ and $p$ <electron subshells>, $2+6=8$, helps motivate the guide for these <chemical elements>, but does not prove universal compliance. Lewis discussed a stable outer group of eight, together with a two-<electron> arrangement for <hydrogen>, in https://zenodo.org/records/1429068[The Atom and the Molecule (1916), pp. 768 and 774]{ref}.

= Valence electron
{parent=Chemical bond}
{wiki}

A <valence electron> belongs to the electronic arrangement used to describe a <chemical atom>'s <chemical bonds> and ordinary <chemical reactions>. For the neutral <hydrogen>, <carbon>, <nitrogen>, and <oxygen> <chemical atoms> used here, the <valence electrons> occupy the outermost occupied <electron shell>. Their respective <electron configurations> are $1s^1$, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^2$, $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^3$, and $1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^4$, giving one, four, five, and six <valence electrons>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Chemistry 2e, 6.4: electron configurations and valence electrons]{ref}.

<Lewis structures> show these <valence electrons> while omitting the inner <electrons>. Consequently, eight drawn <valence electrons> in <water> do not mean eight total <electrons>: neutral <water> has ten, with two inner <oxygen> <electrons> omitted. Neutral <ammonia> likewise has ten total <electrons> but eight <valence electrons>; neutral <carbon dioxide> has twenty-two total <electrons> but sixteen <valence electrons>.

This distinction makes the small counts useful: connections and possible <lone pairs> can be organized without representing every <electron> or solving the entire many-<electron> <wavefunction>. The outermost-<electron shell> shortcut applies to the examples above, rather than serving as a universal definition for all <chemical elements>; the electronic arrangement relevant to <chemical bonds> can also involve other <electron subshells>.

= Polymer
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <polymer> is a large molecular chain or network built from many chemically linked repeating units. Statistical chain models idealize its microscopic structure by segment vectors and describe its conformation through distributions, correlations and scattering. A <freely jointed chain> fixes each segment length while allowing independent orientations; a <Gaussian chain> replaces the segment or coarse-grained displacement distribution by a <Gaussian distribution>. These ideal models neglect excluded-volume and other segment interactions.

= Flory approximation
{c}
{parent=Polymer}
{title2=$\mathcal F/(k_BT)\sim R^2/(Nb^2)+vN^2/R^d$}

The <Flory approximation> balances the entropic cost of expanding an <ideal chain> against a mean-field <excluded volume> interaction. For $N$ segments of length $b$ in $d$ spatial dimensions, minimizing $R^2/(Nb^2)+vN^2/R^d$ gives $R^{d+2}\sim vN^3b^2$. This yields the scaling exponents $3/5$ in three dimensions and $3/4$ in two dimensions. It is a scaling approximation, not an exact numerical free energy or proof of critical exponents; confinement replaces $R^d$ by the accessible coil volume.

= Polymerization
{parent=Polymer}

<Polymerization> converts a <monomer>, or a mixture of <monomers>, into a <polymer>: repeated <chemical reactions> establish a connected molecular chain or network. The central question is how connections form, not merely whether a material becomes solid. Joining small <molecules> can produce a thread that can be handled, while the individual <molecules> remain invisible. See https://old.iupac.org/publications/pac/1974/pdf/4003x0477.pdf[IUPAC, Basic Definitions of Terms Relating to Polymers, definition 2.2]{ref}.

\Image[polymerization-monomers-interface-units.png]
{id=polymerization-monomers-interface-units}
{title=From two liquid layers to a <nylon 6,10> thread: <monomers>, a <constitutional repeating unit>, and statistical segments}
{description=Original schematic of the authored UCSB <polymerization> demonstration described below. Chemical formulas identify the actual reactants and interior repeat; colored blocks identify their contributions. The lower coarse-graining sketch deliberately groups three repeats per model segment. Shapes, sizes, and that grouping are illustrative, not measured molecular geometry or a fitted <Kuhn length>.}

In <step-growth polymerization>, complementary reactive groups on <monomers> and on shorter or longer chains can react with one another. A two-unit chain can join a three-unit chain to make a five-unit chain; growth does not require a specially active end to add only free <monomers>. An example is the <nylon 6,10> reaction of $\mathrm{H_2N(CH_2)_6NH_2}$ with $\mathrm{ClC(=O)(CH_2)_8C(=O)Cl}$: each <amine> end can form an <amide> connection with an acid-chloride end, with formal elimination of $\mathrm{HCl}$. Both reactants have two suitable ends, allowing repeated joining. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/5474dc576b0669a8a09e9b70e39b1239_lec02_09082006.pdf[Paula Hammond, MIT 10.569 lecture 2: step-growth joining]{ref} and https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/d9f9ac7021b0d0f418d3d3c90dbfb94b_lec05_09152006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 5, p. 4: acid chloride–amine reaction]{ref}.

In <chain-growth polymerization>, a <monomer> reacts with an active site on a growing chain, and the growth step regenerates an active site. Schematically, $P_j^*+M\longrightarrow P_{j+1}^*$, where $j$ counts incorporated <monomeric units> and the star denotes reactivity rather than a particular <electric charge>. <Ethene> forming <polyethylene> is an example: the double <chemical bond> is converted into single <chemical bonds> as the <carbon> backbone grows. Initiation creates the active chains; subsequent reactions can deactivate them or transfer reactivity to another <molecule>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00958/plain[IUPAC: chain polymerization]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/31-1-chain-growth-polymers[John McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 31.1: chain growth]{ref}. Whether growth is stepwise or carried by an active site is a different question from whether small byproducts are released: <chain-growth polymerization> can also release them.

\b[A motivating experiment: drawing nylon from a liquid interface.] Feldwinn's UCSB demonstration places $25\,\mathrm{mL}$ of an aqueous <chemical solution> of hexamethylenediamine, $\mathrm{H_2N(CH_2)_6NH_2}$, in a $100\,\mathrm{mL}$ beaker, then gently adds $25\,\mathrm{mL}$ of sebacoyl chloride, $\mathrm{ClC(=O)(CH_2)_8C(=O)Cl}$, in an organic <solvent>. The procedure specifies hexane; other parts of that handout list cyclohexane. The layers remain separate. The instructor catches material at their interface with tweezers and winds it slowly onto a glass rod; the handout reports continued spooling of <nylon 6,10>. It specifies freshly prepared acid-chloride <chemical solution> and washing the thread before touching it. These are observations and conditions of a supervised laboratory demonstration, not measurements of chain lengths. See https://people.chem.ucsb.edu/feldwinn/darby/DemoLibrary/DemoPDFs/Demo044.pdf[Feldwinn, UCSB Demonstration Library, Demo 044, pp. 1–3]{ref}.

The mechanistic interpretation is that the <monomers> encounter one another near the interface, where <amide> connections produce the film; withdrawing it exposes a region where more material can form. An authored research demonstration independently reports a rapidly formed interfacial film collapsing into a thread on withdrawal, using cyclohexane and an aqueous <chemical solution> containing potassium hydroxide. See https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jchemed.6b00767[Polymer Day: Outreach Experiments for High School Students, Journal of Chemical Education (2017), nylon demonstration]{ref}. This is the striking observation: a thread much longer than the beaker can emerge because material is continually supplied to the formation region, rather than because a pre-existing thread was hidden in the liquid.

Useful \i[proposed controls], not results reported in those demonstrations, omit one <monomer> at a time while retaining the same <solvents>, liquid amounts, and pulling procedure. The prediction is loss of a renewable drawable <nylon 6,10> film; merely finding cloudiness would not establish <polymerization>, since other <chemical reactions>, including acid-chloride <hydrolysis>, can occur. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/21-4-chemistry-of-acid-halides[McMurry, 21.4: competing acid-chloride hydrolysis]{ref}. Stopping and restarting withdrawal would probe renewal at the interface. Even successful drawing does not measure the <degree of polymerization>, establish a chain-length <probability distribution>, or prove the entire growth mechanism. Chemical identification and a molecular-size measurement would be needed for those stronger conclusions. These controls and limits are inferences from the setup, not invented experimental data.

\b[Why almost complete reaction matters in step growth.] For ideal linear <step-growth polymerization>, assume exactly two reactive groups per starting <molecule>, equal numbers of complementary groups, only joining of different chains, no rings, no branching, and no loss of chain material. Let $N_0$ count starting <monomer molecules>, including both reactant species, and let $p$ be the fraction of either type of reactive group consumed. There are initially $N_0$ groups of each type, so $pN_0$ connections reduce the number of chain-bearing <molecules> to $N=N_0(1-p)$; the count excludes small byproducts but includes unreacted <monomers>. The number-average <degree of polymerization>, measured in <monomeric units>, is therefore
$$
X_n=\frac{N_0}{N}=\frac{1}{1-p}.
$$
Thus $p=0.90$, $0.99$, and $0.999$ give $X_n=10$, $100$, and $1000$. Losing the last fraction of unreacted groups matters enormously. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/c7b7a987f7d5b66e0832be42fbf52b7d_lec03_09112006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 3: balanced step-growth conversion]{ref}. This counts residues of both feed species; a <nylon 6,10> interior <constitutional repeating unit> contains two such <monomeric units>.

For an $AA+BB$ feed with $A_0\leq B_0$ initial reactive-group counts, put $r=A_0/B_0$ and now let $p$ denote the fraction of the \i[limiting $A$ groups] consumed. Under the same linear, no-ring assumptions, $N_0=(A_0+B_0)/2$ and $N=N_0-pA_0$, giving
$$
X_n=\frac{1+r}{1+r-2rp}.
$$
For $r=0.98$, even complete consumption of $A$ gives $X_n=(1.98)/(0.02)=99$. <Stoichiometry> controls chain length as well as yield. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/5474dc576b0669a8a09e9b70e39b1239_lec02_09082006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 2, pp. 2–3: stoichiometric imbalance]{ref}. The liquid-interface demonstration is not a homogeneous, balanced reactor, so its thread length cannot be inserted into this formula as a molecular chain length.

\Image[polymerization-monomers-conversion-models.png]
{id=polymerization-monomers-conversion-models}
{title=Two ideal <degree of polymerization> counts: late growth in <step-growth polymerization> and fixed-chain growth in ideal <living polymerization>}
{description=Toy analytic curves, not measured data. Left: $X_n=(1+r)/(1+r-2rp)$, with $p$ the limiting-group conversion and $r$ the initial reactive-group ratio. Right: $X_n=qR$, with $q$ the fraction of feed <monomer molecules> incorporated and $R$ their initial number per initiated chain. The two horizontal axes count different entities; the right plot represents a special ideal model, not all <chain-growth polymerization>.}

\b[A contrasting chain count.] In ideal <living polymerization>, assume no chain termination or irreversible chain transfer, all chains initiated before the counted growth, one chain per effective initiator, a fixed chain count $C$, and every consumed <monomer molecule> incorporated into those chains. If $M_0$ is the initial number of feed <monomer molecules> and $q$ their incorporated fraction, conservation gives $X_n=qM_0/C=qR$ in <monomeric units>, excluding initiator fragments. For $R=200$, incorporating half the feed gives $X_n=100$. If initiation consumes one <monomer molecule> per chain, the fixed-chain interval starts at $q=1/R$, rather than $q=0$. See https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/42d2e82b585136f5b6849919a0ee4449_lec21_11012006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 21, p. 1: ideal living-chain count]{ref}. The mean does not imply identical chain lengths. <Living polymerization> does not require reversible loss of <monomers>; the defining absence of chain termination and irreversible chain transfer is explained in https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/IUPAC_PolymEdu_EduWorkshop_PPT_Shipp2.pdf[Devon Shipp, IUPAC Polymer Education workshop, Radical Polymerizations II, slide 50]{ref}. Ordinary <chain-growth polymerization> with new chains, termination, or transfer need not follow the straight line.

The useful connection is between local chemistry and a macroscopic material: selecting reactive ends and their proportions controls connectivity, while the resulting <polymer>'s conformation is a separate problem described by models such as the <freely jointed chain> or <Gaussian chain>. Formation of <chemical bonds>, the count of <monomeric units>, and the count of statistical segments must be kept distinct.

= Step-growth polymerization
{parent=Polymerization}
{wiki}

In <step-growth polymerization>, suitable reactive groups on <molecules> of different chain lengths can join. The linear count and its assumptions are given in <polymerization>.

= Chain-growth polymerization
{parent=Polymerization}
{wiki}

= Chain polymerization
{synonym}

In <chain-growth polymerization>, growth proceeds through reaction of a <monomer> with an active chain site that is regenerated by the growth step. See <polymerization> and https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00958/plain[IUPAC: chain polymerization]{ref}.

= Living polymerization
{parent=Chain-growth polymerization}
{wiki}

<Living polymerization> is <chain-growth polymerization> without chain termination or irreversible chain transfer. A linear mean <degree of polymerization> versus feed conversion additionally needs the fixed-chain assumptions in <polymerization>; reversibility of <monomer> addition is not required. See https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/03/IUPAC_PolymEdu_EduWorkshop_PPT_Shipp2.pdf[Shipp, IUPAC workshop, slide 50]{ref}.

= Degree of polymerization
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}
{title2=$X,\ X_n$}

The <degree of polymerization> counts <monomeric units> in a chain. $X_n$ is its number average over a specified collection of chains. State whether unreacted <monomers> are included and distinguish this count from <constitutional repeating units> or statistical segments. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0304[IUPAC: degree of polymerization]{ref}.

= Repeat unit
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}

= Constitutional repeating unit
{synonym}

A <constitutional repeating unit> is the smallest chemical unit whose repetition describes a regular chain. Its boundaries mark repetition, not necessarily the origin of the <chemical atoms> from a single <monomer>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01286/plain[IUPAC: constitutional repeating unit]{ref}.

= Nylon 6,10
{c}
{parent=Polymer}

<Nylon 6,10> has an <amide>-linked chain whose <constitutional repeating unit> contains a six-<carbon> diamine contribution and a ten-<carbon> diacid-derived contribution. See <monomer> for its <stoichiometry> and <polymerization> for the authored demonstration.

= Polyethylene
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}

In ideal linear <polyethylene>, incorporated <ethene> contributes $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}}$ <monomeric units>; the smallest <constitutional repeating unit> is $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}}$. See <monomer> for the counting distinction.

= Monomer
{parent=Polymer}

A <monomer> is a <chemical substance> whose <molecules> can undergo <polymerization> and contribute units to the essential structure of a <polymer>. A <monomer molecule> is the individual reactant; its incorporated <monomeric unit> is the portion identifiable as derived from that reactant. Incorporation can change <chemical bonds> and release small byproducts, so the unit in the chain need not have the complete <molecular formula> of the feed <molecule>. See https://old.goldbook.iupac.org/html/M/M04019.html[IUPAC: monomer molecule]{ref} and https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0304[IUPAC, A brief guide to polymer terminology: monomeric units]{ref}.

Three different counts answer three different questions. <Monomer molecules> describe the feed; <monomeric units> trace that feed's contributions to the product; a <constitutional repeating unit> describes the smallest chemical pattern repeating along a regular chain. In <nylon 6,10>, two different <monomers> supply one interior <constitutional repeating unit>,
$$
\mathrm{{-}NH{-}(CH_2)_6{-}NH{-}C(=O){-}(CH_2)_8{-}C(=O){-}}.
$$
Its <molecular formula> is $\mathrm{C_{16}H_{30}N_2O_2}$. The diamine feed is $\mathrm{C_6H_{16}N_2}$, and the diacid-chloride feed is $\mathrm{C_{10}H_{16}Cl_2O_2}$; subtracting two $\mathrm{HCl}$ gives the interior-repeat budget. For a finite linear chain made from $n$ of each reactant, there are $2n-1$ joining connections, hence $2n-1$ formal $\mathrm{HCl}$ eliminations, rather than $2n$. One <amine> end and one acid-chloride end remain in that ideal unmodified chain. This is a <stoichiometry> check on the depicted reaction, not a claim that the washed experimental thread retains those ends. The reacting end-group chemistry is documented in https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/10-569-synthesis-of-polymers-fall-2006/d9f9ac7021b0d0f418d3d3c90dbfb94b_lec05_09152006.pdf[Hammond, lecture 5, p. 4]{ref}; the specified <nylon 6,10> reactant pair is reported in https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acs.jchemed.6b00767[Polymer Day, nylon demonstration]{ref}.

<Ethene> shows the reverse counting mismatch. One $\mathrm{CH_2{=}CH_2}$ <monomer molecule> contributes a two-<carbon> $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}}$ <monomeric unit> to ideal linear <polyethylene>, without a small byproduct in that growth step. But the smallest <constitutional repeating unit> of its uniform backbone is just $\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}}$: a chain containing $m$ incorporated <ethene> <monomeric units> contains $2m$ such interior one-<carbon> repeats. End groups are additional bookkeeping. The frequently drawn $[\mathrm{{-}CH_2{-}CH_2{-}}]_m$ is useful for tracing the feed, although it is not the smallest constitutional repeat. The minimum-pattern definition is given in https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01286/plain[IUPAC: constitutional repeating unit]{ref}; the <ethene> growth chemistry is described by https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/31-1-chain-growth-polymers[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 31.1]{ref}.

A segment of a <freely jointed chain> or <Gaussian chain> instead belongs to a statistical model. It can represent many <chemical bonds> and several <constitutional repeating units>; its length and the segment count are chosen to describe chain conformation at the intended scale. The three-repeats-per-segment drawing in <polymerization-monomers-interface-units> is a deliberately arbitrary coarse-graining example, not a measured <Kuhn length>. Always state the counting convention before substituting a segment count for a <degree of polymerization>. This separation lets chemistry track which <chemical atoms> entered the material while statistical models track how the material bends and extends.

= Monomeric unit
{parent=Monomer}

An incorporated <monomeric unit> is the largest constitutional unit identifiable as derived from one <monomer molecule>. It need not be a whole unreacted <molecule> or the smallest <constitutional repeating unit>. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2023-0304[IUPAC: monomeric unit]{ref}.

= Monomer molecule
{parent=Monomer}

An individual <molecule> capable of contributing structural units through <polymerization>. See https://old.goldbook.iupac.org/html/M/M04019.html[IUPAC: monomer molecule]{ref}.

= Ethylene
{parent=Monomer}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_2{=}CH_2}$}

= Ethene
{synonym}

<Ethene> is the two-<carbon> <monomer> in the <polyethylene> example in <monomer>.

= Nucleic acid
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}

A <nucleic acid> is a <polymer> built from linked <nucleotides>. The two principal kinds are <DNA> and <RNA>. Each <nucleotide> contributes a <sugar>, a <nucleobase>, and <phosphate> to the chain; the <nucleobase> is only the letter-bearing component, not the entire <nucleotide>. <DNA> uses <deoxyribose>, whereas <RNA> uses <ribose>. A chain of six <nucleotides> is therefore not six free <nucleobases> placed beside one another. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Nucleic-Acids[NHGRI: nucleic acids]{ref}.

The chemical scaffold and the variable lettering do different jobs. <Covalent bonds> maintain the <sugar>–<phosphate> backbone, while the order of attached <nucleobases> specifies the <nucleic acid sequence>. A <phosphodiester bond> links the $3'$ position of one <sugar> to the $5'$ position of the next through <phosphate>. These position numbers refer to <carbon> <chemical atoms> in the <sugar>; they define a strand's direction even when it bends or forms a circle. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/[Brown, Genomes, 2nd edition: the human genome]{ref}.

This division is useful: the backbone supplies a connected physical record, and <base pairing> allows its letters to recognize partners. <DNA replication> copies that record, and <transcription> uses a <DNA> template to make <RNA>. <RNA> can also fold into functional shapes through <base pairing> within one chain; a <nucleic acid sequence> specifies chemical order, not a requirement for a straight shape. The beauty is that the same kind of <polymer> can carry a message and form a structure that acts on messages.

= Nucleic acid sequence
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

= Nucleotide sequence
{synonym}

A <nucleic acid sequence> records the ordered <nucleobases> of a <nucleic acid> strand, conventionally read from $5'$ to $3'$. The standard <DNA> alphabet is <adenine> A, <cytosine> C, <guanine> G, and <thymine> T; the standard <RNA> alphabet uses <uracil> U in place of <thymine> T. Each letter denotes the <nucleobase> attached to one <nucleotide>, so the six-letter <DNA> <nucleic acid sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTAC}\text{-}3'$ describes six linked <nucleotides>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Base-Pair[NHGRI: representing DNA by a sequence of letters]{ref}.

Order carries information that composition loses. Both ACGTAC and AACCGT contain two <adenine>, two <cytosine>, one <guanine>, and one <thymine> <nucleobase>, but their <nucleic acid sequences> differ at positions 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. At position 2, for example, the first has <cytosine> and the second <adenine>. Reporting only the four counts cannot reconstruct either ordered record.

To obtain the partner of $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ACGTAC}\text{-}3'$, apply the <DNA> <base pairing> rules position by position: A selects T, C selects G, G selects C, T selects A, A selects T, and C selects G. Alignment gives $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TGCATG}\text{-}5'$. Its opposite end labels matter because the two are <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>. Reading the partner from its own $5'$ end reverses the aligned lettering and yields the <reverse complement>, $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GTACGT}\text{-}3'$.

\Image[dna-reverse-complement.png]
{title=Complement, then reverse: <DNA> ACGTAC has aligned partner TGCATG and <reverse complement> GTACGT}

Reversal alone gives CATGCA; complementation alone gives aligned TGCATG. Neither operation alone gives the partner written in the usual $5'$-to-$3'$ convention. Applying the <reverse complement> operation twice returns ACGTAC: GTACGT complements to CATGCA, which reverses to ACGTAC. This convention makes a single reported strand sufficient to specify an ideally complementary <DNA> duplex, provided its direction is known.

= Reverse complement
{parent=Nucleic acid sequence}

A <nucleotide sequence> obtained by reversing the order and complementing each <nucleobase> according to the appropriate <base pairing> rules.

= Base pair
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=bp}

A <base pair> is an association of two <nucleobases> through <hydrogen bonds> in a compatible arrangement. It commonly joins opposite strands of a <nucleic acid double helix>, but can also join two regions of one folded <RNA> strand. Canonical Watson–Crick <DNA> <base pairs> match <adenine> with <thymine> and <guanine> with <cytosine>; canonical <RNA> <base pairs> use <adenine> with <uracil> instead. The <nucleobases> belong to complete <nucleotides>, including their attached <sugars> and backbone <phosphate>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Base-Pair[NHGRI: base pair]{ref}.

The abbreviation bp also measures the length of a paired region. Our ACGTAC/TGCATG duplex has six <base pairs>, containing twelve <nucleotides> altogether. Its upper strand contains two <adenine>, two <cytosine>, one <guanine>, and one <thymine> <nucleobase>; the lower contains one, one, two, and two respectively. Combined, the counts are three of each <nucleobase>. Equality of total <adenine> and <thymine>, or of total <guanine> and <cytosine>, need not hold within either individual strand.

= Base pairing
{parent=Base pair}

<Base pairing> is the recognition and association of compatible <nucleobases>. In canonical Watson–Crick geometry, an <adenine>–<thymine> <base pair> has two <hydrogen bonds>, while a <guanine>–<cytosine> <base pair> has three. These counts refer to those specific geometries and usual chemical forms; they are not a definition of every possible <base pair>. <RNA> can also contain other pairings, and canonical <adenine>–<uracil> pairing replaces <adenine>–<thymine> pairing. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/14-2-dna-structure-and-sequencing[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e: DNA structure]{ref}.

\Image[dna-antiparallel-base-pairs.png]
{title=Six canonical Watson–Crick <DNA> <base pairs>: opposite backbone directions and two or three <hydrogen bonds> per pair}

For this illustrative duplex, the six <base pairs> are A–T, C–G, G–C, T–A, A–T, and C–G. Thus three contribute two <hydrogen bonds> each and three contribute three each:
$$
3\times2+3\times3=15.
$$
Each dotted connector in the diagram counts one <hydrogen bond>; coloured boxes denote whole <nucleobases>, not individual <chemical atoms>. The solid rails denote the <covalent bond> scaffold. Separating the two strands disrupts <base pairing> and <base stacking> without requiring the backbone to be cut.

The count of fifteen <hydrogen bonds> is not fifteen identical contributions to a duplex's <Gibbs free energy>. <Base stacking>, interactions with <water> and <ions>, <temperature>, and the order of neighbouring <base pairs> also matter. Knowing the fraction of <guanine>–<cytosine> <base pairs> alone does not fix a <nucleic acid double helix>'s shape or stability. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/[Brown, Genomes: pairing and stacking interactions]{ref}. What makes <base pairing> especially useful is its selectivity: an exposed <DNA> strand constrains the partner's <nucleic acid sequence>, giving <DNA replication> a chemical recognition rule.

= Nucleic acid double helix
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

A <nucleic acid double helix> consists of two <nucleic acid> strands winding around a common axis. In the familiar <DNA> arrangement, <sugar>–<phosphate> backbones lie outside and paired <nucleobases> lie inside, stacked along the axis. The strands are <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>. The flattened <base pairing> ladder above displays partners and direction; winding that ladder into a <helix> adds spatial organization. <RNA> can also form double-helical paired regions, so the geometry is not exclusive to <DNA>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: DNA structure]{ref}.

The useful surprise is that a structure explains a copying principle. Complementary <base pairs> allow each <DNA> strand to specify the other's <nucleic acid sequence>; separate strands can each act as templates in <DNA replication>. This is a structural constraint, while the <enzymes> and stages of <DNA replication> supply the actual process. A <nucleic acid sequence> specifies letter order without, by itself, giving every detail of the three-dimensional <helix>.

On 25 April 1953, James Watson and Francis Crick published a model with two oppositely directed chains, outside <phosphate> groups, and specific <base pairs>. Their short paper explicitly connected this pairing to a possible copying mechanism, while describing the model as requiring further experimental tests. See the original https://doi.org/10.1038/171737a0[Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids: A Structure for Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid, Nature 171, 737–738]{ref}, with an accessible https://inters.org/files/watsoncrick1953.pdf[reprint of the original paper]{ref}.

Rosalind Franklin and Raymond Gosling's paper in the same issue had a different role: it presented <X-ray> <diffraction> evidence from <DNA> fibres and its structural interpretation, including the helical character of the wetter B form and evidence for <phosphate> groups on the outside. They distinguished forms produced at different water contents and assessed compatibility with the Watson–Crick model. Read their original https://doi.org/10.1038/171740a0[Molecular Configuration in Sodium Thymonucleate, Nature 171, 740–741 (1953)]{ref}. The model and the experimental analysis are distinct contributions; a <diffraction> pattern is evidence constraining a <molecular geometry>, rather than a direct picture of letter-by-letter <base pairing>.

= Antiparallel nucleic acid strands
{parent=Nucleic acid double helix}

Two <nucleic acid> strands with opposite directional alignment: a $5'$-to-$3'$ direction on one is aligned with the $3'$-to-$5'$ direction on the other.

= Base stacking
{parent=Nucleic acid double helix}

<Base stacking> is the close arrangement and interaction of neighbouring <nucleobases> with their approximately planar faces above one another. Along a <nucleic acid double helix>, neighbouring <base pairs> stack successively; <base pairing> instead associates partners across the two strands. Both affect duplex stability, and the interactions depend on the identities and arrangement of neighbouring <nucleobases>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21134/[Brown, Genomes: base pairing and base stacking]{ref}.

= Stem-loop
{parent=Nucleic acid}
{wiki}

= Deoxyribonucleic acid
{parent=Nucleic acid}

= DNA
{c}
{synonym}

<Deoxyribonucleic acid>, or <DNA>, is a <nucleotide> <polymer> that stores biological information in its <nucleotide sequence>. The crucial variable is order: two <DNA> <molecules> can contain the same numbers of each <nucleobase> yet carry different <nucleotide sequences>. This makes a physical <molecule> an inheritable record, rather than merely a mixture of ingredients. <Genes> are functional regions of that record; the whole <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> is not one long <gene>. See https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Deoxyribonucleic-Acid-Fact-Sheet[NHGRI: DNA fact sheet]{ref}.

Each <DNA> strand has a backbone of alternating <deoxyribose> <sugars> and <phosphate> groups joined through <covalent bonds>. Attached <nucleobases> supply the familiar four-letter alphabet: <adenine> A, <thymine> T, <guanine> G, and <cytosine> C. In ordinary double-stranded <DNA>, <base pairing> matches A with T and G with C. The backbone connects successive <nucleotides> along a strand; <base pairing> connects partners across strands. Keeping these two connections distinct explains how strands can separate while each retains its own <nucleotide sequence>.

The two backbones have directions, conventionally labelled $5'$ and $3'$ after numbered <carbon> <chemical atoms> in <deoxyribose>. They form <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>: following one from $5'$ to $3'$ takes you opposite to the other's $5'$-to-$3'$ direction. For the chosen <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{ATGCC}\text{-}3'$, write its partner directly underneath as $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$. Now read that partner from its own $5'$ end: its <reverse complement> is $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GGCAT}\text{-}3'$. Complementing letters and reversing their order are separate operations. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26821/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the structure and function of DNA]{ref}.

\Image[dna-antiparallel-complement.png]
{title=<DNA> <base pairing> and <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>: an unrolled ladder for ATGCC and its <reverse complement>}

The diagram's rails represent <sugar>–<phosphate> backbones and its coloured blocks represent <nucleobases>, not individual <chemical atoms>. Reading left to right gives five matched positions: A–T, T–A, G–C, C–G, C–G. There are two A–T-type <base pairs> and three G–C-type <base pairs>; the complete two-strand example contains ten <nucleotides>. Reversing the bottom row changes the reading convention, not the physical partners. Real double-stranded <DNA> winds into a <nucleic acid double helix>; the flattened ladder emphasizes <base pairing> and direction without pretending to show its three-dimensional geometry.

The motivating consequence is copying by a template. After strands separate, each existing <DNA> strand specifies which incoming <nucleotides> should accompany it through <base pairing>. <DNA replication> uses <enzymes> to build complementary strands, allowing the record to persist through successive <biological cells>. <Base pairing> supplies a selection rule, while <enzymes> perform the chemistry: a matching diagram alone is not a complete copying mechanism. The connection between structure and copying was already central to the model published by Watson and Crick on 25 April 1953. See https://www.nature.com/articles/171737a0[Watson and Crick, Molecular Structure of Nucleic Acids, Nature 171, 737–738 (1953)]{ref}.

Stored information needs interpretation. Some <DNA> regions specify <proteins> through <RNA>; others contribute to functional <RNA> or regulate <gene expression>, and some have no known function. <Non-coding DNA> means sequence that does not specify a <protein>'s sequence of <amino acids>, not automatically useless sequence. Nor does a <DNA> <nucleotide sequence> alone determine an <organism>'s fate: <phenotypes> also depend on biological context and environment. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Non-Coding-DNA[NHGRI: non-coding DNA]{ref}. Its double-stranded form is often modeled mechanically as a <worm-like chain>, with <elasticity> and effective <excluded volume> depending on solvent conditions.

= Ribonucleic acid
{parent=Nucleic acid}

= RNA
{c}
{synonym}

<Ribonucleic acid>, or <RNA>, is a <nucleotide> <polymer> whose <nucleotide sequence> can both carry information and organize a working molecular shape. Its backbone contains alternating <ribose> <sugars> and <phosphate> groups connected through <covalent bonds>. Its usual <nucleobases> are <adenine> A, <uracil> U, <guanine> G, and <cytosine> C. Thus <RNA> uses U where <DNA> uses T; its <ribose> also differs chemically from <DNA>'s <deoxyribose>. See https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Ribonucleic-Acid-RNA[NHGRI: RNA]{ref}.

In <transcription (biology)>, an <enzyme> uses one <DNA> strand as a template for <RNA>. For example, the <DNA> template $3'\text{-}\mathrm{TACGG}\text{-}5'$ specifies $5'\text{-}\mathrm{AUGCC}\text{-}3'$ in <RNA>. The <base pairing> rules now include A–U rather than A–T, while G–C remains. This <RNA> matches the other <DNA> strand's $5'$-to-$3'$ lettering after T is replaced by U. Identifying the template strand matters: replacing T by U in an arbitrarily chosen <DNA> strand is not a general method for deriving the <RNA> product. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26887/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: from DNA to RNA]{ref}.

<RNA> is often a single strand, but single-stranded does not mean straight or entirely unpaired. Complementary regions within one <RNA> <molecule> can undergo <base pairing>, bringing distant positions together. Consider the illustrative twelve-<nucleotide> <nucleotide sequence> $5'\text{-}\mathrm{GCAUUUUUAUGC}\text{-}3'$. Pair positions 1 with 12, 2 with 11, 3 with 10, and 4 with 9. The resulting <stem-loop> has four <base pairs>: G–C, C–G, A–U, U–A. Positions 5–8 remain unpaired in the loop. Both arms belong to the same continuous backbone, and the paired arms have the directions of <antiparallel nucleic acid strands>.

\Image[rna-sequence-and-folding.png]
{title=One <RNA> <nucleotide sequence>, two drawings: numbered letters and an illustrative <stem-loop> with four <base pairs>}

The diagram proposes a possible pairing pattern, not a prediction that this short <RNA> must adopt that shape under every condition. Actual <RNA> folding depends on additional interactions, <temperature>, <ions>, and associated <proteins>; the full three-dimensional arrangement contains more information than the drawn <base pairs>. The example's purpose is to show how a linear <nucleotide sequence> can specify contacts within itself.

Different <RNA> roles make this flexibility useful. <Messenger RNA> carries a message that a <ribosome> reads during <translation (biology)> to build a <protein>. <Transfer RNA> helps match that message to <amino acid> building blocks; see https://www.genome.gov/genetics-glossary/Transfer-RNA-tRNA[NHGRI: transfer RNA]{ref}. <Ribosomal RNA> forms structural and catalytic parts of the <ribosome>. Other <RNA> participates in <gene regulatory networks>, changing how strongly <genes> are expressed. Many <RNA> <molecules> therefore function without being translated into <proteins>; for some <genes>, functional <RNA> is the product itself.

Most strikingly, some folded <RNA> acts as an <enzyme>: these catalytic <RNA> <molecules> are <ribozymes>. The <ribosome>'s bond-forming <active site> is built from <RNA>, although the complete <ribosome> also contains <proteins>. Structural evidence appears in https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10937990/[Nissen et al., The structural basis of ribosome activity in peptide bond synthesis, Science 289, 920–930 (2000)]{ref}. <RNA> therefore connects a readable record with physical recognition, regulation, and catalysis: the same kind of <polymer> can be a message, an adaptor, or part of molecular machinery.

= Messenger RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= mRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Transfer RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= tRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Ribosomal RNA
{parent=Ribonucleic acid}
{wiki}

= rRNA
{c}
{synonym}

= Radius of gyration
{parent=Polymer}
{wiki}
{title2=$R_g^2=\frac1{2N^2}\sum_{m,n}\langle|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2\rangle$}

For $N$ equally weighted positions, the squared <radius of gyration> is the average squared distance from the <center of mass>: $R_g^2=N^{-1}\sum_n\langle|\mathbf R_n-\mathbf R_{\rm cm}|^2\rangle$, where $\mathbf R_{\rm cm}=N^{-1}\sum_n\mathbf R_n$. Expanding pair differences gives $\sum_{m,n}|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2=2N\sum_n|\mathbf R_n-\mathbf R_{\rm cm}|^2$, and therefore
$$
R_g^2=\frac1{2N^2}\sum_{m,n}\langle|\mathbf R_m-\mathbf R_n|^2\rangle.
$$
For a <Gaussian chain> or <freely jointed chain> with these $N$ positions and mean squared link length $b^2$, each pair <variance> is $b^2|m-n|$. Summing separations yields $R_g^2=b^2(N^2-1)/(6N)\sim Nb^2/6$. Counting all endpoints of $N$ links instead gives $N+1$ positions and the corresponding finite-size formula.

= Ion
{parent=Chemistry}
{title2=$q=ze$}

An <ion> is a <chemical atom> or a group of <chemical atoms> with a nonzero net <electric charge>. A positive <ion> is a <cation>; a negative <ion> is an <anion>. The whole entity carries the <electric charge>: a <hydronium> <ion> is a group of four <chemical atoms> with net charge $+e$. This definition agrees with https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03158[IUPAC Gold Book: ion]{ref}.

For an ordinary <ion> containing only <atomic nuclei> and <electrons>, let $N_p$ count all its <protons> and $N_e$ all its <electrons>. Each <proton> contributes the <elementary charge> $+e$, each <electron> contributes $-e$, and <neutrons> contribute zero. Therefore
$$
q=(N_p-N_e)e=ze,\qquad z=N_p-N_e.
$$
The signed integer $z$ is the net <electric charge> number. Removing an <electron> increases $z$ by one; adding an <electron> decreases $z$ by one. Changing the <electron> count leaves the <atomic number>, and therefore the identity of the <chemical element>, unchanged.

For example, a neutral <sodium> <chemical atom> has $11$ <protons> and $11$ <electrons>. Its <cation> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ still has $11$ <protons>, but only $10$ <electrons>, so $q=(11-10)e=+e$. A neutral <chlorine> <chemical atom> has $17$ <protons> and $17$ <electrons>; its <anion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ has $17$ <protons> and $18$ <electrons>, so $q=(17-18)e=-e$. These are particle inventories, not pictures of <atomic orbitals>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds>[§2.6: ions and ionic compounds].

\Image[ion-charge-bookkeeping.png]
{title=<Ion> <electric charge> bookkeeping: neutral <sodium> and <chlorine> <chemical atoms> compared with their <cation> and <anion>}
{description=Every colored dot counts one <proton> or one <electron>. Rows are counting arrays, not spatial models of <atomic nuclei> or <electron shells>. The net <electric charges> are $0,+e,0,-e$.}

One <sodium chloride> <formula unit> accounts for one $\mathrm{Na}^+$ <cation> and one $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ <anion>, whose net <electric charges> sum to zero. Ordinary solid <sodium chloride> is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. This is a useful distinction: a neutral material can contain charged constituents, and its <chemical formula> tells us their ratio without claiming that each pair is a separate <molecule>.

<Ion> bookkeeping connects <chemistry> to <electric current>: moving <ions> can transport <electric charge>, while stationary <ions> cannot supply a transport <current density>. In <electrochemistry>, both signs of <ions> can contribute to the same <conventional current>. An <ion> opposing a fixed <electric charge> is called a <counterion>.

= Cation
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

A <cation> is an <ion> with positive net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Na}^+$ or <hydronium>.

= Anion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}

An <anion> is an <ion> with negative net <electric charge>, for example $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ or <hydroxide>.

= Nitrate
{parent=Anion}
{title2=$\mathrm{NO}_3^-$}
{wiki}

= Nitrate ion
{synonym}

The <nitrate ion>, $\mathrm{NO}_3^-$, is an <anion> with one <nitrogen> connected to three <oxygen> <chemical atoms> and net <electric charge> $-1$ in units of <elementary charge>. Its 24 <valence electrons> admit three equivalent octet <resonance structures>; see <Lewis structure> for their construction and <chemical resonance> for their interpretation. The N–O connections are <covalent bonds> within the <ion>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/7.2 Covalent Bonding>.

= Hydrogen ion
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H^+$}

In <acid-base chemistry>, <hydrogen ion> usually refers to positively charged hydrogen, represented by $\mathrm H^+$. In <water>, this notation describes solvated species, commonly represented by <hydronium>, rather than an isolated bare <proton>. The <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity> defines <pH>.

= Hydronium
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+$}

<Hydronium> is the <ion> formed when a <water> <molecule> accepts a <proton>. It provides the usual aqueous shorthand for the <hydrogen ion> used in <pH> calculations.

= Hydroxide
{parent=Ion}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}^-$}

= Counterion
{parent=Ion}

A mobile <ion> whose charge has the opposite sign to a charged macromolecule, surface or other fixed species. Its local <concentration> can increase close to that fixed charge; <counterion condensation> distinguishes a bound fraction that remains localized in an infinite-dilution limit.

= Electrochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}

<Electrochemistry> studies how <chemical reactions>, transport, and <chemical equilibrium> couple to <electric charge> and <electric potential>. In a cell, <electrons> travel through the external circuit, while mobile <ions> carry <electric current> through the <electrolyte>. At the <electrodes>, <redox> reactions connect these two forms of <electric charge> transport. This connection makes it possible both to obtain electrical <work> from a <chemical reaction> and to use electrical <work> to drive a <chemical reaction>.

An <anode> is an <electrode> where <oxidation> occurs; a <cathode> is an <electrode> where <reduction> occurs. These definitions refer to the <chemical reactions>, rather than universal positive or negative terminal signs. In a <galvanic cell> delivering electrical <work>, the <anode> is negative and the <cathode> positive. In an <electrolytic cell> driven by an external source, the <anode> is positive and the <cathode> negative. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109[Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), §§3.1 and 3.4]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §17.2: galvanic cells]{ref}.

For a worked example, consider an <electrolytic cell> containing molten <sodium chloride>, with the <anode> connected to the positive terminal and the <cathode> to the negative terminal of an external source. At the <cathode>, <sodium> <cations> gain <electrons>; at the <anode>, <chlorine> <anions> lose <electrons>:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{cathode, reduction:}\quad&2\mathrm{Na}^++2e^-\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Na},\\
\text{anode, oxidation:}\quad&2\mathrm{Cl}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cl}_2+2e^-,\\
\text{overall:}\quad&2\mathrm{Na}^++2\mathrm{Cl}^-\longrightarrow2\mathrm{Na}+\mathrm{Cl}_2.
\end{aligned}
$$
The first line has net <electric charge> zero on each side; the second has $-2e$ on each side. Adding the lines cancels the transferred <electrons> and preserves both <chemical atom> counts and net <electric charge>. The <sodium> product is neutral, and the <chlorine> product is a diatomic <molecule>. These equations specify molten <sodium chloride>; aqueous <chemical solutions> introduce competing <water> reactions. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis>[§17.7: electrolysis of molten sodium chloride].

<Electrochemistry> underlies <battery> charging and the manufacture of chemicals by <electrolysis>. Its particularly useful idea is that an <electron> inventory in a balanced <chemical equation> also accounts for transported <electric charge>. Transport alone is a different question from an <electrode> <chemical reaction>: an <ion> may carry <electric current> through the <electrolyte> without itself being consumed at an <electrode>.

Historically, Michael Faraday introduced the terms <electrode>, <electrolyte>, <anion>, <cation>, and <ion> in the terminology section of his 1834 paper, <Experimental Researches in Electricity, Seventh Series by Michael Faraday>[§§662–667]. His §667 explicitly treats the direction assigned to <electric current> as a convention. His <ion> language predates the modern <electron> and <atomic nucleus> picture used in the charge-counting example above.

For an <electrolyte> near <chemical equilibrium>, a <Poisson-Boltzmann equation> can approximate the distribution of <ions> in an <electric potential>. Such an equilibrium model does not by itself describe a cell carrying sustained <electric current>.

= Electrode
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

An <electrode> is an electronic <electrical conductor> in contact with an <electrolyte>. In a cell carrying sustained <redox> <electric current>, <chemical reactions> at this interface exchange <electrons> with <chemical species>. An <electrode> can participate in the <chemical reaction>, as the dissolving <zinc> <anode> of a <Daniell cell> does, or provide a conducting surface without appearing in the net <chemical equation>. This distinction is illustrated by active and inert <electrodes> in https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells[Flowers, Theopold, Langley and Robinson, Chemistry 2e, §17.2]{ref}.

The interface connects two kinds of <electric charge> transport: <electrons> in the external <electrical circuit> and <ions> in the <electrolyte>. The same <electron> does not travel through the wire, swim through the <salt bridge>, and complete a circuit! The <half-reactions> account for how electronic and ionic transport meet. This is the useful idea behind both obtaining electrical <work> in a <galvanic cell> and supplying electrical <work> in an <electrolytic cell>.

Name an <electrode> from its net <chemical reaction>: <oxidation> means <anode>, and <reduction> means <cathode>. State the operating mode before assigning terminal signs. For a <galvanic cell> supplying a passive load, the <anode> is negative and the <cathode> positive; for an ordinary two-terminal <electrolytic cell> driven by a source, the <anode> is positive and the <cathode> negative. Here positive means higher terminal <electric potential> than the other <electrode>, not a claim that the bulk material has a large positive net <electric charge>. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109[Pingarrón et al., Terminology of electrochemical methods of analysis (IUPAC Recommendations 2019), §§3.1 and 3.4]{ref}.

= Anode
{parent=Electrode}
{wiki}

The <anode> is the <electrode> at which the net <electrode> <chemical reaction> is <oxidation>: the reacting <chemical species> loses <electrons>. This definition is independent of terminal sign. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00370[IUPAC Gold Book: anode]{ref}.

During <Daniell cell> discharge, a <zinc> <chemical atom> becomes a $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ <cation>, leaving two <electrons> in the <electrode>; those <electrons> leave through the external <electrical circuit>. The <zinc> <anode> is the negative terminal. In an externally driven <electrolytic cell>, <electrons> instead leave the <anode> through the lead to the source's positive terminal. <Conventional current> in that lead points toward the <anode>, opposite to <electron> motion. The <anode> can therefore be positive while still being the site of <oxidation>. See <Daniell cell> for the balanced <half-reaction> and <electrode> for the operating-mode sign conventions.

= Cathode
{parent=Electrode}
{wiki}

The <cathode> is the <electrode> at which the net <electrode> <chemical reaction> is <reduction>: the reacting <chemical species> gains <electrons>. This definition is independent of terminal sign. During <Daniell cell> discharge, $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ <cations> accept <electrons> arriving at the <copper> <cathode> and become neutral <copper>. The <cathode> is then the positive terminal. In an externally driven <electrolytic cell>, the source's negative terminal supplies <electrons> to the negative <cathode>. See https://doi.org/10.1515/pac-2018-0109[Pingarrón et al., §3.4: cathode]{ref} and <Daniell cell>.

An <ion> carrying <electric current> need not undergo <reduction> at the <cathode>. For example, in the ideal <Daniell cell> charge-balance picture, <potassium> <cations> arriving from a <salt bridge> remain dissolved while $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ <cations> are consumed. Follow the <half-reaction> to identify the reacting species; follow <electric charge conservation> to account for the other moving <ions>.

= Galvanic cell
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

A <galvanic cell> supplies electrical <work> from a spontaneous <redox> <chemical reaction> under the specified conditions. In discharge through a passive load, <oxidation> occurs at its negative <anode> and <reduction> at its positive <cathode>. Separating the reacting compartments makes the <electrons> take an external route, where their transport can deliver useful <work>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/17-2-galvanic-cells[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §17.2: separated reactions and electrical work]{ref}.

\Image[galvanic-electrodes-daniell-paths.png]
{title=<Daniell cell> discharge: <electron> flow outside and <ion> transport inside}
{description=Original schematic of a <zinc>/<copper> <galvanic cell> connected to a passive load. A chosen <potassium> <nitrate> <salt bridge> supplies compensating <anions> to the <anode> compartment and <cations> to the <cathode> compartment. The arrows represent charge-balance roles, not measured trajectories or relative fluxes. The bottom inventory is an ideal accounting example for one <mole> of net cell reaction.}

In the metallic external <electrical circuit>, <electrons> move from <anode> to <cathode>, while <conventional current> goes from <cathode> through the load to <anode>. Inside the cell, net ionic <conventional current> closes the loop from the <anode> compartment toward the <cathode> compartment. Oppositely moving <cations> and <anions> can both contribute to that direction; see <electrolyte> for the signed <current density> calculation. A cell can maintain a nonzero terminal <electric potential difference> near open circuit while delivering almost no <power>; the <Daniell cell voltage and load experiment> makes this distinction concrete.

= Daniell cell
{c}
{parent=Galvanic cell}
{wiki}

A <Daniell cell> uses the <zinc>/$\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$/<copper> pairs. In the separated-compartment teaching version, a <zinc> <electrode> contacts an aqueous <chemical solution> containing $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$, and a <copper> <electrode> contacts one containing $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$. A <salt bridge> supplies an ionic connection; a wire and load supply an electronic connection. During discharge,
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{anode, oxidation:}\quad&\mathrm{Zn}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-,\\
\text{cathode, reduction:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}(s),\\
\text{net:}\quad&\mathrm{Zn}(s)+\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}(aq)+\mathrm{Cu}(s).
\end{aligned}
$$
The <zinc> <electrode> loses material and <copper> is deposited at the <cathode>. Each <half-reaction> conserves <chemical atom> counts and <electric charge>: the first has net charge zero on each side, the second $+2e-2e=0$ on the left and zero on the right. The net reaction has $+2e$ on both sides, with no <electrons> left in its <chemical equation>. The Zn/Cu teaching cell and these <half-reactions> are described in https://www.chem.fsu.edu/chemlab/chm1lab/redox/background.html[Florida State University, Redox Reactions in Voltaic Cells: Construction of a Potential Series, lab background]{ref}.

Creating one <mole> of $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ adds $2F$ of positive <electric charge> to the <anode> compartment's ionic inventory; consuming one <mole> of $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ removes $2F$ of positive <electric charge> from the <cathode> compartment. Here $F$ is the <Faraday constant>. In an ideal bookkeeping example using a <potassium> <nitrate> <salt bridge>, two <moles> of <nitrate> <anions> entering the <anode> compartment compensate the first change, and two <moles> of <potassium> <cations> entering the <cathode> compartment compensate the second. Other <ions> in the cell can share the actual transport. The two compensating inventory changes do not mean that $4F$ flowed through the load: the same series <electric current> transports $2F$ through each cross-section of the circuit per <mole> of net reaction. See <salt bridge>.

Under standard-state conditions near $298\,\mathrm K$, tabulated reduction potentials give a cell <electromotive force> of approximately $1.10\,\mathrm V$. This is a reversible reference value, not a promised loaded reading; actual composition matters through <thermodynamic activity>. The tabulated <copper> and <zinc> values and their subtraction are given in https://chemed.chem.purdue.edu/genchem/topicreview/bp/ch20/electro.php[Purdue University, Electrochemical Reactions: standard-state reduction potentials]{ref}. For the net <chemical equation> as written, the molar <Gibbs free energy> change obeys
$$
\Delta_r G=-2F\mathcal E.
$$
Thus $\mathcal E=1.10\,\mathrm V$ corresponds to about $-212\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ of net reaction. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, $-\Delta_r G$ is the maximum reversible electrical <work> per <mole> for this cell reaction. It is not automatically all the <heat> released by a finite-current cell. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.4 Potential, Free Energy, and Equilibrium>[§17.4: cell potential and free energy].

= Daniell cell voltage and load experiment
{c}
{parent=Daniell cell}

The motivating observation is that a <chemical reaction> can produce a signed electrical reading even when its reacting materials occupy separate vessels. UC San Diego's teaching demonstration places a <copper> strip in $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$ and a <zinc> strip in $0.1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{ZnSO_4}$, using two $400\,\mathrm{mL}$ beakers with about $300\,\mathrm{mL}$ of <chemical solution> in each. The strips connect to a voltage meter. Initially there is no ionic connection; the demonstration specifies a zero reading. A filter-paper <salt bridge> containing aqueous $\mathrm{Na_2SO_4}$ then connects the <chemical solutions>. With the meter's positive lead on <copper> and negative lead on <zinc>, its reported readings are about $+1.1\,\mathrm V$; exchanging the leads reverses the reading. These are observations reported by the university, not data generated for this article. See https://chemistryandbiochemistry.ucsd.edu/undergraduate/teaching-labs/demos/demo9b[UC San Diego, demonstration 9B: apparatus, bridge control and meter polarity]{ref}.

The controls answer different questions. Exchanging only the meter leads checks the sign convention; it does not reverse the <chemical reaction> or swap the <anode> and <cathode>. Interrupting only the <salt bridge> tests the need for an ionic return path. Its absence prevents sustained cell <electric current>, but a very high-input-resistance meter may still show a transient or unstable reading while interfaces store <electric charge>. The zero reading specified for the university's apparatus is therefore not a universal theorem for every meter. Reconnecting the same <salt bridge> distinguishes a reversible interruption from a disconnected wire or exhausted <chemical solution>. This interpretation follows from <electric charge conservation> in a model allowing interfacial <electric charge> storage; see https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03068[IUPAC Gold Book: integral capacitance of an electrode]{ref} for the <capacitance> associated with <electrode> charge.

To distinguish terminal <electric potential difference> from delivered <power>, compare the nearly open-circuit reading with readings across a known <resistor> load. In the nearly open-circuit condition, a meter with input <electrical resistance> much larger than the cell's effective internal <electrical resistance> draws little <electric current>. With a load, measure its terminal <electric potential difference> $V$ and infer $I=V/R$ using <Ohm's law>; track the readings over <time> and repeat the nearly open-circuit reading afterwards. Keep the <temperature>, <chemical solution> compositions, immersed <electrode> areas, bridge geometry and electrical contacts comparable. This is a model-based extension of the documented demonstration, not a claim that UC San Diego published a load sweep. A finite-current drop can reflect ionic <electrical resistance>, changes of interfacial <reaction rates>, or depletion near an <electrode>; a single voltage reading cannot identify which dominates. The interfacial and composition contributions are distinguished in https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04358[IUPAC Gold Book: overpotential]{ref} and https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01230[IUPAC Gold Book: concentration overpotential]{ref}.

For a deliberately simplified calculation, model the cell by a constant <electromotive force> $\mathcal E$ in series with a constant internal <electrical resistance> $r$, and a passive load $R>0$. Take $I$ positive from the <cathode> through the load to the <anode>. Then
$$
I=\frac{\mathcal E}{R+r},\qquad V=IR=\mathcal E-Ir,\qquad P_{\rm load}=IV=I^2R.
$$
The nearly open-circuit limit $R\to\infty$ gives $V\to\mathcal E$ but $I\to0$ and $P_{\rm load}\to0$. A straight $V$-versus-$I$ line is a prediction of constant $r$, not a general law for <electrochemistry>. See https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-1-electromotive-force[Ling, Sanny and Moebs, University Physics II, §10.1: internal resistance, terminal voltage and load]{ref}.

\Image[galvanic-electrodes-load-budget.png]
{title=Toy <Daniell cell> load calculation: terminal <electric potential difference>, <electric charge> and available <work>}
{description=Calculated line and energy bars, not measurements. Chosen inputs are $\mathcal E=1.10\,\mathrm V$, $r=100\,\Omega$, $R=100\,\Omega$ and a $600\,\mathrm s$ interval. The fixed-composition approximation and unit efficiency for the two intended <half-reactions> are assumed. The open-circuit marker is an ideal limit; no fit to experimental data is made.}

For the chosen values, $I=5.50\,\mathrm{mA}$ and $V=0.550\,\mathrm V$. Holding these toy parameters fixed for $600\,\mathrm s$ gives transported <electric charge> $Q=It=3.30\,\mathrm C$ and an <extent of reaction> $\xi=Q/(2F)\simeq17.1\,\mathrm{\mu mol}$, assuming all the <electric current> serves the two specified <half-reactions>. The same $\xi$ counts <zinc> consumed and <copper> deposited, rather than equal <masses>. The energy budget is
$$
\mathcal E Q=3.630\,\mathrm J
=\underbrace{VQ}_{1.815\,\mathrm J\text{ delivered to load}}
+\underbrace{I^2rt}_{1.815\,\mathrm J\text{ internal dissipation}}.
$$
The load's electrical <work> also becomes <Joule heating> when the load is a <resistor>. Here $\mathcal E Q$ accounts for the reaction's available reversible electrical <work>; a full cell thermal budget would also require its thermodynamic <entropy> contribution. Real measurements need uncertainty estimates, a specified meter input <electrical resistance> and calibration, and allowance for changing composition and unwanted <chemical reactions>. The toy value $r=100\,\Omega$ is not a measured property of the university's cell.

= Electrolysis
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

<Electrolysis> uses an external source of electrical <work> to drive <redox> <chemical reactions> at the <electrodes> of an <electrolytic cell>. Its characteristic application is forcing a <chemical reaction> in a direction that is nonspontaneous under the specified conditions: a discharging <galvanic cell> supplies electrical <work>, whereas <electrolysis> consumes electrical <work>. This makes <electrolysis> useful for producing otherwise difficult-to-obtain <chemical substances>, charging a rechargeable <battery>, and depositing a metal coating on a conducting object.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis>[§17.7]

Always identify the <anode> by <oxidation> and the <cathode> by <reduction>. In ordinary driven two-terminal operation, the source withdraws <electrons> from the positive <anode> and delivers <electrons> to the negative <cathode>. In a discharging <galvanic cell>, the <anode> is negative and the <cathode> positive. Thus terminal signs change with operating mode, while the reaction definitions remain valid. Through the <electrolyte>, <ions> transport <electric charge>; they need not all be consumed at an <electrode>. The <electrolyte> and <electrolytic cell> articles explain this transport and its <conventional current> direction.

For a net <chemical equation> transferring $z$ <electrons> per reaction event, the molar reaction <Gibbs free energy> obeys
$$
\Delta_rG=-zF E_{\mathrm{chem}},
$$
where $F$ is the <Faraday constant> and $E_{\mathrm{chem}}$ is the reversible cell <electromotive force> for the reaction in the written direction. A nonspontaneous direction has $\Delta_rG>0$ and $E_{\mathrm{chem}}<0$ at fixed <temperature> and <pressure>. The ideal opposing voltage magnitude is $\Delta_rG/(zF)$; sustaining a finite <electric current> generally needs additional drive because of <electrical resistance> and finite <electrode> <reaction rates>. This thermodynamic voltage concerns the actual composition, rather than necessarily standard conditions.<Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.4 Potential, Free Energy, and Equilibrium>[§17.4] Not every use of <electrolysis> increases bulk chemical <Gibbs free energy>: transferring <copper> between equivalent surfaces can have zero bulk $\Delta_rG$ while the source still supplies dissipated electrical <work>. See the existing <electrolytic cell> example.

A particularly revealing experiment measures <copper> transfer instead of merely looking for a coating. Two separated <copper> <electrodes> stand in aqueous $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$ <electrolyte> in a vessel, with insulated leads to a regulated direct-current power supply, a series ammeter and a switch. A balance reads each <electrode>'s <mass> before and after a timed run. The Institute of Physics describes copper cells with ammeters, adjustable current and removable <cathodes>; its comparison uses three similar cells at $1\,\mathrm A$ for ten minutes, $0.5\,\mathrm A$ for ten minutes, and $0.5\,\mathrm A$ for twenty minutes. The specified observation to test is that deposited <copper> <mass> is proportional to <electric current> times <time>. These are documented experimental settings, not measurements generated here.<Electrolysis of copper sulfate solution by Institute of Physics>{ref}

The ideal <half-reactions> are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{anode, oxidation:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-,\\
\text{cathode, reduction:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}(s).
\end{aligned}
$$
Each deposited <copper> <chemical atom> requires two <electrons>, so $z=2$. The <anode> loses <copper> and the <cathode> gains it; ideally their <mass> changes have equal magnitudes, and the bulk <copper> <cation> inventory stays constant. Local <concentrations> near the <electrodes> can nevertheless differ. The sulfate <anions> participate in ionic transport without appearing in these net <half-reactions>; electronic <electric current> in the wires becomes ionic <electric current> in the <electrolyte> through the two interfaces.<Elektrolyse, Bestimmung von e by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, Faculty of Physics teaching laboratory>{ref}

\Image[electrolysis-copper-process.png]
{title=Driven <copper> transfer: <oxidation> at the positive <anode> and <reduction> at the negative <cathode>}
{description=Original model schematic, not a measured trajectory map. The source withdraws <electrons> on the left and supplies them on the right; bulk ionic <conventional current> closes the circuit. Before/after dry <electrode> <masses> and the integrated <electric current> provide independent readouts. The two <half-reactions> count the same transferred <electric charge>, so their charges must not be added together.}

Clean, rinse, dry and identify the <electrodes> before their initial weighing. After interrupting the <electric current>, rinse away adhering <electrolyte>, dry again and weigh without rubbing off the deposit. KIT's university practical explicitly calls for distilled-water rinsing, careful drying and weighing both <electrodes>; it warns that loose <copper> grains can be lost during handling.<Elektrolyse, Bestimmung von e by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, Faculty of Physics teaching laboratory>{ref} Useful additional controls are repeated drying and weighing until the reading stabilizes, and a matched unpowered immersion followed by the same rinse/dry cycle. These controls test retained liquid, residual dissolved salts, handling loss and chemical changes unrelated to passed <electric charge>; they are proposed checks, not additional observations reported by KIT. Keep immersed <electrode> area, spacing, <electrolyte> composition and <temperature> comparable across runs, and keep the <electrodes> from touching.

Record the <electric current> as a function of <time>, together with the exact switch-on and switch-off times. Taking $I(t)\geq0$ in the chosen deposition direction, the transported <electric charge> is
$$
Q=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}I(t)\,dt.
$$
For constant <electric current>, $Q=I(t_1-t_0)$; with sampled readings, use <numerical integration> rather than assuming that one ammeter reading remained constant. The two equal-charge Institute of Physics settings each give $600\,\mathrm C$, while its shorter $0.5\,\mathrm A$ setting gives $300\,\mathrm C$. With $M_{\mathrm{Cu}}=63.546\,\mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$, the <mass> per <amount of substance> tabulated by KIT, and $F\approx96485.33212\,\mathrm{C\,mol^{-1}}$, ideal <Faraday's laws of electrolysis> predict
$$
m_{\mathrm{Cu}}=\frac{M_{\mathrm{Cu}}Q}{2F}
\approx\begin{cases}
0.09879\,\mathrm g,&Q=300\,\mathrm C,\\
0.19758\,\mathrm g,&Q=600\,\mathrm C.
\end{cases}
$$
These are calculated predictions for complete conversion of the measured <electric charge> into retained <copper>, not experimental results. For a stable process, doubling the <electric charge> doubles the deposited <mass>; equal <electric charge> gives equal deposited <mass> even when the <electric current> and duration differ.

\Image[electrolysis-charge-mass.png]
{title=<Faraday's laws of electrolysis> connect transported <electric charge> to deposited <copper> <mass>}
{description=Original calculated model, with no experimental points. Left: assumed constant-current histories with equal areas $Q=600\,\mathrm C$. Right: ideal <copper> <mass> versus <electric charge> and a stipulated 80 percent current-efficiency comparison. The dashed line assumes that 20 percent of the <electric charge> drives other same-direction <electrode> reactions and that all deposited <copper> remains attached.}

The balance reading is a test of the whole model. Competing <reduction> of <water> or other <chemical species> can divert <electric current> from <copper> deposition; transient <electrode> <capacitance> can also consume part of a short pulse without producing <copper>. A fragile deposit can detach even when its formation used the expected <electric charge>. Conversely, retained <electrolyte> or salts can increase an apparent <mass> gain. Comparing <anode> loss with <cathode> gain helps detect such problems but does not identify every cause. A slope below $M_{\mathrm{Cu}}/(2F)$ alone cannot distinguish competing <chemical reactions> from lost deposit. Current efficiency measures the fraction of transported <electric charge> assigned to a specified <electrode> reaction, rather than the fraction of electrical <energy> stored chemically.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/current efficiency>{ref}

The beautiful connection is that an electrical measurement counts chemical events: integrating <electric current> and reading a balance provide two ways of following the same <electron> <stoichiometry>. This makes <electrolysis> useful for controlling coating quantity and for testing a charge-to-<amount of substance> relation. It does not determine coating uniformity, adhesion or the required voltage, which depend on transport and <electrode> <chemical kinetics> as well.

= Faraday's laws of electrolysis
{c}
{parent=Electrolysis}
{wiki}
{title2=$m=MQ/(zF)$}

<Faraday's laws of electrolysis> state that the <mass> of material formed or consumed by a specified <electrode> reaction is proportional to the transported <electric charge>, and that equal <electric charge> produces <masses> proportional to $M/z$ for different reactions. Here $M$ is the product's <mass> per <mole>, and $z$ is the positive number of <electrons> transferred per product entity. For complete use of the <electric charge> in that reaction,
$$
n_{e^-}=\frac QF,\qquad n_{\mathrm{product}}=\frac{Q}{zF},\qquad m=\frac{MQ}{zF}.
$$
The first relation uses the <Faraday constant>; the second uses <half-reaction> <stoichiometry>; the third converts <amount of substance> to <mass>. For equal transported <electric charge> in two ideal cells,
$$
\frac{m_1}{m_2}=\frac{M_1/z_1}{M_2/z_2}.
$$
The number $z$ belongs to the balanced <half-reaction>, rather than to the metal's name alone: <copper> deposited from $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ needs $z=2$. These modern forms of both laws are given in KIT's practical.<Elektrolyse, Bestimmung von e by Karlsruhe Institute of Technology, Faculty of Physics teaching laboratory>{ref}

If other same-direction <electrode> reactions share the measured <electric current>, define an integrated current efficiency $\eta=Q_{\mathrm{product}}/Q$. Provided the product is neither subsequently consumed nor lost, the retained <mass> is
$$
m=\eta\frac{MQ}{zF},\qquad
\eta=\frac{\int I_{\mathrm{product}}(t)\,dt}{\int I(t)\,dt}.
$$
Here $0\leq\eta\leq1$ follows from the specified same-direction partition of <electric current>. It is not a universal bound on every signed partial-current convention: IUPAC notes that simultaneous <oxidation> and <reduction> at one <electrode> can produce a current-efficiency ratio above one.<IUPAC Compendium of Chemical Terminology, fifth edition (Gold Book) by International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry/current efficiency>{ref} When efficiency varies during a run, use the charge-weighted ratio above, rather than an unweighted average over <time>. If deposition removes material from a multicomponent <chemical solution>, specify which <chemical species> is being counted and balance its <half-reaction> before choosing $z$.

Michael Faraday's accessible 1834 paper, Experimental Researches in Electricity, Seventh Series, §783 states proportionality between chemical action and the quantity of electricity passed. In §§789–791 he describes connecting a molten tin-chloride cell in series with a water-decomposition charge indicator, weighing the receiving <electrode> before and after, and comparing the deposited metal with the indicator's gas readout. The common series <electric current> makes these two readouts comparable. This was a quantitative test of chemical equivalents, rather than merely an observation that a deposit appeared. It is historical evidence, not a suggested classroom procedure; the modern <electron>-counting derivation above uses concepts unavailable to Faraday.<Experimental Researches in Electricity, Seventh Series by Michael Faraday>[§783; §§789–791; pp. 101–103]

= Electrolytic cell
{parent=Electrolysis}
{wiki}

An <electrolytic cell> uses an external source of electrical <work> to drive <electrode> <chemical reactions>. In the ordinary two-terminal driven mode, its positive <anode> supports <oxidation> and its negative <cathode> supports <reduction>. The source removes <electrons> from the <anode> and supplies <electrons> to the <cathode>; through the <electrolyte>, net <conventional current> goes from <anode> toward <cathode>. Terminal signs refer to the driven cell's terminals relative to each other. The source supplies electrical <power> to the cell. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis>[§17.7: electrolysis and electroplating].

For example, transferring <copper> from a soluble <copper> <anode> onto a conducting object at the <cathode> has the ideal <half-reactions>
$$
\begin{aligned}
\text{positive anode:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}(s)\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-,\\
\text{negative cathode:}\quad&\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}(aq)+2e^-\longrightarrow\mathrm{Cu}(s).
\end{aligned}
$$
Their sum transfers <copper> between surfaces. For identical bulk states, this transfer need not have a positive bulk <Gibbs free energy> change; finite <electric current> still needs applied drive to overcome losses. Thus "consumes electrical <work>" does not mean every <electrolytic cell> stores all that <work> as chemical <Gibbs free energy>. <Electrolysis> can instead redistribute material. The charge-to-amount relation remains $\xi=Q/(2F)$ when only these <half-reactions> carry the <electric current>; competing <water> reactions would change the deposited amount. The electroplating principle and charge accounting are described in <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/17.7 Electrolysis>[§17.7: electroplating and quantitative aspects].

The University of Washington's teaching demonstration makes this material transfer visible with a <copper> <electrode> and a stainless-steel <electrode> in a glass cell containing $0.5\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ $\mathrm{CuSO_4}$. The external source connects its positive lead to <copper> and negative lead to steel; the reported observation is a <copper> coating on the steel. Reversing the source polarity removes the deposited <copper> again: the coated surface now supports <oxidation> and acts as the <anode>. This polarity reversal changes the drive, unlike swapping the measurement leads in the <Daniell cell voltage and load experiment>. Coating appearance alone does not measure charge efficiency or exclude competing <chemical reactions>. See https://chem.washington.edu/lecture-demos/electroplating-copper-stainless-steel[University of Washington, Electroplating Copper on Stainless Steel: apparatus and reversible coating demonstration]{ref}.

The <copper> <cathode> in this driven example is negative, while the <copper> <cathode> of a discharging <Daniell cell> is positive: both support exactly the same <reduction> <half-reaction>. Likewise, an <anode> need not lose material if its conducting surface is absent from its net <half-reaction>. Neither terminal sign nor a universal mass-change rule defines an <anode> or <cathode>.

= Half-reaction
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

A <half-reaction> is a balanced <chemical equation> for either the <oxidation> or the <reduction> part of a <redox> <chemical reaction>, explicitly including exchanged <electrons>. Match the electron counts before adding the two <half-reactions>; the net equation then contains no transferred <electrons>. A <half-reaction> is bookkeeping, not necessarily an <elementary reaction>. See https://www.chem.fsu.edu/chemlab/chm1lab/redox/background.html[Florida State University, lab background: separate electrode reactions]{ref} and <Daniell cell>.

= Salt bridge
{parent=Electrochemistry}
{wiki}

A <salt bridge> is an ionic connection between <electrolyte> compartments that limits bulk mixing while permitting <electric charge> transport. Its mobile <ions> compensate changes caused by the <electrode> <half-reactions>, keeping the bulk <chemical solutions> approximately electrically neutral during sustained <electric current>. It does not conduct <electrons> like the external wire, and its <ions> need not be consumed at either <electrode>. The ionic return path and compensating ion flows are explained in https://chemed.chem.purdue.edu/genchem/topicreview/bp/ch20/electro.php[Purdue University, Electrochemical Reactions: salt bridge and charge balance]{ref}.

In a discharging <Daniell cell>, <anions> moving toward the <anode> compartment and <cations> moving toward the <cathode> compartment both contribute to the internal <conventional current> from <anode> toward <cathode>. This charge-balance role is compatible with a negative <anode>: terminal signs do not imply that the whole <anode> <chemical solution> is negatively charged. The simple compensating-inventory example in <Daniell cell> does not fix all individual <ion> fluxes; <diffusion> and the cell's other mobile <ions> can contribute. A real bridge also introduces <electrical resistance> and may introduce a junction <electric potential difference>, so its presence does not guarantee an exact thermodynamic cell reading. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/09080[IUPAC Gold Book: salt bridge and liquid-junction potential]{ref}.

= Electrolyte
{parent=Electrochemistry}

An <electrolyte> is a conducting medium in which mobile <ions> carry <electric current>, according to https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/09061[IUPAC Gold Book: electrolyte]{ref}. In introductory <chemistry>, a substance that yields <ions> when dissolved in <water> is also called an <electrolyte>. Both usages point to the same mechanism: an <electric field> gives mobile <cations> and <anions> directed <drift velocities>.

Mobility matters as much as the presence of <electric charge>. In a simple ideal model of solid <sodium chloride>, <ions> are confined to their sites and do not carry a sustained transport <electric current>. Molten <sodium chloride> has mobile <ions>; dissolved <sodium chloride> gives mobile $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ <ions> in <water>. Real solids can have some ionic transport through imperfections; the immobile-solid picture is an introductory limit, rather than a claim that all solids are perfect insulators.

A <strong electrolyte> yields essentially all <ions> from the dissolved substance, whereas a <weak electrolyte> yields only a fraction under the specified conditions. Dissolved <sodium chloride> is a <strong electrolyte>. <Sucrose> is a <nonelectrolyte>: it dissolves as neutral <molecules>, so dissolving something does not automatically create mobile <ions>. <Water>'s own small population of <hydronium> and <hydroxide> remains possible. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/11.2 Electrolytes>[§11.2: strong, weak, and nonelectrolytes] and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/11-4-colligative-properties[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §11.4, Table 11.3: sucrose and sodium chloride]{ref}.

<Acetic acid> is a <weak electrolyte> and a <weak acid>. In <water>, some neutral <acetic acid> <molecules> transfer a <proton> to <water>, producing <hydronium> and the negatively charged partner:
$$
\mathrm{CH_3COOH}+\mathrm{H_2O}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{CH_3COO^-}+\mathrm{H_3O^+}.
$$
Both sides have net <electric charge> zero. At <chemical equilibrium>, neutral <acetic acid> coexists with these <ions>; the fraction converted depends on the conditions. <Electrolyte> strength describes this extent of ion production, while <concentration> describes how much substance is present per specified quantity of <chemical solution>. A dilute <strong electrolyte> can contain fewer <ions> per volume than a concentrated <weak electrolyte>. Neither the words strong and weak nor a conductivity comparison alone specifies <concentration>.

Now specify an <electrolytic cell> with a positive <anode> on the left, a negative <cathode> on the right, and an approximately uniform <electric field> pointing right in the bulk <electrolyte>. With a steady external source and neglecting <diffusion> and <convection>, positive <cations> drift right toward the negative <cathode>; negative <anions> drift left toward the positive <anode>. Yet both populations contribute a rightward <conventional current>! For <number densities> $n_+,n_-$ and <drift velocities> $\mathbf v_+,\mathbf v_-$, the transport <current density> is
$$
\mathbf J=n_+(+e)\mathbf v_++n_-(-e)\mathbf v_-.
$$
The product of <electric charge> and <velocity> determines <conventional current>: for the <anion>, both the charge and its rightward velocity component are negative, making its rightward contribution positive. This is the same sign rule used for <electron> <conventional current>. It follows directly from the existing <charge carrier> formula.

\Image[electrolyte-ion-drift-current.png]
{title=Opposite <ion> <drift velocities> contribute to the same <conventional current> in a specified <electrolytic cell>}
{description=The positive <anode> is left and the negative <cathode> right. The upper arrows show <ion> motion; the lower arrows show the signs of $nqv_x$ for <current density>. A uniform bulk <electric field> is assumed; <diffusion>, <convection>, and <electrode> boundary layers are omitted. Arrow lengths are schematic.}

For an independent numerical example, stipulate $n_+=n_-=10^{25}\,\mathrm{m^{-3}}$, $v_{+,x}=2\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$, and $v_{-,x}=-3\times10^{-4}\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}$. With the exact <elementary charge> $e=1.602176634\times10^{-19}\,\mathrm C$, the contributions are
$$
J_{+,x}=320.4353268\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}},\qquad
J_{-,x}=480.6529902\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}},\qquad
J_x=801.088317\,\mathrm{A\,m^{-2}}.
$$
Equal <number densities> of singly charged <cations> and <anions> give zero bulk <charge density>, while their motion gives nonzero <current density>. This is why a neutral <electrolyte> can conduct. These numbers are chosen toy inputs, not measurements of <sodium chloride>; maintaining sustained cell <electric current> also requires suitable <electrode> reactions. Blocking <electrodes> can instead lead to charge accumulation and decay of the <electric current>.

An <electrolyte> connects the microscopic motion of <ions> to observable <electrical conductivity>. A counterion-only model keeps just the mobile <counterions> needed to oppose a fixed <electric charge>; <Poisson-Boltzmann equations> use ideal <Boltzmann distributions> to model their equilibrium distributions, rather than the drift example above.

= Strong electrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}
{wiki}

A <strong electrolyte> yields essentially all <ions> from its dissolved substance in the specified <solvent> and conditions. Strength does not specify <concentration>.

= Weak electrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}
{wiki}

A <weak electrolyte> yields only a fraction of <ions> from its dissolved substance under the specified conditions, as illustrated by <acetic acid> in <water>.

= Nonelectrolyte
{parent=Electrolyte}

A <nonelectrolyte> supplies neutral <molecules> rather than <ions> on dissolving in the specified <solvent>, as illustrated by <sucrose> in <water>.

= Chemical reaction
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <chemical reaction> interconverts <chemical species>, as defined by the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01033[IUPAC Gold Book, chemical reaction]{ref}. The consumed species are <reactants>; the formed species are <reaction products>. In ordinary <chemical reactions>, <chemical atoms> are rearranged while each <chemical element>'s total atom count is conserved. For a complete <chemical equation>, net <electric charge> is conserved too.

For example, the balanced <chemical equation>
$$
2\mathrm{H}_2+\mathrm{O}_2\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}
$$
describes conversion of <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> into <water>. Both sides contain four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. Three <reactant> <molecules> become two <reaction product> <molecules>: atom counts are conserved, but molecule counts need not be. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-1-writing-and-balancing-chemical-equations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, balancing chemical equations]{ref}.

The coefficients describe net consumption and formation, without asserting that three <molecules> collide in one step. A balanced <chemical equation> supplies quantitative constraints; it does not determine how quickly the <chemical reaction> occurs, or whether <chemical equilibrium> prevents complete conversion. Those questions need further information. Keeping these roles distinct makes <stoichiometry> useful even when the microscopic details are complicated.

= Redox
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Oxidation-reduction reaction
{synonym}

A <redox> <chemical reaction> couples <oxidation> and <reduction>. In the <electrode> examples here, <oxidation> releases <electrons> and <reduction> accepts them. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-2-classifying-chemical-reactions[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §4.2: oxidation-reduction reactions]{ref}.

= Oxidation
{parent=Redox}

In the <electrochemistry> examples here, <oxidation> is loss of <electrons> by a <chemical species>; it occurs at the <anode>.

= Reduction
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Redox}

= Reduction
{synonym}

In the <electrochemistry> examples here, <reduction> is gain of <electrons> by a <chemical species>; it occurs at the <cathode>.

= Hydrolysis
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Hydrolysis> is a <chemical reaction> involving <water> that breaks a <chemical bond>. The <free energy> released by suitably coupled <ATP> <hydrolysis> can drive <active transport>; the available <free energy> depends on the actual composition and conditions.

= Energy coupling
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{title2=$\Delta G_{\mathrm{net}}=\Delta G_1+\Delta G_2$}

<Energy coupling> links an <endergonic reaction> to an <exergonic reaction> through a <reaction mechanism>, so that the combined transformation has a favourable change in <Gibbs free energy>. <ATP> <hydrolysis> is a common driving <chemical reaction>. A shared <reaction intermediate>, produced in one step and consumed in another, can physically connect the transformations; merely placing two independent <chemical reactions> in the same <chemical solution> does not establish <energy coupling>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: coupled reactions and activated intermediates]{ref}.

\Image[atp-energy-coupling.png]
{title=<Energy coupling>: $+20-30.5=-10.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ under the same stipulated conditions}
{description=Original diagram of cumulative changes in <Gibbs free energy> as two contributions are added. The levels are bookkeeping steps, not a <reaction mechanism>, actual <reaction intermediates>, elapsed time, or <activation energy> barriers. A <reaction mechanism> must enforce the joint transformation for the net favourable value to drive the desired conversion.}

For a computed example, stipulate an <endergonic reaction> $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ with $\Delta G_1^{\circ\prime}=+20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ under precisely the same biochemical <standard state> conditions as the quoted <ATP> <hydrolysis> value. Assume a <reaction mechanism> couples one <ATP> <hydrolysis> to each A-to-B conversion. The formal sum is
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A&\longrightarrow\mathrm B,
&\Delta G_1^{\circ\prime}&=+20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\\
\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}&\longrightarrow\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i,
&\Delta G_2^{\circ\prime}&=-30.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}},\\
\mathrm A+\mathrm{ATP}+\mathrm{H_2O}&\longrightarrow\mathrm B+\mathrm{ADP}+\mathrm P_i,
&\Delta G_{\mathrm{net}}^{\circ\prime}&=-10.5\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The sum $+20+(-30.5)=-10.5$ gives an <exergonic reaction>. The $+20$ is a chosen teaching value, not a measured property of an identified substance. Outside these reference conditions, add the actual $\Delta G_1$ and $\Delta G_2$, each evaluated using consistent <thermodynamic activities>; mixing a standard value for one <chemical reaction> with an unrelated cellular value for the other gives no justified prediction.

Why do the changes in <Gibbs free energy> add? <Gibbs free energy> is a <state function>. Adding consistent reaction changes cancels any shared <reaction intermediate> and leaves the difference between the combined <reactants> and combined <reaction products>. This explains the thermodynamic accounting; it does not construct the <reaction mechanism>. In many <ATP>-dependent routes, <phosphorylation> transfers a <phosphate> group to a <substrate> or an <enzyme>, and subsequent transformation consumes that modified material. The shared material is what connects the steps. Other <reaction mechanisms> use <ATP> differently. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26838/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: ATP-driven biosynthesis]{ref}.

The useful achievement is that a common regenerated <nucleotide> can connect many different processes in <metabolism>. The net negative change in <Gibbs free energy> permits the coupled conversion, but supplies no <reaction rate>: <enzymes> can lower <activation energy> barriers without changing the endpoint difference. <Energy coupling> changes which overall <chemical reaction> is being considered; <catalysis> accelerates an available route. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: free-energy changes and reaction barriers]{ref}.

A historical landmark is Fritz Lipmann's https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470122464.ch4[1941 essay, Metabolic Generation and Utilization of Phosphate Bond Energy]{ref}. Its sections on the <phosphate> cycle, generation and utilization of phosphate-bond <energy>, and group transfer organized these processes around a reusable chemical carrier. The historical terminology should be read with the modern distinction between net <hydrolysis> and the cost of breaking a <chemical bond>.

= Exergonic reaction
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta G<0$}

An <exergonic reaction> has negative molar reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> under the specified conditions. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, its forward direction is thermodynamically favourable; its <reaction rate> remains a separate question. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: exergonic reactions]{ref}.

= Endergonic reaction
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\Delta G>0$}

An <endergonic reaction> has positive molar reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> under the specified conditions. Sustained net forward conversion needs a driving process, such as <energy coupling>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-2-potential-kinetic-free-and-activation-energy[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.2: endergonic reactions]{ref}.

= Phosphorylation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Phosphorylation> attaches a <phosphate> group to a <molecule>. Transfer from <ATP> to a <substrate> or <enzyme> can participate in <energy coupling>; the name alone does not specify the complete <reaction mechanism>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/6-4-atp-adenosine-triphosphate[OpenStax Biology 2e, 6.4: phosphorylation]{ref}.

= Chemical kinetics
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

<Chemical kinetics> studies how quickly <chemical reactions> proceed and how their <reaction rates> depend on composition and conditions. It connects a measured change in <concentration> to a <rate law>, then uses a <rate equation> to predict future <concentrations>. This makes it useful for choosing how long to wait for conversion and for testing a proposed <reaction mechanism>. Changing <temperature>, <reactant> <concentrations>, or the presence of a <catalyst> can change <reaction rates>; see https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-2-factors-affecting-reaction-rates[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.2: factors affecting reaction rates]{ref}.

<Chemical equilibrium> specifies the eventual balance under given constraints; <chemical kinetics> determines how rapidly it is approached. At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, a decrease in <Gibbs free energy> favours conversion but does not guarantee a fast <chemical reaction>: reaching a <transition state> can require crossing a substantial barrier. Thus a mixture can change imperceptibly over an observation period without being at <chemical equilibrium>. The distinction separates which direction is favoured from how long conversion takes. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-5-collision-theory[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.5: collision theory and reaction barriers]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/16-1-spontaneity[Chemistry 2e, §16.1: spontaneity and speed]{ref}.

= Activation energy
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}
{title2=$E_a$}

= Arrhenius activation energy
{c}
{synonym}

<Activation energy> quantifies how strongly a <rate constant> depends on <thermodynamic temperature>. For a positive <rate constant> $k(T)$, the empirical definition is
$$
E_a(T)=RT^2\frac{d}{dT}\ln\frac{k(T)}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}},
$$
where $R$ is the <molar gas constant> and $k_{\mathrm{ref}}>0$ is a fixed reference with the same units as $k$. The ratio is a <dimensionless quantity>, keeping the <natural logarithm> meaningful without changing its <derivative>. Thus $E_a$ is a molar <energy>, commonly in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ or $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The <Arrhenius equation> makes $E_a$ constant over its assumed range. This rate-based definition, and the need for care when identifying it with a threshold on a <potential energy surface>, are stated in the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00102[IUPAC Gold Book: activation energy]{ref}.

In a simple barrier picture, reaching a <transition state> costs <energy> relative to the starting <reactants>. The height of that barrier is different from the difference between the <reactants> and <reaction products>. An energetically favourable endpoint can still be reached slowly. An <energy profile (chemistry)> makes the distinction visible, while an <Arrhenius plot> tests temperature sensitivity from <rate constants>. A measured $E_a$ need not equal a barrier read from an arbitrarily chosen diagram.

For a computed example, assume a <first-order reaction> has $k(300\,\mathrm K)=0.100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, constant $E_a=80\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and a temperature-independent <pre-exponential factor>. The <Arrhenius equation> gives
$$
\frac{k(310\,\mathrm K)}{k(300\,\mathrm K)}
=\exp\left[\frac{80000}{8.314462618}\left(\frac1{300}-\frac1{310}\right)\right]
\approx2.81398.
$$
Hence $k(310\,\mathrm K)\approx0.281398\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Its <half-life> falls from $\ln2/0.100\approx6.93147\,\mathrm s$ to $\ln2/0.281398\approx2.46322\,\mathrm s$. These are chosen model parameters, not measurements of an identified <chemical reaction>. The exponent uses <kelvin>, not unconverted Celsius temperatures, and $80000\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ must match $R$ in $\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$.

= Arrhenius equation
{c}
{parent=Activation energy}
{wiki}
{title2=$k(T)=A\exp[-E_a/(RT)]$}

The <Arrhenius equation> models the <temperature> dependence of a <rate constant> by
$$
k(T)=A\exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right),\qquad A>0,\quad T>0.
$$
Here $A$ is the <pre-exponential factor>, $E_a$ the <activation energy>, $R$ the <molar gas constant>, and $T$ the absolute <thermodynamic temperature>. In its original form, $A$ and $E_a$ are held constant over the range being modeled. It is an empirical relationship, not a universal rule for every <reaction mechanism>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00446[IUPAC Gold Book: Arrhenius equation]{ref}.

Eliminating $A$ between two positive <temperatures> gives the useful prediction
$$
\ln\frac{k(T_2)}{k(T_1)}
=\frac{E_a}{R}\left(\frac1{T_1}-\frac1{T_2}\right).
$$
For $E_a>0$, warming increases $k$. The logarithmic sensitivity is $d\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})/dT=E_a/(RT^2)$, so there is no universal doubling per ten degrees. The same $10\,\mathrm K$ rise from $300$ to $310\,\mathrm K$ gives a factor $1.67749$ for $E_a=40\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, versus $2.81398$ for $80\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.

\Image[arrhenius-temperature-sensitivity.png]
{title=Temperature sensitivity and straight-line <Arrhenius plots> for two illustrative <activation energies>}
{description=Both models are anchored at $k(300\,\mathrm K)=0.100\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, so they have different <pre-exponential factors>. Left: the higher <activation energy> produces a larger relative change with <temperature>. Right: the <natural logarithm> of the dimensionless ratio $k(T)/k(300\,\mathrm K)$ is linear in inverse <temperature>. These exact model curves are not experimental data.}

The useful simplification is that a strong change in <reaction rate> becomes a straight-line relation after changing variables. Measurements at several <temperatures> can estimate the slope and test whether one pair of constants describes the chosen range. Curvature can indicate a failure of that approximation; fitting a line does not uniquely establish a microscopic <reaction mechanism>.

Svante Arrhenius's 1889 paper on acid-catalysed inversion of cane sugar compared the strong observed temperature dependence with the much smaller increase in collision frequency. He discussed van't Hoff's earlier temperature relations and proposed an activated-sugar hypothesis to interpret the dependence. That historical hypothesis should be distinguished from the modern <transition state> description. See the https://webserver.lemoyne.edu/giunta/arrlaw.html[translated excerpt of Arrhenius, On the Reaction Velocity of the Inversion of Cane Sugar by Acids, Zeitschrift für Physikalische Chemie 4 (1889), pp. 226–248]{ref}.

= Pre-exponential factor
{parent=Arrhenius equation}
{wiki}
{title2=$A$}

= Arrhenius prefactor
{c}
{synonym}

= Frequency factor
{synonym}

The <pre-exponential factor> multiplies the <exponential function> in the <Arrhenius equation>. It has the same units as the <rate constant>, so for a <first-order reaction> both have units $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$. Its physical interpretation depends on the <reaction mechanism>; an assumed constant $A$ should not be identified with a universal collision frequency. Comparing <activation energies> alone does not rank absolute <rate constants> when the <pre-exponential factors> differ. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04811[IUPAC Gold Book: pre-exponential factor]{ref}.

= Arrhenius plot
{c}
{parent=Arrhenius equation}
{wiki}
{title2=$\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})\ \text{against}\ 1/T$}

An <Arrhenius plot> places $\ln(k/k_{\mathrm{ref}})$ against $1/T$, using a fixed reference <rate constant> with the same units as $k$. Under the constant-parameter <Arrhenius equation>,
$$
\ln\frac{k(T)}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}}
=\ln\frac{A}{k_{\mathrm{ref}}}-\frac{E_a}{R}\frac1T.
$$
The slope is $-E_a/R$ and the intercept is $\ln(A/k_{\mathrm{ref}})$. Changing the reference units shifts the intercept but leaves the slope unchanged. If the horizontal coordinate is displayed in $10^{-3}\,\mathrm{K^{-1}}$, its numerical slope must be rescaled before multiplying by $-R$. A temperature-dependent <pre-exponential factor> or <activation energy> can make the plot curved.

= Reaction mechanism
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

A <reaction mechanism> proposes the sequence of <elementary reactions> that produces an overall <chemical reaction>. Its predicted <rate law> must agree with observations; matching one <rate law> need not uniquely identify a <reaction mechanism>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-6-reaction-mechanisms[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.6: reaction mechanisms]{ref}.

= Reaction intermediate
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}

A <reaction intermediate> is formed in one step of a <reaction mechanism> and consumed in a later step, cancelling from the net <chemical equation>. A <reaction intermediate> can connect the transformations in <energy coupling>; it is distinct from a <transition state>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/I03096[IUPAC Gold Book: intermediate]{ref}.

= Reaction coordinate
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}
{title2=$q$}

A <reaction coordinate> parametrizes changes in molecular configuration along a <chemical reaction> path. It can involve a <bond length>, a <bond angle>, or a combination of geometric changes. It is not elapsed time and is not the bulk <extent of reaction>: one describes configurations, while the other accounts for how much material has converted. The precise choice matters when interpreting an <energy profile (chemistry)>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05168[IUPAC Gold Book: reaction coordinate]{ref}.

= Potential energy surface
{parent=Reaction coordinate}
{wiki}
{title2=$V(\mathbf R_1,\ldots,\mathbf R_N)$}

A <potential energy surface> assigns <potential energy> to the configurations of the reacting <chemical atoms>, for a specified electronic description. A <reaction coordinate> selects a path through this many-dimensional configuration space; a <potential-energy profile> then records the <potential energy> along that path. Different paths can share endpoints but cross different barriers. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04780[IUPAC Gold Book: potential-energy reaction surface]{ref}.

= Energy profile
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Reaction coordinate}
{wiki}

= Reaction energy profile
{synonym}

An <energy profile (chemistry)> plots an explicitly specified <energy> against a <reaction coordinate>. Identify whether the vertical axis is <potential energy>, <enthalpy>, or <Gibbs free energy>; these descriptions are related but not interchangeable. A <potential-energy profile> describes a path through configurations, while a free-energy description also accounts for the relevant thermodynamic conditions.

\Image[reaction-barriers-and-endpoints.png]
{title=Two schematic <potential-energy profiles> share endpoints but have different barriers}
{description=The reactant level is $0$, the product level is $-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, and the two maxima are $80$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. These chosen curves illustrate endpoint differences and barrier heights; they are not measured surfaces, trajectories in time, or a complete catalytic cycle.}

For the two chosen profiles, the <reaction energy barriers> from the <reactants> are $80$ and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. From the <reaction products>, the same maxima are $100$ and $70\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ higher. Both paths have endpoint difference $-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The barrier is measured from the relevant starting endpoint, not from an arbitrary zero of <potential energy>.

The appeal of the diagram is that it separates two useful questions: which endpoint is lower, and which path is easier to traverse? <Catalysis> changes the route through <transition states> while retaining the endpoint thermodynamics of the same overall <chemical reaction>. A real catalytic route may have several steps and <reaction intermediates>; the two simple curves do not specify such a <reaction mechanism>. The plotted potential-energy difference alone is not an <equilibrium constant> or a calculated <rate constant>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis[Chemistry 2e, 12.7: alternative reaction routes and catalysis]{ref}.

= Potential-energy profile
{parent=Energy profile (chemistry)}

A <potential-energy profile> plots the <potential energy> of the reacting system along a specified path from <reactants> through a <transition state> to <reaction products>. It is a particular kind of <energy profile (chemistry)>. For an <elementary reaction>, a suitable <reaction coordinate> parametrizes the path; a multi-step <reaction mechanism> needs the coordinates of its successive steps. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04779[IUPAC Gold Book: potential-energy profile]{ref}.

= Reaction energy barrier
{parent=Energy profile (chemistry)}
{title2=$\Delta E^\ddagger=E_{\mathrm{peak}}-E_{\mathrm{start}}$}

A <reaction energy barrier> is the height of a specified peak relative to the starting state in an <energy profile (chemistry)>. State which <energy> is used and which direction is being considered. A schematic barrier is not automatically the empirical <activation energy> obtained from the temperature dependence of a <rate constant>.

= Elementary reaction
{parent=Reaction mechanism}
{wiki}

An <elementary reaction> is a single step in a <reaction mechanism>. The <law of mass action> models its directional <reaction rate> using <reactant> <concentrations> raised to the step's stoichiometric multiplicities. Applying that rule requires evidence that the step is elementary; a net <chemical equation> alone supplies no such evidence.

= Catalysis
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

<Catalysis> accelerates a <chemical reaction> by providing an alternative <reaction mechanism> with a more accessible barrier through its <transition states>. A <catalyst> participates in the route and is regenerated. At fixed <temperature>, <catalysis> changes how quickly <chemical equilibrium> is reached without changing the <equilibrium constant>; it does not make an unfavourable overall change in <Gibbs free energy> favourable. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.7: catalysis]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-3-shifting-equilibria-le-chateliers-principle[Chemistry 2e, §13.3: effect of a catalyst on equilibrium]{ref}.

= Catalyst
{parent=Catalysis}

A <catalyst> makes a <chemical reaction> proceed faster by participating in an alternative <reaction mechanism> and being regenerated. This makes repeated conversion possible: the same <catalyst> can help many successive <reactants> become <reaction products>. An <enzyme> is a biological <catalyst>. The overall standard change in <Gibbs free energy> is unchanged; see the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00876[IUPAC Gold Book: catalyst]{ref}.

Regeneration does not mean that the <catalyst> sits unchanged throughout every step. For example, a schematic <reaction mechanism> can temporarily bind A to a <catalyst> C in a <reaction intermediate> AC:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm A+\mathrm C&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm{AC},\\
\mathrm{AC}&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B+\mathrm C,\\
\text{net:}\qquad\mathrm A&\rightleftharpoons\mathrm B.
\end{aligned}
$$
Adding the steps cancels both the <reaction intermediate> AC and the <catalyst> C from the net <chemical equation>. AC is produced and later consumed; C is used and later recovered. These letters describe possible roles, not a measured <reaction mechanism>. The alternative route is useful only if its <chemical kinetics> permits faster conversion. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.7: catalysts and alternative mechanisms]{ref}.

For a worked barrier example, consider an idealized reversible A-to-B conversion at fixed <temperature> $T=300\,\mathrm K$. Stipulate standard molar <Gibbs free energy> levels $G_{\mathrm A}^\circ=0$ and $G_{\mathrm B}^\circ=-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$, with effective <transition state> levels $80$ without a <catalyst> and $50\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ with a <catalyst>. Use the same <standard states> and the same zero for both routes; the catalytic route includes the regenerated C at both endpoints. Unlike the separate <potential-energy profile> example, this illustration uses <Gibbs free energy> at one specified <temperature>.

\Image[catalyst-alternative-reaction-path.png]
{title=A <catalyst> lowers the stipulated <Gibbs energy of activation> in both directions while retaining the same endpoints}
{description=Original smooth schematic <Gibbs free energy> profiles at $300\,\mathrm K$, with levels in $\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The horizontal <reaction coordinate> describes progress along each schematic route, not time. These are chosen effective barriers, not measured <potential energy surfaces>, actual molecular paths, or the two-step <reaction mechanism> above. A real catalytic route can have several <transition states> and <reaction intermediates>.}

The <Gibbs energy of activation> is measured from the starting endpoint in the chosen direction. Thus the four barriers are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\Delta G_{f,\mathrm{uncat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=80-0=80,&
\Delta G_{f,\mathrm{cat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=50-0=50,\\
\Delta G_{r,\mathrm{uncat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=80-(-20)=100,&
\Delta G_{r,\mathrm{cat}}^{\ddagger\circ}&=50-(-20)=70,
\end{aligned}
\qquad\text{all in }\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}.
$$
Both directional barriers fall by $30\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$. The endpoint difference stays $\Delta_rG^\circ=-20\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$.

To turn those barriers into <rate constants>, use the Eyring form of <transition state theory> for this effective <first-order reaction> pair, with a common prefactor and unit probability of successful passage:
$$
k=\nu\exp\left(-\frac{\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}}{RT}\right),
\qquad \nu=\frac{k_{\mathrm B}T}{h}.
$$
Here $k_{\mathrm B}$ is the <Boltzmann constant>, $h$ is the <Planck constant>, and $R$ is the <molar gas constant>. The common prefactor cancels from the comparisons. These <Gibbs energies of activation> are not the empirical <activation energy> $E_a$ of the <Arrhenius equation>. With the stipulated value $R=8.314462618\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}\,K^{-1}}$, the speedup is
$$
\frac{k_{f,\mathrm{cat}}}{k_{f,\mathrm{uncat}}}
=\frac{k_{r,\mathrm{cat}}}{k_{r,\mathrm{uncat}}}
=\exp\left(\frac{30000}{8.314462618\times300}\right)
\approx1.67248\times10^5.
$$
The exponent is a <dimensionless quantity>: convert $30\,\mathrm{kJ\,mol^{-1}}$ to $30000\,\mathrm{J\,mol^{-1}}$ before dividing by $RT$. The <exponential function> explains why a modest barrier reduction can have a dramatic kinetic effect. This number follows from the chosen free-energy barriers and common prefactor; it is not a universal catalytic multiplier or a prediction across <temperatures>.

\Image[catalyst-forward-reverse-rate-ratios.png]
{title=Both directional <rate constants> increase by about $1.67\times10^5$, while their forward-to-reverse ratio stays about $3036$}
{description=Original bar plots at $300\,\mathrm K$. Left: base-ten <logarithms> of the <dimensionless> normalized <rate constants> $k/\nu$ for the four stipulated barriers. A less negative value means faster conversion. Right: the <logarithm> of $k_f/k_r$ is identical for the two routes. Absolute units factor out through the common $\nu=k_{\mathrm B}T/h$; no temperature dependence outside this one model point is asserted.}

Assume ideal <thermodynamic activities> with a common concentration <standard state>, and directional <rate laws> $J_f=k_f[\mathrm A]$ and $J_r=k_r[\mathrm B]$. At <chemical equilibrium>, equal <reaction rates> give $K=[\mathrm B]/[\mathrm A]=k_f/k_r$ for this model. The barriers give
$$
K_{\mathrm{uncat}}=\frac{e^{-80000/(RT)}}{e^{-100000/(RT)}}
=e^{20000/(RT)}
=\frac{e^{-50000/(RT)}}{e^{-70000/(RT)}}=K_{\mathrm{cat}}
\approx3035.58.
$$
The <catalyst> speeds up both directions, so it changes how quickly <chemical equilibrium> is approached without changing this <equilibrium constant>. For a general <chemical reaction>, the thermodynamic relation is $K=\exp[-\Delta_rG^\circ/(RT)]$ with consistent <standard states>; an arbitrary multistep <reaction mechanism> need not permit $K=k_f/k_r$ for two measured <rate constants>. At the same <temperature>, <pressure>, and <thermodynamic activities>, a <catalyst> also leaves the actual overall reaction change in <Gibbs free energy> unchanged. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/13-3-shifting-equilibria-le-chateliers-principle[Chemistry 2e, §13.3: a catalyst does not shift equilibrium]{ref}. To drive an unfavourable overall conversion, a process such as <energy coupling> must change the combined transformation.

= Transition state theory
{parent=Catalyst}
{wiki}
{title2=$k=(k_{\mathrm B}T/h)\exp[-\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}/(RT)]$}

<Transition state theory> relates an <elementary reaction>'s <rate constant> to a <Gibbs energy of activation>. The Eyring expression used in the <catalyst> example assumes every passage counted at the <transition state> succeeds. More generally, a factor accounting for unsuccessful passage multiplies it. Comparing barriers alone gives the quoted speedup only when the remaining prefactors agree. Free-energy profiles can depend on <temperature>, so the example's values at $300\,\mathrm K$ do not determine a temperature curve. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06470[IUPAC Gold Book: transition state theory]{ref}.

= Gibbs energy of activation
{c}
{parent=Transition state theory}
{title2=$\Delta G^{\ddagger\circ}$}

= Gibbs energies of activation
{c}
{synonym}

= Activation free energy
{synonym}

The <Gibbs energy of activation> is the standard molar <Gibbs free energy> change associated with going from the starting <reactants> to the <transition state> in <transition state theory>. It specifies the directional barrier, rather than the overall endpoint change in <Gibbs free energy>. Use consistent <standard states> when comparing <Gibbs energies of activation> and <rate constants>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/G02631[IUPAC Gold Book: Gibbs energy of activation]{ref}.

= Transition state
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{wiki}

A <transition state> describes configurations at the barrier between <reactants> and <reaction products> in an <elementary reaction>. It is a crossing region, rather than a stable substance that accumulates in a bottle. The IUPAC definition characterizes these configurations by equal probabilities of proceeding toward <reactants> or <reaction products>: https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06468[IUPAC Gold Book: transition state]{ref}. Lowering the barrier relative to the starting configuration can increase a <reaction rate>, even when the overall change in <Gibbs free energy> is unchanged.

= Reaction rate
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{title2=$v$}
{wiki}

For one <chemical reaction> in a <closed system> of fixed <volume> $V$, with uniform <concentrations> and no competing reactions or appreciable accumulation of unlisted species, choose the <chemical equation>
$$
a\mathrm A+b\mathrm B\longrightarrow c\mathrm C,\qquad a,b,c>0.
$$
Brackets denote <amount of substance> per <volume>, for example $[\mathrm A]=n_{\mathrm A}/V$. The <stoichiometry> gives one instantaneous <reaction rate>
$$
v=-\frac1a\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}
=-\frac1b\frac{d[\mathrm B]}{dt}
=\frac1c\frac{d[\mathrm C]}{dt}
=\frac1V\frac{d\xi}{dt},
$$
where $\xi$ is the existing <extent of reaction>. The minus signs make forward consumption positive; dividing by the coefficients makes measurements of different <chemical species> agree. Units can be $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05156[IUPAC Gold Book: rate of reaction]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-1-chemical-reaction-rates[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.1: chemical reaction rates]{ref}.

For the illustrative <chemical equation> $2\mathrm A+\mathrm B\longrightarrow3\mathrm C$, a measured $d[\mathrm C]/dt=0.060\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$ implies $v=0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, $d[\mathrm A]/dt=-0.040\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$, and $d[\mathrm B]/dt=-0.020\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. These numbers express <stoichiometry>, without supplying a <rate law>.

Each <derivative> is the slope of a <concentration> curve at one instant. Over a finite interval, $-\Delta[\mathrm A]/(a\Delta t)$ instead gives an average <reaction rate>. For A converting reversibly to B, the net <reaction rate> is $v=d[\mathrm B]/dt=J_f-J_r$; both directional <reaction rates> can remain positive when $v=0$ at <chemical equilibrium>.

= Rate law
{parent=Reaction rate}

A <rate law> specifies how a <reaction rate> depends on <concentrations> under stated conditions. A common form is
$$
v=k[\mathrm A]^\alpha[\mathrm B]^\beta,
$$
where $k$ is a <rate constant> and the exponents are partial <orders of reaction>. Determine them experimentally, or derive them from a justified <reaction mechanism>. Do not infer $\alpha=a$ and $\beta=b$ from the net <chemical equation>: its coefficients describe consumption ratios, whereas the exponents describe sensitivity to <concentration>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/R05141[IUPAC Gold Book: rate law]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-3-rate-laws[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.3: rate laws]{ref}.

A <rate law> becomes a prediction of time evolution when combined with <stoichiometry> in a <rate equation>, for example $d[\mathrm A]/dt=-av$. Agreement with a measured <rate law> is useful evidence for a <reaction mechanism>, but several routes can give the same dependence.

= Order of reaction
{parent=Rate law}
{title2=$n$}

= Orders of reaction
{synonym}

= Reaction order
{synonym}

In a power-form <rate law> $v=k[\mathrm A]^\alpha[\mathrm B]^\beta$, the partial <orders of reaction> are $\alpha$ and $\beta$, and the overall <order of reaction> is $n=\alpha+\beta$. For example, doubling $[\mathrm A]$ at fixed $[\mathrm B]$ multiplies $v$ by $2^\alpha$: factors of one, two, and four correspond to partial <orders of reaction> zero, one, and two. These exponents can also be fractional or negative. A single constant <order of reaction> need not describe a <rate law> that is not a power form. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04322[IUPAC Gold Book: order of reaction]{ref}.

For a simple irreversible conversion $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ with <rate equation> $-d[\mathrm A]/dt=k[\mathrm A]^n$, initial <concentration> $A_0>0$, and fixed $k>0$, compare <zero-order reactions>, <first-order reactions>, and <second-order reactions> using the <dimensionless quantities>
$$
X=\frac{[\mathrm A]}{A_0},\qquad \tau=kA_0^{n-1}t,\qquad \frac{dX}{d\tau}=-X^n.
$$
Their integrated curves, starting at $X(0)=1$, are
$$
\begin{aligned}
n=0:&\quad X=1-\tau\quad(0\leq\tau<1),\\
n=1:&\quad X=e^{-\tau},\\
n=2:&\quad X=\frac1{1+\tau}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For the <zero-order reaction>, the plotted extension $X=\max(1-\tau,0)$ stops conversion at depletion. It does not continue to obey $dX/d\tau=-1$ once $X=0$. A negative <concentration> would be unphysical. The <first-order reaction> and <second-order reaction> approach zero without reaching it at finite time in these models.

\Image[reaction-order-concentration-curves.png]
{title=<Dimensionless> <concentration> decays for three <orders of reaction>, with <zero-order reaction> depletion and the corresponding linearizing plots}

The common $\tau$ axis compares curve shapes after each model's own time rescaling, rather than equal laboratory times for an identical numerical $k$. In the lower panels, $X$, $\ln X$, and $1/X$ respectively give straight lines with slopes $-1$, $-1$, and $+1$ for the matching <order of reaction>. The <natural logarithm> uses a <dimensionless quantity>. These transformed plots help test a proposed <rate law>; the <zero-order reaction> line applies only before depletion. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-4-integrated-rate-laws[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §12.4: integrated rate laws]{ref}.

= First-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <first-order reaction> has a <reaction rate> proportional to one <reactant> <concentration> in the simple single-species case. For an irreversible $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$ in a <closed system> at fixed <volume>, take $[\mathrm A](0)=A_0$, $[\mathrm B](0)=0$, and constant <rate constant> $k>0$. Its <rate equation> and solution are
$$
\frac{d[\mathrm A]}{dt}=-k[\mathrm A],\qquad
[\mathrm A](t)=A_0e^{-kt},\qquad
[\mathrm B](t)=A_0(1-e^{-kt}),\qquad
v(t)=kA_0e^{-kt}.
$$
A short check is enough: differentiating $A_0e^{-kt}$ gives $-kA_0e^{-kt}$, and the two <concentrations> sum to $A_0$, as required by the one-to-one <stoichiometry>. Thus the same <exponential decay> describes both remaining <reactant> and decreasing <reaction rate>. Fixed $k$ means a fixed fractional loss per unit time, not a fixed absolute consumption rate.

Choose illustrative values $A_0=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and $k=0.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$, with $t$ measured in seconds. Then $[\mathrm A]=e^{-0.2t}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm B]=(1-e^{-0.2t})\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and $v=0.2e^{-0.2t}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. The <half-life> is
$$
t_{1/2}=\frac{\ln2}{k}=\frac{\ln2}{0.2}\,\mathrm s\approx3.4657\,\mathrm s.
$$
At each successive <half-life>, half the remaining <reactant> is consumed; $t_{1/2}$ is independent of $A_0$ for this <first-order reaction>. At $t=10\,\mathrm s$, the calculated values are $[\mathrm A]\approx0.135335\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, $[\mathrm B]\approx0.864665\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, and $v\approx0.027067\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. These are model predictions for chosen parameters, rather than experimental measurements.

\Image[chemical-first-order-reaction.png]
{title=Illustrative <first-order reaction>: <reactant> loss, <reaction product> formation, and declining <reaction rate> for $k=0.2\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}$}

The left panel tracks the two <concentrations>; the right tracks their instantaneous conversion <reaction rate>. Their different vertical units matter: $v=k[\mathrm A]$, so the numerical curves need not have the same height. This gives a practical way to translate a measured <concentration> into a forecast of both conversion and waiting time.

= Zero-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <zero-order reaction> has a <reaction rate> independent of the relevant <reactant> <concentration> over the range where its <rate law> applies. For $\mathrm A\longrightarrow\mathrm B$, $[\mathrm A]=A_0-kt$ is valid only before depletion at $t=A_0/k$. The constant-consumption model must stop or change there.

= Second-order reaction
{parent=Order of reaction}

A <second-order reaction> has overall <order of reaction> two. The simple <rate equation> $-d[\mathrm A]/dt=k[\mathrm A]^2$ gives $1/[\mathrm A]=1/A_0+kt$. An overall <second-order reaction> can instead involve two different <reactants>, for example $v=k[\mathrm A][\mathrm B]$; its <concentration> curves need not have the single-species form above.

= Rate constant
{parent=Chemical kinetics}
{title2=$k$}

A <rate constant> is the coefficient in a specified <rate law>, held fixed under specified conditions. It can describe an empirical overall <chemical reaction>, as well as an <elementary reaction>. Fixed <temperature> matters: changing <temperature> can change $k$, even for the same <chemical reaction>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/O04322[IUPAC Gold Book: order of reaction and rate constants]{ref} and https://old.goldbook.iupac.org/html/R/R05137.html[IUPAC Gold Book: rate coefficient]{ref}.

For $v=k[\mathrm A]^n$, dimensional consistency requires
$$
\operatorname{units}(k)=(\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}})^{1-n}\,\mathrm{s^{-1}}.
$$
A <zero-order reaction> therefore uses $\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$; a <first-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{s^{-1}}$; a <second-order reaction> uses $\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$. Comparing bare numerical values of $k$ across different <orders of reaction> is meaningless without their units and <concentrations>. Even for the same <order of reaction>, state whether $k$ multiplies the normalized <reaction rate> $v$ or a particular <reactant> disappearance rate: a stoichiometric coefficient can change that convention.

= Protonation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Chemical equation
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

A <chemical equation> represents a <chemical reaction> using <chemical formulas>, with <reactants> on the left and <reaction products> on the right. A balanced <chemical equation> has the same number of <chemical atoms> of each <chemical element> on both sides. For <molecular hydrogen> and <molecular oxygen> forming <water>,
$$
2\mathrm{H}_2+\mathrm{O}_2\longrightarrow2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}.
$$
The coefficients $2,1,2$ give the relative numbers of <molecules>; the coefficient $1$ is normally omitted. Count <hydrogen> as $2\times2=4$ on the left and $2\times2=4$ on the right, and <oxygen> as $1\times2=2$ on the left and $2\times1=2$ on the right. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-1-writing-and-balancing-chemical-equations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §4.1: writing and balancing chemical equations]{ref}.

\Image[chemical-equation-atom-balance.png]
{title=Two <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> and one <molecular oxygen> <molecule> account for two <water> <molecules>}
{description=Exactly four <hydrogen> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> occur on each side. The circles and connecting lines are an invented schematic: they indicate grouping and <chemical bonds>, without representing actual <bond lengths>, <bond angles> or a <reaction mechanism>.}

To balance the initial expression $\mathrm H_2+\mathrm O_2\longrightarrow\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, first put $2$ before <water> to match the two <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. This creates four <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> on the right, so put $2$ before <molecular hydrogen>. Changing a coefficient multiplies the entire <chemical formula>. Changing a subscript changes composition and usually the <chemical substance>: $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ is two <water> <molecules>, whereas $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$ is one <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Never balance a <chemical equation> by changing its subscripts.

The bookkeeping follows from conservation of the <atomic nuclei> in ordinary, non-nuclear <chemical reactions>; <chemical bonds> and the distribution of <electrons> can change. For a <chemical equation> involving <ions>, total <electric charge> must also match. For example, the existing <self-ionization of water> equation has zero total <electric charge> on each side, since the $+1$ of <hydronium> cancels the $-1$ of <hydroxide>. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds>[§2.6: nuclei and ions].

The same $2:1:2$ ratio relates changes in <amount of substance>: two <moles> of <molecular hydrogen> consumed per <mole> of <molecular oxygen> correspond to two <moles> of <water> formed. A <mole> is a defined unit, not one <molecule>; the little whole-number picture is an abstract counting model, not a claim that a macroscopic sample contains just five <molecules>. See <molecular count and amount of substance> for the exact conversion and https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole[BIPM, SI base unit: mole]{ref} for the definition.

This is why a balanced <chemical equation> is useful: <stoichiometry> turns the same atom accounting into relations between measurable quantities. It does not determine <reaction rate>, the final conversion set by <chemical equilibrium>, or the actual yield of recovered <reaction products>. The <limiting reagent> gives an upper bound under the specified <chemical reaction>.

= Reactant
{parent=Chemical reaction}

A <chemical species> consumed in a <chemical reaction>.

= Reagent
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Product
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Reaction product
{synonym}

= Substrate
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}

= Reaction substrate
{synonym}

A chemical species on which a reagent or catalyst acts in a <chemical reaction>. An <enzyme> acts on a biochemical <substrate>, a particular instance of this broader reaction role.

= Thermal dissociation
{parent=Chemical reaction}

Thermal dissociation is temperature-driven breaking of a molecule into simpler fragments. The <chemical equilibrium> abundance depends on pressure as well as temperature: reactions increasing particle number are generally favored at low pressure. In an <exoplanet atmosphere>, reduced <water> on a very hot dayside does not by itself establish a low planetary oxygen abundance.

= Stoichiometry
{parent=Chemical reaction}
{wiki}
{title2=$\nu_i$}

<Stoichiometry> relates the quantities consumed and formed in a <chemical reaction>. Its coefficients are ratios of entity counts, or equivalently of <amount of substance>, rather than ratios of <masses>. For the <water> <chemical equation>, consuming two <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> per <molecular oxygen> <molecule> gives the same $2:1$ ratio as consuming two <moles> of <molecular hydrogen> per <mole> of <molecular oxygen>. Multiplying every entity count by the same factor preserves the ratio. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-3-reaction-stoichiometry[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, reaction stoichiometry]{ref}.

Coefficients and subscripts do different jobs. The coefficient in $2\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$ counts two <water> <molecules>; the subscript says that each contains two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>. Changing coefficients scales quantities. Changing a subscript changes the specified <chemical substance>.

\Image[stoichiometry-particle-counts.png]
{title=The same stoichiometric ratio at molecular and molar scales}
{description=The upper schematic counts individual <molecules>; the lower line multiplies each count by the <Avogadro constant> times one <mole>. Circle sizes and positions are illustrative.}

This distinction catches a common counting error: one <mole> of <water> <molecules> contains two <moles> of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and one <mole> of <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. Always specify what is counted.

<Stoichiometry> records reactant and product multiplicities in a <chemical reaction> or a <chemical reaction network>. The change caused by one reaction is its <stoichiometric vector>; multiplying by the reaction rate gives its contribution to the concentration equations. For two identical reactants the rate-constant convention absorbs any combinatorial factor.

= Molecular count and amount of substance
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{title2=$N=nN_{\mathrm A}$}

The molecular count $N$ is a dimensionless integer; the <amount of substance> $n$ has unit <mole>, symbol $\mathrm{mol}$. They satisfy
$$
N=nN_{\mathrm A},\qquad N_{\mathrm A}=6.02214076\times10^{23}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}.
$$
The <Avogadro constant> is exact by the modern definition of the <mole>: see https://www.bipm.org/en/si-base-units/mole[BIPM, SI base unit: mole]{ref}. Thus two <water> <molecules> correspond to $2/N_{\mathrm A}$ <moles>, whereas two <moles> of <water> contain $1.204428152\times10^{24}$ <molecules>. A <mole> is a unit for counting specified entities, which may also be <chemical atoms>, <ions> or <electrons>.

= Extent of reaction
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{title2=$\xi$}
{wiki}

For a closed system with one specified <chemical reaction>, the <extent of reaction> $\xi$ records progress from $\xi=0$ through $n_i=n_{i,0}+\nu_i\xi$. Here $n_i$ is the <amount of substance> of species $i$, and the signed <stoichiometric vector> has negative entries for <reactants> and positive entries for <reaction products>. For the <water> <chemical equation> in the order $\mathrm{H}_2,\mathrm{O}_2,\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$, it is $(-2,-1,2)$. The normalization of $\xi$ depends on the chosen coefficients. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/E02283[IUPAC Gold Book, extent of reaction]{ref}.

= Limiting reagent
{parent=Stoichiometry}
{wiki}

= Limiting reactant
{synonym}

The <limiting reagent> is the <reactant> exhausted first if the specified <chemical reaction> proceeds to the maximum conversion permitted by <stoichiometry>. With initial values of <amount of substance> $n_{i,0}$ for the <reactants> and positive consumption coefficients $a_i$, the largest forward <extent of reaction> is $\xi_{\max}=\min_i(n_{i,0}/a_i)$. Compare these quotients, rather than simply choosing the smallest initial quantity. This calculation predicts the maximum <reaction product> quantity and identifies which <reactant> supply must increase to raise it. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-4-reaction-yields[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, limiting reactants]{ref}.

Start with $3\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen>, $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular oxygen> and no <water>. Assume complete conversion by the displayed <chemical reaction>, without competing reactions. Then
$$
\xi_{\max}=\min\left(\frac{3\,\mathrm{mol}}2,\frac{1\,\mathrm{mol}}1\right)=1\,\mathrm{mol}.
$$
The <molecular oxygen> is the <limiting reagent>. Consumption of $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen> and $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular oxygen> forms $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <water>, leaving $1\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <molecular hydrogen>. Initially there are $1.806642228\times10^{24}$ <molecular hydrogen> <molecules> and $6.02214076\times10^{23}$ <molecular oxygen> <molecules>; after conversion there are $1.204428152\times10^{24}$ <water> <molecules> and $6.02214076\times10^{23}$ leftover <molecular hydrogen> <molecules>.

\Image[limiting-reagent-amounts.png]
{title=Oxygen runs out while one mole of molecular hydrogen remains}
{description=The horizontal axis is <extent of reaction>, rather than time. The endpoint assumes complete conversion by one <chemical reaction>; the curves express <stoichiometry>.}

As a check, the initial inventory has $6\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms> and $2\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. The final <water> contains $4\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and leftover <molecular hydrogen> supplies the remaining $2\,\mathrm{mol}$. The atom totals balance even though the total <amount of substance> of <molecules> falls from four to three <moles>.

= Acid-base chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}

<Acid-base chemistry> studies how <chemical acids> and <chemical bases> exchange <protons> or <electron pairs>. Here the focus is transfer of <protons> in <water>: a <chemical acid> donates a <proton>, and a <chemical base> accepts it. The resulting <chemical equilibrium> determines <pH>, while a <buffer solution> can resist changes in <pH>. This connects microscopic <chemical reactions> with a compact, measurable description of a <chemical solution>.

The important distinction is between how much <chemical acid> is present and how readily it transfers a <proton>. A <weak acid> and a <strong acid> at the same total <concentration> need not have the same <pH>. Conversely, two <buffer solutions> can have the same <pH> while containing very different total quantities of their <chemical acids> and <chemical bases>.

= Acid
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki=Acid}

= Chemical acid
{synonym}

A <chemical acid> can donate a <proton>, or accept an <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond>. The <pH> and <buffer solution> examples below use the former meaning. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00071[IUPAC Gold Book, acid]{ref}.

= Strong acid
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}

In <water>, a <strong acid> transfers its available <proton> essentially completely to <water> under the dilute conditions considered here. For the <pH> example, each initial <chemical acid> unit supplies one <hydronium> <ion>. “Strong” describes this conversion, rather than a high <concentration>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-3-relative-strengths-of-acids-and-bases[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, relative strengths of acids and bases]{ref}.

= Weak acid
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}

A <weak acid> transfers only part of its available <protons> to <water>, leaving both forms at <chemical equilibrium>. Its <acid dissociation constant> and the composition determine the <hydronium> <concentration>; substituting total <chemical acid> <concentration> into the <pH> formula is generally incorrect.

= Acid dissociation constant
{parent=Acid (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$K_a$}

For $\mathrm{HA}$ transferring a <proton> to <water>, the <acid dissociation constant> is the <equilibrium constant> written, when the <thermodynamic activity> of <water> is approximated by one, as $K_a=a_{\mathrm H^+}a_{\mathrm A^-}/a_{\mathrm{HA}}$. Define $\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a$. All <thermodynamic activities> and $K_a$ here are <dimensionless quantities>, with consistent <standard states>.

= pKa
{c}
{parent=Acid dissociation constant}
{title2=$\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a$}

The <pKa> is the negative base-ten <logarithm> of the <dimensionless> <acid dissociation constant>. A lower <pKa> means a larger <acid dissociation constant> and a stronger tendency to donate a <proton> under the same <standard state>, <solvent>, and <temperature> conventions. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> places equal <weak acid> and <conjugate base> <thermodynamic activities> at <pH> equal to <pKa>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation]{ref}.

= Base
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}

= Chemical base
{synonym}

A <chemical base> can accept a <proton>, using an available <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond>; more generally it can donate an <electron pair> to form a <covalent bond> with another species. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00601[IUPAC Gold Book, base]{ref}.

= Conjugate base
{parent=Base (chemistry)}

A <conjugate base> is what remains after a <chemical acid> donates one <proton>. In the schematic pair $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm A^-$, $\mathrm{HA}$ is the <chemical acid> and $\mathrm A^-$ its <conjugate base>; accepting a <proton> reverses the conversion.

= pH
{c}
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{pH}=-\log_{10}a_{\mathrm H^+}$}

The <pH> of a <chemical solution> is the negative base-ten <logarithm> of its <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}=-\log_{10}a_{\mathrm H^+},\qquad a_{\mathrm H^+}=10^{-\mathrm{pH}}.
$$
The <thermodynamic activity> is a <dimensionless quantity>, so the <logarithm> has a <dimensionless> argument. The definition uses <thermodynamic activity>, rather than total <chemical acid> <concentration>. Single-<ion> <thermodynamic activities> require a convention; practical <pH> measurements are tied to calibrated standards. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04524[IUPAC Gold Book, pH]{ref}.

In a dilute ideal <chemical solution> in <water>, a useful approximation is
$$
a_{\mathrm H^+}\approx\frac{[\mathrm H^+]}{c^\circ},\qquad c^\circ=1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}},\qquad
\mathrm{pH}\approx-\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm H^+]}{1\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}}\right).
$$
Square brackets denote <concentration> in <moles> per litre. In <water>, $[\mathrm H^+]$ is shorthand for the <hydronium> <concentration>, often written $[\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^+]$; it does not describe isolated bare <protons>. Departures from ideal behavior need <activity coefficients>. The <concentration> formula approximates the <thermodynamic activity> definition.

For an ideal <strong acid> supplying one <proton> per initial unit, stipulate a total <concentration> of $0.001\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and negligible <self-ionization of water>. Then $[\mathrm H^+]\approx10^{-3}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$, so
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx-\log_{10}(10^{-3})=3.
$$
Reducing that <concentration> tenfold by <dilution> gives $10^{-4}\,\mathrm{mol\,L^{-1}}$ and <pH> $4$, under the same assumptions. More generally, dividing the <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity> by ten raises <pH> by one. This is why the scale compresses an enormous range: equal steps of <pH> represent multiplicative changes, rather than equal differences in <concentration>. These are model calculations, rather than measured samples.

\Image[ph-logarithmic-scale.png]
{title=One step of <pH> represents a tenfold change in <hydrogen ion> <thermodynamic activity>}
{description=The <logarithm> makes equal spacing on the <pH> axis correspond to factors of ten on the <thermodynamic activity> axis. The marked <strong acid> example uses the dilute ideal <concentration> approximation and negligible <self-ionization of water>. The plotted interval is illustrative; it does not bound possible <pH> values.}

Neutrality depends on <temperature>. In the dilute ideal <water> model, define the <dimensionless> ion product $K_w=([\mathrm H^+]/c^\circ)([\mathrm{OH}^-]/c^\circ)$ and $\mathrm pK_w=-\log_{10}K_w$. Equal <hydronium> and <hydroxide> <concentrations> imply
$$
\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{neutral}}=\frac{\mathrm pK_w}{2}.
$$
At $25\,{}^\circ\mathrm C$, the textbook approximation $K_w\approx10^{-14}$ gives neutral <pH> $7$. Since $K_w$ varies with <temperature>, neutrality need not mean <pH> $7$. Nor is <pH> restricted to $0$–$14$: the activity definition permits negative <pH> when $a_{\mathrm H^+}>1$, and <pH> above $14$ when $a_{\mathrm H^+}<10^{-14}$. High <concentrations> can invalidate the dilute ideal approximation. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-2-ph-and-poh[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, pH and pOH]{ref}.

<S. P. L. Sørensen> introduced <pH> at the Carlsberg Laboratory in 1909, as recorded in the https://carlsbergfondet.dk/en/about-the-foundation/our-history/timeline-of-the-foundation-s-history/[Carlsberg Foundation historical timeline]{ref}.

= Buffer solution
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki}

A <buffer solution> contains appreciable quantities of a <weak acid> and its <conjugate base>, and resists changes in <pH> caused by small quantities of added <chemical acid> or <chemical base>. The <conjugate base> accepts added <protons>; the <weak acid> supplies <protons> when a <chemical base> consumes them. Consuming most of either partner undermines the buffering action. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffers]{ref}.

For a separate toy calculation, stipulate <pKa> $5$ for a <weak acid> $\mathrm{HA}$; this is an input, not measured material data. Initially $1\,\mathrm L$ of <buffer solution> contains $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of $\mathrm{HA}$ and $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of its <conjugate base> $\mathrm A^-$. In the ideal <concentration> approximation, the <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> gives initial <pH> $5$. Add $0.010\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide> with negligible volume change. The dominant <neutralization> has one-to-one <stoichiometry>:
$$
\mathrm{HA}+\mathrm{OH}^-\longrightarrow\mathrm A^-+\mathrm H_2\mathrm O.
$$
Each <mole> of consumed <hydroxide> removes one <mole> of <weak acid> and forms one <mole> of <conjugate base> and <water>. Thus the inventories after <neutralization> are
$$
n_{\mathrm{HA}}\approx(0.10-0.01)\,\mathrm{mol}=0.09\,\mathrm{mol},\qquad
n_{\mathrm A^-}\approx(0.10+0.01)\,\mathrm{mol}=0.11\,\mathrm{mol}.
$$
The shared volume cancels from the <concentration> ratio. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> therefore predicts
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx5+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{0.11}{0.09}\right)=5+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{11}{9}\right)=5.0871501757\ldots.
$$
This small increase illustrates how conversion between abundant partners absorbs added <chemical base>. The calculation neglects the tiny free <hydronium> and <hydroxide> inventories, changes during re-establishment of <chemical equilibrium>, <self-ionization of water>, and <activity coefficient> corrections. The displayed inventories approximate the final <chemical species> distribution; they are not an exact solution of all <chemical equilibrium> conditions.

\Image[buffer-neutralization-inventory.png]
{title=Added <hydroxide> transfers a finite inventory from <weak acid> to <conjugate base>}
{description=Toy <pKa> $5$, initial volume $1\,\mathrm L$, and negligible added volume. Bars show the dominant <neutralization> bookkeeping before and after adding $0.010\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide>; the <pH> values use the ideal <concentration> approximation. The acid-plus-base inventory stays $0.20\,\mathrm{mol}$. Neither the bars nor the <pH> values are measured material data or exact speciation.}

A <buffer solution> has a finite <buffer capacity>: its <weak acid> inventory supplies protection against added <chemical base>, and its <conjugate base> inventory supplies protection against added <chemical acid>. Here an addition approaching $0.10\,\mathrm{mol}$ of <hydroxide> uses up the available $\mathrm{HA}$ in the dominant <stoichiometry> calculation. Close to that limit, the neglected <chemical equilibrium> contributions matter; once $\mathrm{HA}$ is exhausted, inserting a zero denominator into the <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> cannot predict the actual <pH>. Excess <hydroxide> and the remaining <chemical species> require a fresh <chemical equilibrium> calculation. This is why a <buffer solution> stabilizes conditions for <chemical reactions> over a finite range of additions. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffer capacity and added base]{ref}.

For the pair $\mathrm{HA}/\mathrm A^-$, rearranging the <acid dissociation constant> gives the activity relation
$$
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
With <thermodynamic activity> of <water> approximated by one and ideal dilute <thermodynamic activities> for both partners, this yields the familiar <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm A^-]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}\right).
$$
The <concentrations> are at <chemical equilibrium>; using initial values requires negligible equilibration changes. Below, also neglect <self-ionization of water> and competing <chemical reactions>.

Stipulate $\mathrm pK_a=4.76$ for an illustrative <weak acid>, without identifying it with a measured substance. A <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratio of two gives
$$
\mathrm{pH}\approx4.76+\log_{10}2=5.061029995\ldots\approx5.06103.
$$
Equal <concentrations> give a ratio of one, whose <logarithm> is zero, hence <pH> $4.76$. Multiplying the ratio by ten raises <pH> by one: ratios $0.1$, $1$, and $10$ give <pH> values $3.76$, $4.76$, and $5.76$. The ratio controls the ideal prediction, while the absolute quantities control how much added <chemical acid> or <chemical base> the <buffer solution> can accommodate.

\Image[buffer-ratio-ph.png]
{title=The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> turns a tenfold ratio change into one unit of <pH>}
{description=The stipulated <weak acid> has $\mathrm pK_a=4.76$. Points mark <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratios $0.1$, $1$, $2$, and $10$. The curve uses ideal dilute <thermodynamic activities>, <chemical equilibrium>, and negligible <self-ionization of water>; it is a calculation, rather than measured data.}

= Henderson-Hasselbalch equation
{c}
{parent=Buffer solution}
{wiki=Henderson–Hasselbalch_equation}
{title2=$\mathrm{pH}\approx\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}([\mathrm A^-]/[\mathrm{HA}])$}

The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> estimates <buffer solution> <pH> using a <weak acid>'s <acid dissociation constant> and the <conjugate base> to <weak acid> <concentration> ratio.

Its activity form is an exact rearrangement of the chosen <chemical equilibrium> relation. Write the acid dissociation step as $\mathrm{HA}\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H^++\mathrm A^-$, with hydrated <hydrogen ions> understood in <water> and the <solvent> convention incorporated into the <acid dissociation constant>. For consistent <standard states>,
$$
K_a=\frac{a_{\mathrm H^+}a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}},\qquad
\mathrm pK_a=-\log_{10}K_a,\qquad
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{a_{\mathrm A^-}}{a_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
Here each $a_i$ is a <dimensionless> <thermodynamic activity> at <chemical equilibrium>. Exactness refers to this specified <acid dissociation constant> relation and the chosen single-<ion> <thermodynamic activity> convention, not to identifying the ratio with quantities initially mixed. If <water> is instead written explicitly as a <reactant>, its <thermodynamic activity> must be treated consistently. See https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf[Hippler and Metcalfe, Using activities to correct the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation (2020), equations 3–4]{ref}.

For a toy <weak acid> with stipulated <pKa> $5$, use the ideal dilute <concentration> approximation and put $r=[\mathrm A^-]/[\mathrm{HA}]$. The <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> becomes $\mathrm{pH}\approx5+\log_{10}r$:
$$
r=0.1\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx4,\qquad
r=1\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx5,\qquad
r=10\ \Rightarrow\ \mathrm{pH}\approx6.
$$
More <conjugate base> relative to <weak acid> means higher <pH>. Each tenfold increase in the ratio contributes exactly one to the calculated <pH>. The stipulated <pKa> is not a tabulated value for an identified substance.

\Image[buffer-ph-versus-species-ratio.png]
{title=Toy <pKa> $5$: ratios $0.1$, $1$, and $10$ give calculated <pH> $4$, $5$, and $6$}
{description=Both panels show the same <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation> in the ideal <concentration> approximation, with linear and <logarithmic scales> for the <conjugate base> to <weak acid> ratio. The <logarithmic scale> turns the curve into a straight line. The plotted range is illustrative, rather than a guarantee of useful <buffer capacity>.}

The distinction between <thermodynamic activity> and <concentration> is explicit when $a_i=\gamma_i[i]/c^\circ$, where $\gamma_i$ is an <activity coefficient> and $c^\circ$ is the reference <concentration>:
$$
\mathrm{pH}=\mathrm pK_a+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{[\mathrm A^-]}{[\mathrm{HA}]}\right)+\log_{10}\!\left(\frac{\gamma_{\mathrm A^-}}{\gamma_{\mathrm{HA}}}\right).
$$
Neglecting the final term gives the usual approximate <Henderson-Hasselbalch equation>. It is justified when the ratio of <activity coefficients> is sufficiently close to one, as in a suitable dilute ideal approximation. <Ionic strength>, including contributions from other dissolved <ions>, affects <activity coefficients>; a nominally dilute <weak acid> alone does not establish that all corrections are small. The ratio must use <chemical equilibrium> <concentrations>. Replacing these by prepared amounts or post-<neutralization> inventories is a further approximation, suitable only when subsequent redistribution is small. See https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf[Hippler and Metcalfe, concentration, activity, and analytical-concentration approximations]{ref}.

The short derivation is to solve the <acid dissociation constant> relation for $a_{\mathrm H^+}$, take a negative base-ten <logarithm>, and use the definitions of <pH> and <pKa>. Its useful beauty is that a <chemical equilibrium> becomes a simple rule about a ratio: select a <weak acid> with <pKa> near the desired <pH>, then adjust the partner ratio. Scaling both partner <concentrations> equally leaves the ideal <pH> prediction unchanged, while changing <buffer capacity>. In practical <buffer solutions>, <temperature>, <ionic strength>, and the available inventories still matter.

= Buffer capacity
{parent=Buffer solution}

<Buffer capacity> describes how much added <chemical acid> or <chemical base> a specified volume of <buffer solution> can accommodate for a specified change in <pH>. It depends on the total <concentration> and the balance of the conjugate partners; <pH> alone does not determine it. At the same initial partner ratio and volume, larger inventories generally permit a larger addition before the same <pH> change in the ideal model. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-6-buffers[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, buffer capacity]{ref}.

= Neutralization
{parent=Acid-base chemistry}
{wiki=Neutralization_(chemistry)}

A <neutralization> is a <chemical reaction> between a <chemical acid> and a <chemical base>. In the <buffer solution> example, <hydroxide> takes a <proton> from the <weak acid> to form <water> and the <conjugate base>. The <stoichiometry> updates the partner inventories before the approximate <chemical equilibrium> calculation. The name does not require the resulting <pH> to be $7$.

= Mixture
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Solution
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Mixture}
{wiki}

= Chemical solution
{synonym}

A <chemical solution> is a homogeneous <mixture> in which designated <solutes> are distributed in a <solvent>. The distinction between <solute> and <solvent> is part of the description; <water> is the <solvent> in the aqueous <pH> examples.

= Ionic strength
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}
{title2=$I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$}

The concentration-based <ionic strength> is $I_c=\frac12\sum_i c_i z_i^2$, where $c_i$ is each dissolved <ion>'s <concentration> and $z_i$ its signed <electric charge> number. It has the same units as $c_i$; some formulas instead use the <dimensionless> ratio $I_c/c^\circ$. All dissolved <ions> contribute, including those that balance the <electric charge> of a <buffer solution>'s <conjugate base>. <Ionic strength> helps describe the interactions represented by <activity coefficients>. See https://eprints.whiterose.ac.uk/id/eprint/167121/1/BM_5_2020_Unterricht_Hippler_Metcalfe.pdf[Hippler and Metcalfe, activity coefficients and dimensionless ionic strength]{ref}.

= Solute
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

A <solute> is a component of a <chemical solution> distinguished from its <solvent>. Its <concentration> describes how much is present relative to a specified quantity of <chemical solution> or <solvent>.

= Solvent
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}
{wiki}

= Dilution
{parent=Solution (chemistry)}

<Dilution> lowers a <solute>'s <concentration> by adding <solvent>. When the <amount of substance> of that <solute> is conserved and $c$ denotes its amount per <volume (thermodynamics)> of <chemical solution>, $c_1V_1=c_2V_2$. This conservation statement does not by itself preserve the <pH>: a <chemical equilibrium> may change as the <chemical solution> is diluted.

= Infrared spectroscopy
{parent=Chemistry}

= IR spectroscopy
{c}
{synonym}

<Infrared spectroscopy> measures a sample's response to <infrared> <electromagnetic radiation>. Absorption associated with stretching or bending motions of <chemical bonds> helps identify groups and their environments. Peak positions are commonly reported as reciprocal <wavelength>, in $\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$; a lower value means lower <frequency>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/12-6-infrared-spectroscopy[McMurry: infrared spectroscopy and its wavelength convention]{ref}

= Stereochemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Stereochemical
{synonym}

<Stereochemistry> studies the spatial arrangement of <chemical atoms> in <molecules>. A <Fischer projection> records this information at successive <stereocenters>, whereas a <molecular formula> only counts <chemical atoms>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-2-representing-carbohydrate-stereochemistry-fischer-projections[McMurry: representing carbohydrate stereochemistry]{ref}.

= Stereocenter
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

At a <stereocenter>, exchanging two attached groups produces a different spatial configuration. The open-chain <ribose> and <glucose> examples have <carbon> <stereocenters> bearing four different groups; C2 of <deoxyribose> has two identical <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/RS.html#stereogenic[Moss, Basic Terminology of Stereochemistry, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: stereogenic units]{ref}.

= Fischer projection
{c}
{parent=Stereochemistry}
{wiki}

In a <Fischer projection>, each cross represents a <carbon> position: horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. For the open-chain <aldehyde> <sugars> illustrated here, place $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom. This is a convention for <stereochemistry>, not a measured molecular shape. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/stereo/FM.html#Fischer[Moss, IUPAC Recommendations 1996: Fischer projection]{ref}.

In these <Fischer projections>, D specifies that the <hydroxy group> at the lowest <stereocenter> lies on the right: C4 for <ribose> and <deoxyribose>, C5 for <glucose>. D does not specify every other <stereocenter>, and it does not specify the direction of rotation of polarized <light>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars[McMurry: the meaning of D and L]{ref}.

= Hydroxy group
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{OH}$}

= Hydroxyl group
{synonym}

A <hydroxy group> is an $\mathrm{OH}$ group attached to the rest of a <molecule>. In the illustrated <sugars>, its <oxygen> is bonded to <carbon> and to <hydrogen>.

= Chemical substance
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Amine
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$R\mathrm{NH}_2,\ R_2\mathrm{NH},\ R_3\mathrm N$}

A simple neutral <amine> has <nitrogen> bonded to one, two, or three groups attached through <carbon>, with <hydrogen> filling the remaining positions in the neutral three-bond form. These are the primary, secondary, and tertiary forms respectively. An <amide> has different connectivity to a <carbonyl group>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-1-naming-amines[McMurry: classification of amines]{ref}

= Amide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$R-\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)-\mathrm{NR'R''}$}

Here an <amide> means a <carbonyl group> whose <carbon> is bonded directly to <nitrogen>; each of $R$, $R'$ and $R''$ may be <hydrogen> or a group attached through <carbon>. A <peptide bond> contains this linkage. <Chemical resonance> distinguishes its <nitrogen> from that of an <amine>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/24-3-basicity-of-amines[McMurry: amide resonance]{ref} and https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/20-4-amines-and-amides[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, 20.4: amide and peptide connectivity]{ref}

= Hydrogen peroxide
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}_2$}
{wiki}

= Acyl group
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}}$}

For the <carboxylic acid>-derived examples here, an <acyl group> is the group remaining after formal removal of $\mathrm{OH}$ from $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}OH}$. It includes the <oxygen> at the end of the <fatty acid>-derived chain, so it is more than just a <hydrocarbon> region. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/carba.html[IUPAC class names: carboxylic acids and derivatives]{ref}.

= Sphingosine
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Zwitterion
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Zwitterionic
{synonym}

A <zwitterion> has opposite formal <electric charges> in different regions of a <molecule>, with zero net <electric charge>. <Phosphatidylcholine> provides a membrane-related example. This is stronger than merely having <chemical polarity> through fractional <partial atomic charges>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/Z06752[IUPAC Gold Book: zwitterions]{ref}.

= Lipid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Lipids> are a chemically diverse family of <chemical substances>, conventionally associated with biological origin and solubility in <nonpolar> <solvents>. They include <triglycerides>, <phospholipids>, and <cholesterol>. The name does not specify one <molecular formula>, one repeating <monomer>, or one kind of <polymer>. Some <lipids> have both <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions rather than being uniformly <nonpolar>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/L03571[IUPAC Gold Book: lipids]{ref}.

Structure explains the different jobs. A <triglyceride> has three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> attached to <glycerol> and serves as an <energy> store. A typical <glycerophospholipid> instead combines two such chains with a <phosphate>-containing head, making it an <amphiphile> suited to a <lipid bilayer>. <Cholesterol> has a different structure and also contributes to <cell membrane> properties. Thus a <lipid> is a chemical building material, while a <lipid bilayer> is an organization of many <molecules>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.3: lipid structures and functions]{ref}.

= Triglyceride
{parent=Lipid}

= Triacylglycerol
{synonym}

A <triglyceride> contains <glycerol> joined to three <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages. Compare the three chains with the two-chain, <phosphate>-headed <glycerophospholipid> illustrated below. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: fats and oils]{ref}.

= Cholesterol
{parent=Lipid}

<Cholesterol> is a <lipid> with four fused rings and a small <hydrophilic> region. It helps regulate the fluidity of animal <cell membranes>; it is structurally distinct from a <phospholipid>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: cholesterol]{ref}.

= Phospholipid
{parent=Lipid}
{wiki}

<Phospholipids> are <lipids> containing a <phosphate> group as part of their structure linked by <covalent bonds>. The familiar membrane-forming <phospholipids> are <amphiphilic>: a <hydrophilic> head region with <chemical polarity> coexists with a <hydrophobic>, largely <nonpolar> <hydrocarbon> region. These descriptions refer to different parts of the same <molecule>, not to two separate substances. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/P04559[IUPAC Gold Book: phospholipids]{ref} and https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303[IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic]{ref}.

A typical two-chain <glycerophospholipid> provides a useful example: a <glycerol> scaffold carries two <fatty acid>-derived <acyl groups> through <ester> linkages, plus a <phosphate>-containing head region. <Phosphatidylcholine> is one example. This scaffold describes an important subclass, rather than all <phospholipids>: <sphingomyelin> has a different backbone, and even <glycerophospholipids> need not all have two <acyl groups>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html[IUPAC–IUB, Nomenclature of Lipids (1976), Lip-1.5 and Lip-2.1–2.6]{ref}.

\Image[phospholipid-amphiphilic-schematic.png]
{title=Polar head and <hydrophobic> chains of an illustrative <glycerophospholipid>}
{description=Colored regions represent a <phosphate>-containing head, a <glycerol> scaffold, and two <fatty acid>-derived chains. The small paired-chain symbol is shorthand for this illustrative subclass. Shapes and connectors show organization, not <chemical atoms>, exact <chemical bonds>, chain lengths, or a measured <molecular geometry>. The head is not assigned a universal <electric charge>.}

Polar does not mean negatively charged overall. At near-neutral <pH>, <phosphatidylcholine> is <zwitterionic>: its <phosphate> region carries negative <electric charge> and its <choline> region positive <electric charge>, with zero net <electric charge> for the <molecule>. Counting those opposite charges gives $(-1)+(+1)=0$; it does not remove their local interactions with <water>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/[Lodish et al., Molecular Cell Biology: membrane lipids]{ref}.

In a <lipid bilayer>, two <membrane leaflets> orient their <hydrophilic> heads toward the <water> on either side and their <hydrophobic> chains toward the shared interior. The <hydrophobic effect> helps explain this organization through the <free energy> of the <water> and <lipids> together. It is not a special repulsive <chemical bond> between chains and <water>. Which organization is favored also depends on <molecular geometry>, <temperature>, <concentration>, and the surrounding <chemical solution>; a <phospholipid> label alone does not guarantee a <lipid bilayer>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26871/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the lipid bilayer]{ref}.

\Image[phospholipid-bilayer-organization.png]
{title=Two <membrane leaflets> shelter a <hydrophobic> interior between water-facing heads}
{description=An illustrative <phospholipid>-based <lipid bilayer> has <water> on both sides. Individual <phospholipids> can undergo <diffusion> within a fluid <membrane leaflet>. The heads and chains are schematic regions; spacing, arrows, and chain shapes are unscaled. This is a local cross-section, without assigning either side to a particular <biological cell> compartment.}

This organization supplies a flexible barrier for compartments. A fluid <lipid bilayer> allows <lipids> to undergo <diffusion> within each <membrane leaflet>; barrier function therefore coexists with motion of <molecules>, as in the <fluid mosaic model>. It is not completely impermeable: small <nonpolar> <molecules> such as <molecular oxygen> and <carbon dioxide> cross much more readily than <ions>. In a <cell membrane>, <ion channels> and other <membrane transport proteins> provide selective routes; the existing <membrane transport> article explains their driving forces. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26815/[Alberts et al.: principles of membrane transport]{ref}.

In 1925, E. Gorter and F. Grendel extracted <lipids> from <red blood cells> and compared the <surface area> of a one-<molecule>-thick film on <water> with the estimated total <surface area> of the <red blood cells>. Their table gave ratios near two, supporting a two-layer covering; they also proposed heads facing the aqueous surroundings on both sides. The inference depends on recovering the relevant <lipids>, estimating the <red blood cell> <surface area> correctly, and comparing compatible packing in the film and the <cell membrane>. It was indirect evidence, not an image of the <lipid bilayer>. The paper itself notes possible losses during evaporation. See https://doi.org/10.1084/jem.41.4.439[Gorter and Grendel, On Bimolecular Layers of Lipoids on the Chromocytes of the Blood, Journal of Experimental Medicine 41 (1925), 439–443]{ref}.

= Glycerophospholipid
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Glycerophospholipids> are <phospholipids> built on a <glycerol>-<phosphate> scaffold. The illustrated two-<acyl group> form is common, but the subclass also includes other substitutions and one-chain derivatives. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/lipid/lip1n2.html[IUPAC–IUB lipid nomenclature, Lip-2.2 and Lip-2.6]{ref}.

= Phosphatidylcholine
{parent=Glycerophospholipid}
{wiki}

= PC
{c}
{synonym}

<Phosphatidylcholine>, abbreviated <PC>, is a <glycerophospholipid> with a <phosphate>-<choline> head. Its <zwitterionic> head illustrates why a <hydrophilic> region need not have a nonzero net <electric charge>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK21726/[Lodish et al.: membrane lipids]{ref}.

= Sphingomyelin
{parent=Phospholipid}
{wiki}

<Sphingomyelin> is a <phospholipid> whose backbone is based on <sphingosine>, rather than <glycerol>. One long <hydrocarbon> region belongs to that backbone and another comes from an attached <fatty acid>. It is a counterexample to defining every <phospholipid> by the illustrated <glycerol> scaffold. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: the molecular composition of cells]{ref}.

= Aldehyde
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

An <aldehyde> has a group $\mathrm R\mathrm C(=\mathrm O)\mathrm H$, written $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top of the illustrated open-chain <Fischer projections>; $R$ denotes the attached remainder of the <molecule>.

= Carbohydrate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

<Carbohydrates> are a family of <chemical substances> encompassing <monosaccharides>, combinations of <monosaccharides>, and their derivatives. <Glucose> is a small <carbohydrate>; <cellulose> is a long-chain <polysaccharide>. <Deoxyribose> illustrates why composition alone does not define the family: replacing a <hydroxy group> in <ribose> by <hydrogen> still leaves a <carbohydrate>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820[IUPAC Gold Book: carbohydrates]{ref}.

Many familiar <monosaccharides> have a <molecular formula> of the form $\mathrm C_n\mathrm H_{2n}\mathrm O_n$, equivalently $\mathrm C_n(\mathrm H_2\mathrm O)_n$. This explains the historical name, “hydrate of <carbon>”, but does not mean the <molecule> consists of <carbon> mixed with intact <water> <molecules>. Nor is it a universal formula: <deoxyribose> has $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. The family is defined through chemical structure and relationships, not that ratio. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C00820[IUPAC's definition and origin of the name carbohydrate]{ref}.

The useful surprise is the variety of roles obtainable from related building blocks. <Glucose> participates in <metabolism>, <ribose> forms part of <RNA>, and linked <glucose> units make structural <cellulose> in a plant <cell wall>. Understanding <carbohydrates> therefore connects cellular <energy>, molecular information, and material structure. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the chemical components of a cell]{ref}.

= Sucrose
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C_{12}H_{22}O_{11}}$}

<Sucrose> dissolves in <water> as neutral <molecules>; it is a <nonelectrolyte> in this <solvent>.

= Sugar
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

In chemical usage, <sugars> commonly means small <carbohydrates>, including single-unit <monosaccharides> and two-unit <disaccharides>. <Glucose>, <ribose>, and <deoxyribose> are <monosaccharides>. <Cellulose> is instead a <polysaccharide> assembled from many <glucose> units. The distinction counts chemically linked units; it does not classify every <carbohydrate> by sweetness or biological role. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Clark, Douglas and Choi, Biology 2e, 3.2: carbohydrates]{ref}.

A useful first description of a <monosaccharide> asks how many <carbon> <chemical atoms> it contains and what its open-chain structure is. <Ribose> is an <aldopentose>, a five-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugar>; <glucose> is an <aldohexose>, with six <carbon> <chemical atoms>. A <Fischer projection> then records the <stereochemistry>, information missing from the <molecular formula>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.1: carbohydrate classification]{ref}.

= Ribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$}

<Ribose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. Its biologically familiar form is D-ribose, an <aldopentose> whose open-chain <Fischer projection> has $\mathrm{CHO}$ at the top, $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom, and <hydroxy groups> on the right at C2, C3, and C4. The opposite horizontal substituents are <hydrogen>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-3-d-l-sugars[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.3: D-ribose and D-glucose configurations]{ref}.

\Image[pentose-sugar-fischer-comparison.png]
{title=Open-chain <Fischer projections> of D-<ribose> and 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>}
{description=At each cross, horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer and vertical <chemical bonds> point away. Both drawings number the free <sugar> from its <aldehyde> carbon, C1. In <deoxyribose>, C2 has two <hydrogen> substituents and is not a <stereocenter>. These are open-chain reference structures, not a depiction of the predominant <chemical species> in <water>.}

Read the drawing by counting groups: $\mathrm{CHO}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H\mathrm O$, three $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups contribute $\mathrm C_3\mathrm H_6\mathrm O_3$, and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ contributes $\mathrm C\mathrm H_3\mathrm O$. Their sum is $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$. The count checks composition; the right/right/right pattern additionally specifies the <stereochemistry> of D-<ribose>.

In <RNA>, <ribose> occurs as a ring within each <nucleotide>, joining a <nucleobase> to the <phosphate>-linked backbone. Its C2 position retains a <hydroxy group>, unlike <deoxyribose> in <DNA>. In a <nucleotide>, the <sugar> positions carry primes, so this is C2′. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: nucleotide sugars in RNA and DNA]{ref}. Free <ribose> in <water> is also predominantly cyclic, with several ring forms in <chemical equilibrium> and very little open-chain form; the <Fischer projection> is valuable for comparing configurations, not for estimating their abundances. See https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3819725/[Mason et al., The Conformation of a Ribose Derivative in Aqueous Solution: A Neutron Scattering and Molecular Dynamics Study (2013), introduction]{ref}.

= Deoxyribose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$}

In the usual <DNA> context, <deoxyribose> means 2-deoxy-D-<ribose>, a five-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. Relative to D-<ribose>, the <hydroxy group> at C2 is replaced by <hydrogen>. The locant 2 matters: it identifies which position loses its <oxygen>. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P10.html#P-102.5.3[Favre and Powell, IUPAC Blue Book, P-102.5.3: deoxy sugars]{ref} and https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/class/nucle.html[IUPAC: nucleic acids and their component sugars]{ref}.

The paired <Fischer projections> in <ribose> make the change concrete. C2 has <hydrogen> on both sides, while C3 and C4 retain their <hydroxy groups> on the right. C2 is therefore not a <stereocenter>: exchanging its two identical <hydrogen> substituents cannot produce a distinct configuration. The unchanged C3 and C4 still carry <stereochemical> information.

Replacing $\mathrm{OH}$ by $\mathrm H$ removes one <oxygen> but leaves the total <hydrogen> count unchanged: the <hydrogen> belonging to the removed <hydroxy group> is replaced by a <hydrogen> bonded directly to <carbon>. Thus $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_5$ becomes $\mathrm C_5\mathrm H_{10}\mathrm O_4$. This comparison describes structures, rather than a complete <chemical reaction> or its mechanism.

In <DNA>, <deoxyribose> is incorporated as a ring in each <nucleotide>, with <phosphate> connections building the backbone. Its C2′ bears <hydrogen> where <RNA>'s <ribose> bears a <hydroxy group>. This small local difference lets the <sugar> structure identify the two kinds of <nucleic acid>; the <nucleobases> supply their sequence information. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: nucleotides as the subunits of DNA and RNA]{ref}.

= Glucose
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_6\mathrm{H}_{12}\mathrm{O}_6$}

<Glucose> is a <monosaccharide> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. Its familiar biological form, D-glucose, is an <aldohexose>. In the open-chain <Fischer projection>, $\mathrm{CHO}$ is at the top and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ at the bottom; the <hydroxy groups> at C2, C3, C4, and C5 point right, left, right, right. This full pattern distinguishes D-<glucose> from other six-<carbon> <aldehyde> <sugars>. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-4-configurations-of-the-aldoses[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.4: configurations of the aldoses]{ref}.

\Image[glucose-fischer-projection.png]
{title=D-<glucose>: six <carbon> positions and four open-chain <stereocenters>}
{description=Horizontal <chemical bonds> point toward the viewer; vertical <chemical bonds> point away. The <hydroxy group> pattern from C2 through C5 is right/left/right/right. $\mathrm{CHO}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ are condensed end groups. The diagram records an open-chain reference structure, not the predominant form in an aqueous <chemical solution>.}

The <molecular formula> can be checked directly: one $\mathrm{CHO}$ group, four $\mathrm{CHOH}$ groups, and one $\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{OH}$ group give six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms>. All four interior <carbon> positions are <stereocenters> in this open-chain structure. Neither their orientations nor the arrangement of <chemical bonds> can be recovered from the <molecular formula> alone.

In <water>, <glucose> exists mainly in six-membered rings in <chemical equilibrium> with a small amount of open-chain <glucose>. Ring closure connects the <hydroxy group> at C5 to the <aldehyde> carbon at C1; the ring contains five <carbon> <chemical atoms> and one <oxygen>, with C6 outside it. No <chemical atoms> are lost, so the <molecular formula> remains $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-5-cyclic-structures-of-monosaccharides-anomers[McMurry, Organic Chemistry, 25.5: cyclic structures of monosaccharides]{ref}.

<Glucose> is useful both as a substrate in cellular <metabolism> and as a building block of <polysaccharides>. <Cellulose> chains assembled from <glucose> units help support plant <cell walls>. The same small <sugar> thus connects chemical fuel to a structural material; its role depends on how it is transformed or linked. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK9879/[Cooper, The Cell: A Molecular Approach, the molecular composition of cells]{ref}.

= Monosaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <monosaccharide> is a single-unit <carbohydrate> that cannot be split into smaller <sugars> by <hydrolysis>. <Ribose>, <deoxyribose>, and <glucose> are examples. “Single-unit” does not mean a single <chemical atom> or a permanently open chain. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry: simple carbohydrates]{ref}.

= Aldopentose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldopentose> is a five-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with an <aldehyde> group in its open-chain form. <Ribose> is an example; <deoxyribose> is its 2-deoxy derivative. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry: carbohydrate classification]{ref}.

= Aldohexose
{parent=Monosaccharide}

An <aldohexose> is a six-<carbon> <monosaccharide> with an <aldehyde> group in its open-chain form. <Glucose> is an example. See https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/25-1-classification-of-carbohydrates[McMurry: carbohydrate classification]{ref}.

= Disaccharide
{parent=Sugar}
{wiki}

A <disaccharide> contains two linked <monosaccharide> units. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Biology 2e: disaccharides]{ref}.

= Polysaccharide
{parent=Carbohydrate}
{wiki}

A <polysaccharide> is a <polymer> built from many linked <monosaccharide> units. <Cellulose> provides a structural example. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-2-carbohydrates[Biology 2e: polysaccharides]{ref}.

= Cellulose
{parent=Polysaccharide}
{wiki}

<Cellulose> is a <polysaccharide> of <glucose> units whose chains form strong assemblies in plant <cell walls>. It illustrates a structural role for <carbohydrates>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26928/[Alberts et al., Molecular Biology of the Cell: the plant cell wall]{ref}.

= Triose phosphate
{parent=Carbohydrate}

A <triose phosphate> is a three-<carbon> <sugar> carrying a <phosphate> group. <Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate> is one example. The <Calvin cycle> supplies a net <triose phosphate> for further <carbohydrate> synthesis. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: the Calvin cycle's carbohydrate product]{ref}.

= Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate
{parent=Triose phosphate}
{wiki}

= G3P
{c}
{synonym}

= Phosphate
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Inorganic phosphate
{parent=Phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm P_i$}

<Inorganic phosphate>, abbreviated $\mathrm P_i$, denotes the free <phosphate> pool in biochemical <chemical equations>, including <ATP> <hydrolysis>. Its constituent <chemical species> depend on <pH> and <protonation>; $\mathrm P_i$ does not specify a single <electric charge> or proton count. See the https://iubmb.qmul.ac.uk/thermod2/[IUBMB recommendations on biochemical reactant pools]{ref}.

= Chemical formula
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <chemical formula> uses symbols of <chemical elements> and numerical subscripts to record composition. In the <molecular formula> $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ of <water>, the subscript $2$ counts two <hydrogen> <chemical atoms>, and the omitted subscript after $\mathrm O$ means one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. A <molecular formula> describes one <molecule>; a <chemical formula> need not describe a discrete <molecule>.

Read a preceding coefficient separately from the subscripts. One $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains two <hydrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>; $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$ contains four <hydrogen> and two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> distributed between two <water> <molecules>. In $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$, the second subscript instead puts two <oxygen> <chemical atoms> into each <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Thus $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\ne\mathrm H_2\mathrm O_2$: the coefficient scales the whole <chemical formula>, while a subscript specifies its composition. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-4-chemical-formulas[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, §2.4: chemical formulas]{ref}.

\Image[chemical-formula-and-coefficient.png]
{title=A coefficient counts whole <molecules>; a subscript counts <chemical atoms> within each <molecule>}
{description=The three columns show one <water> <molecule>, two <water> <molecules>, and one <hydrogen peroxide> <molecule>. Their <hydrogen> counts are $2,4,2$ and their <oxygen> counts are $1,2,2$. Connections are schematic $\mathrm{H-O-H}$ and $\mathrm{H-O-O-H}$, not models of actual <bond angles> or <bond lengths>.}

For crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, the <chemical formula> specifies a $1:1$ ratio of <sodium> <ions> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ to <chlorine> <ions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$. Its <formula unit> is the accounting entity; the ordinary crystal is an extended arrangement of <ions>, rather than a collection of discrete $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <molecules>. Specifying the entity matters when assigning an <amount of substance>. See <formula unit> and <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/2.6 Ionic and Molecular Compounds>[§2.6: ionic and molecular compounds].

A <molecular formula> gives an atom inventory, not the full arrangement of <chemical bonds> or the <molecular geometry>. For example, $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_{12}\mathrm O_6$ counts six <carbon>, twelve <hydrogen>, and six <oxygen> <chemical atoms> in a <glucose> <molecule>, but does not specify the pattern shown by its <Fischer projection>. Different structures can share a <molecular formula>. This compact inventory is useful for <chemical equation> accounting precisely because it leaves the structural detail to other representations.

The choice of letters has an early explanation in https://web2.lemoyne.edu/~giunta/berzelius.html[Jöns Jacob Berzelius, Essay on the Cause of Chemical Proportions (1813–1814), §III, transcribed by Carmen Giunta]{ref}: letters made chemical signs easier to write and print, and formulas compressed information about composition. His numerical notation differed from modern subscripts; the lasting idea was to make proportions readable in ordinary text.

= Molecular formula
{parent=Chemical formula}

A molecular formula gives the number of <chemical atoms> of each <chemical element> in a <molecule>. For <urea>, $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ counts one <carbon>, four <hydrogen>, two <nitrogen> and one <oxygen> <chemical atom>. It records composition rather than the arrangement of <chemical bonds>.

= Formula unit
{parent=Chemical formula}
{wiki}

A <formula unit> is a specified compositional accounting entity for a <chemical substance> that need not consist of discrete <molecules>. For ordinary crystalline $\mathrm{NaCl}$, one <formula unit> counts one <sodium> <ion> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and one <chlorine> <ion> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$; it does not identify an isolated pair within the crystal. https://iupac.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/05/IUPAC-GB3-2012-2ndPrinting-PDFsearchable.pdf#page=65[Cohen et al., IUPAC Green Book, 3rd ed., §2.10.1(iii)(b), p. 51]{ref} defines a <formula unit> as a specified group of <chemical atoms>, rather than a unit of measurement. An <amount of substance> of one <mole> of $\mathrm{NaCl}$ <formula units> therefore corresponds to one <mole> of each kind of <ion>.

= Organic acid
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Carboxylic acid
{parent=Organic acid}
{wiki}

= Acetic acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH_3COOH}$}

<Acetic acid> is a <weak acid> that partly transfers <protons> to <water>, supplying <hydronium> and negative <ions>. It is therefore a <weak electrolyte> in <water>.

= Fatty acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}

A <fatty acid> is a <carboxylic acid> with a <hydrocarbon> chain. Its chain supplies much of the <hydrophobic> region when incorporated into a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid>. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: fatty acids]{ref}.

= Amino acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}

= Methionine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Met},\ \mathrm M$}

Methionine is an <amino acid> represented by Met or M in <protein> sequence notation. In the standard <genetic code>, AUG encodes it, as in the worked <gene expression> example. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: the standard genetic code]{ref}

= Alanine
{parent=Amino acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ala},\ \mathrm A$}

Alanine is an <amino acid> represented by Ala or A in <protein> sequence notation. GCU is one of its <codons> in the standard <genetic code>, and supplies the second residue in the worked <gene expression> example. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/15-1-the-genetic-code[OpenStax Biology 2e, 15.1: alanine codons]{ref}

= Citric acid
{parent=Carboxylic acid}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_8\mathrm O_7$}

Its <chemical formula> and structure are recorded in https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:30769[ChEBI, citric acid]{ref}.

= Citrate
{parent=Citric acid}

Citrate denotes an <ion> derived from <citric acid>. The commonly specified form has <electric charge> equal to that of three <electrons> and <chemical formula> $\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_5\mathrm O_7^{3-}$; it results from removing three <hydrogen> <ions> from <citric acid>. Forms with additional <hydrogen> <ions> attached through <protonation> also occur. https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chebi/CHEBI:16947[ChEBI, citrate(3−)]{ref}

= Hydrogen deuteride
{parent=Chemical substance}
{title2=$\mathrm{HD}$}

The diatomic molecule containing one ordinary hydrogen nucleus and one deuterium nucleus is an isotopic form of <molecular hydrogen>. It has a weak electric dipole and can supply low-temperature <molecular line cooling> in primordial gas when its abundance is sufficient.

= Chemical element
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

A <chemical element> is an identity shared by all <chemical atoms> whose <atomic nuclei> contain the same number of <protons>. That number is its <atomic number>, $Z$: six means <carbon>, eleven means <sodium>. The identity survives differences in <neutron> count, which distinguish <isotopes>, and differences in <electron> count, which distinguish neutral <chemical atoms> from <ions>. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/C01022[IUPAC Gold Book, chemical element]{ref}.

The same name can also denote a pure <chemical substance> containing only that <chemical element>. These two uses answer different questions. <Molecular oxygen>, $\mathrm O_2$, is a <chemical substance> made entirely of <oxygen>; <water>, $\mathrm H_2\mathrm O$, is a <chemical compound> containing both <hydrogen> and <oxygen>. A <water> <molecule> contains three <chemical atoms> but only two <chemical elements>. Joining <chemical atoms> through <chemical bonds> changes their arrangement without changing their <atomic numbers>.

This distinction makes <stoichiometry> possible: ordinary <chemical reactions> rearrange <chemical atoms>, so one can count each <chemical element> before and after a transformation. Changing the <proton> count instead changes the <chemical element> and requires a <nuclear reaction>. The <periodic table> organizes these identities into a useful map, while <atomic number> bookkeeping keeps identity, <isotope>, and <electric charge> separate.

= Zinc
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Zn}$}

= Copper
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cu}$}

= Periodic table
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}

The <periodic table> arranges <chemical elements> in increasing <atomic number>, across successive <periodic table periods>. Its columns are <periodic groups>, numbered 1–18. The layout places recurring outer <electron configurations> above one another, making similarities in <chemical bonds> and <chemical reactions> easier to recognize. Ordering is by <proton> count, not by <mass>; the <mass number> of a particular <isotope> does not determine its box. See the https://iupac.org/what-we-do/periodic-table-of-elements/[official IUPAC periodic table and numbering conventions]{ref}.

For the first eighteen neutral <chemical atoms> in their <ground states>, a compact description counts <electrons> in successive <electron shells>. Thus <carbon> has $(2,4)$, meaning two <electrons> in the first <electron shell> and four in the second; the sum $2+4=6$ agrees with its <atomic number>. <Sodium> has $(2,8,1)$, whose sum is $11$. These are occupancy counts, not circular trajectories of <electrons>.

\Image[first-three-periods.png]
{title=The first three <periodic table periods>: <atomic numbers> and neutral <ground state> <electron shell> occupancies}
{description=Colours distinguish <periodic group> columns only, without encoding numerical property trends. The spaces in columns 3–12 preserve the eighteen-column <periodic table> layout: no <chemical elements> occupy those positions in these three <periodic table periods>. Each cell gives a <chemical element>'s symbol, <atomic number>, and <electron shell> counts.}

Reading each row from left to right gives:
* <Periodic table period> 1: <hydrogen> $(1)$ and <helium> $(2)$.
* <Periodic table period> 2: <lithium> $(2,1)$, <beryllium> $(2,2)$, <boron> $(2,3)$, <carbon> $(2,4)$, <nitrogen> $(2,5)$, <oxygen> $(2,6)$, <fluorine> $(2,7)$, and <neon> $(2,8)$.
* <Periodic table period> 3: <sodium> $(2,8,1)$, <magnesium> $(2,8,2)$, <aluminium> $(2,8,3)$, <silicon> $(2,8,4)$, <phosphorus> $(2,8,5)$, <sulfur> $(2,8,6)$, <chlorine> $(2,8,7)$, and <argon> $(2,8,8)$.

The counting outline is short: the occupied <electron subshells> in this range are $1s$, $2s$, $2p$, $3s$, and $3p$, filled in that order for these neutral <ground states>. Each $s$ <electron subshell> holds up to two <electrons> and each $p$ <electron subshell> up to six, giving $2$, $2+6=8$, and $2+6=8$ along these three rows. This $2,8,8$ summary applies only through <argon>; it is not a general rule that each <electron shell> fills completely before the next begins, nor a claim that the third <electron shell> can hold only eight <electrons>. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/6-4-electronic-structure-of-atoms-electron-configurations[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, electronic structure and electron configurations]{ref}.

The recurring outer counts make the arrangement useful. <Lithium> and <sodium> each have one <valence electron>, whereas <fluorine> and <chlorine> each have seven <valence electrons>. Such similarities help anticipate <chemical reactions>, without making all properties identical throughout a <periodic group>. <Helium> is an instructive exception to an eight-<electron> shortcut: its two <electrons> complete the first <electron shell>, so it belongs above <neon> and <argon> in <periodic group> 18. <Hydrogen> shares column 1 with <lithium> and <sodium> but has its own distinct chemistry. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-5-the-periodic-table[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, periodic-table organization]{ref}.

= Group
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic group
{synonym}

= Periodic table group
{synonym}

A <periodic group> is a vertical column of the <periodic table>. For example, <fluorine> and <chlorine> are in <periodic group> 17, with seven <valence electrons> in each neutral <ground state> <chemical atom>. A shared outer <electron configuration> supports similarities in <chemical reactions>, rather than equality of every property.

= Period
{disambiguate=periodic table}
{parent=Periodic table}
{wiki}

= Periodic table period
{synonym}

A <periodic table period> is a horizontal row of the <periodic table>, read in increasing <atomic number>. <Periodic table period> 2 runs from <lithium>, $Z=3$, to <neon>, $Z=10$: $10-3+1=8$ <chemical elements>. In these neutral <ground states>, the second <electron shell> gains <electrons> while the first retains two.

= Boron
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm B$, $Z=5$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> five.

= Fluorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm F$, $Z=9$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> nine.

= Aluminium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Al}$, $Z=13$}
{wiki}

= Aluminum
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> thirteen.

= Phosphorus
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm P$, $Z=15$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> fifteen.

= Sulfur
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm S$, $Z=16$}
{wiki}

= Sulphur
{synonym}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> sixteen.

= Chlorine
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Cl}$, $Z=17$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> seventeen.

= Argon
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ar}$, $Z=18$}
{wiki}

The <chemical element> with <atomic number> eighteen.

= Sodium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Na}$}

Sodium is the <chemical element> of <atomic number> eleven. Carbon-fusion channels in <carbon burning> include ${}^{12}\mathrm C+{}^{12}\mathrm C\longrightarrow{}^{23}\mathrm{Na}+p$, producing sodium-23 and a <proton>. Its abundance in expelled stellar material depends on later reactions and mixing, rather than on this one production channel alone.

= Calcium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ca}$}
{wiki}

= Potassium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{K}$}
{wiki}

= Atomic number
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$Z$}
{wiki}

The <atomic number> $Z$ is the number of <protons> in an <atomic nucleus>, and fixes the <chemical element>. It is a count, with no units. An <atomic nucleus> with six <protons> is always <carbon>, regardless of how many <neutrons> accompany them or how many <electrons> surround it. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00499[IUPAC Gold Book, atomic number]{ref}.

Three independent bookkeeping questions become easy once their symbols are kept separate. Let $N$ count <neutrons>, $n_e$ count <electrons>, $A$ be the <mass number>, and $Q$ be the net <electric charge>. A <proton> carries <electric charge> $+e$ and an <electron> carries $-e$, where $e$ is the positive <elementary charge>; a <neutron> carries zero net <electric charge>. Hence
$$
A=Z+N,\qquad N=A-Z,\qquad Q=(Z-n_e)e,\qquad n_e=Z-Q/e.
$$
The reasoning is just counting: add <protons> and <neutrons> for $A$, and subtract negative <electron> contributions from positive <proton> contributions for $Q$. A neutral <chemical atom> has $Q=0$, so $n_e=Z$. For a positively charged <ion>, fewer <electrons> remain. Notation ${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$ gives the <mass number> at upper left and <atomic number> at lower left, with $\mathrm X$ standing for the <chemical element>; a right superscript such as $+$ specifies the <electric charge> in units of $e$. See https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/2-3-atomic-structure-and-symbolism[Flowers et al., Chemistry 2e, atomic structure and symbolism]{ref}.

For neutral <carbon-12>, ${}^{12}_{6}\mathrm C$, the <atomic number> gives six <protons>, the subtraction $N=12-6=6$ gives six <neutrons>, and neutrality gives six <electrons>. Neutral <carbon-13>, ${}^{13}_{6}\mathrm C$, instead has $N=13-6=7$ <neutrons>, with the same six <protons> and six <electrons>. Thus these two <isotopes> occupy the same <carbon> box in the <periodic table>.

For neutral <sodium> of <mass number> $23$, ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}$, the counts are eleven <protons>, $23-11=12$ <neutrons>, and eleven <electrons>. Removing one <electron> gives the <ion> ${}^{23}_{11}\mathrm{Na}^{+}$: $Q/e=1$, so $n_e=11-1=10$. Both its <atomic number> and its twelve <neutrons> stay unchanged. This is <ionization>, not conversion into the <chemical element> <neon>, even though a neutral <neon> <chemical atom> also has ten <electrons>.

\Image[atomic-number-isotopes-and-ions.png]
{title=<Atomic number> fixes <chemical element> identity: changing <isotopes> changes <neutrons>, while forming a <sodium> <ion> changes <electrons>}
{description=The dots are counting tokens, with <protons> and <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus> inventory and <electrons> in a separate inventory. Their layout does not depict physical positions, sizes, or orbits. Neutral <carbon-12> and <carbon-13> differ by one <neutron>; neutral <sodium> of <mass number> 23 and its singly positive <ion> differ by one <electron>.}

This bookkeeping is useful whenever a symbol must be translated into a physical inventory. <Mass number> answers how many <protons> plus <neutrons> there are; <electric charge> answers the imbalance between <protons> and <electrons>; <atomic number> alone answers which <chemical element> it is. Keeping those questions distinct prevents an <isotope> or an <ion> from being mistaken for a different <chemical element>.

= Beryllium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Be}$}
{wiki}

Beryllium is the <chemical element> with atomic number four. Its isotope <beryllium-7> provides an important route to primordial <lithium-7>.

= Beryllium-7
{parent=Beryllium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Be}$}

Beryllium-7 contains four <protons> and three <neutrons>. It forms in reactions including $^3\mathrm{He}+{}^4\mathrm{He}\to{}^7\mathrm{Be}+\gamma$ and becomes <lithium-7> through <electron capture>. In the early universe its ionization state affects when capture occurs, so the final primordial lithium inventory includes the earlier beryllium inventory rather than just lithium present when nuclear burning ends.

= Lithium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{Li}$}
{wiki}

Lithium is the <chemical element> with atomic number three. Its stable isotopes include lithium-6 and <lithium-7>. Lithium observed in old stellar atmospheres is an important but processing-sensitive test of <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Lithium-7
{parent=Lithium}
{title2=$^7\mathrm{Li}$}

Lithium-7 has three <protons> and four <neutrons>. In <Big Bang nucleosynthesis> it can form directly or through <beryllium-7>, which later undergoes <electron capture>. Competition between direct lithium production, lithium destruction and beryllium production produces a valley in the primordial lithium abundance versus <baryon-to-photon ratio>. Its measured stellar surface abundance need not equal its birth abundance because stellar transport and nuclear burning can remove lithium.

= Isotope
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=${}^{A}_{Z}\mathrm X$}
{wiki}

Isotopes are atoms of the same <chemical element>, with the same number of <protons> but different numbers of <neutrons>. Their abundance ratios can distinguish material sources and biological or chemical fractionation, while radioactive isotopes can additionally carry chronological information.

= Mass number
{parent=Isotope}
{title2=$A=Z+N$}
{wiki}

The <mass number> $A$ counts <protons> plus <neutrons> in an <atomic nucleus>. It is a whole-number count, rather than a measured <mass>. Subtracting the <atomic number> gives the <neutron> count, $N=A-Z$. See the https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/M03726[IUPAC Gold Book, mass number]{ref}.

= Uranium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{U}$, $Z=92$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 92. Its long-lived radioactive isotopes illustrate actinide production by the <rapid neutron-capture process>.

= Lead
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Pb}$, $Z=82$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 82. Lead isotopes are important heavy products of the <slow neutron-capture process> in suitable neutron-exposure conditions.

= Barium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ba}$, $Z=56$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 56. Enrichment in barium can reveal transferred <slow neutron-capture process> products, as in a <barium star>.

= Silicon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Si}$, $Z=14$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 14. <Oxygen burning> produces silicon-group nuclei; subsequent <silicon burning> is a reaction network approaching an iron-group mixture.

= Magnesium
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Mg}$, $Z=12$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 12. Magnesium isotopes occur among products of <carbon burning> and alpha-capture neutron-source reactions.

= Neon
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ne}$, $Z=10$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 10. <Neon burning> uses photodisintegration and alpha capture, while neon-22 can provide neutrons through $^{22}\mathrm{Ne}(\alpha,n){}^{25}\mathrm{Mg}$.

= Cobalt
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Co}$, $Z=27$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 27. Cobalt-56 is the intermediate radioactive nucleus in the <nickel-56 decay chain>.

= Nickel
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Ni}$, $Z=28$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 28. Radioactive nickel-56 is produced in explosive burning; the <nickel-56 decay chain> contributes energy to supernova emission.

= Nickel-56 decay chain
{parent=Nickel}
{title2=$^{56}\mathrm{Ni}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Co}\longrightarrow{}^{56}\mathrm{Fe}$}

The radioactive chain converts nickel-56 through cobalt-56 into iron-56, releasing energy that can power supernova light curves. <Electron capture> and, where allowed, <beta-plus decay> convert protons into neutrons; gamma rays and charged decay products deposit some of the released energy. Ionization and escape of decay radiation affect the observed heating.

= Iron
{parent=Chemical element}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{Fe}$, $Z=26$}

The <chemical element> with atomic number 26. Iron-group <nuclear binding energy> and <silicon burning> are central to the termination of ordinary energy-producing stellar fusion.

= Oxygen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

= Oxygen-16
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$^{16}\mathrm O$}

= Molecular oxygen
{parent=Oxygen}
{title2=$\mathrm O_2$}

The diatomic form of <oxygen>. Modern terrestrial abundance is maintained largely by oxygenic <photosynthesis>, balanced by chemical and biological sinks.

= Nitrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm N$}
{wiki}

= Carbon
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-12
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{12}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

= Carbon-13
{parent=Carbon}
{title2=$^{13}\mathrm C$}
{wiki}

The <carbon> <isotope> with six <protons> and seven <neutrons>. A neutral <carbon-13> <chemical atom> has six <electrons>.

= Helium
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

= Helium-3
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^3\mathrm{He}$}
{wiki}

This stable <helium> isotope contains two <protons> and one <neutron>. In solar proton-proton burning it is an intermediate: two such nuclei can form <helium-4> and return two <protons>, or one can capture <helium-4> to enter the second chain branch. The balance between production and destruction determines the <helium-3 equilibrium abundance> and its <helium-3 relaxation time>.

= Helium-4
{parent=Helium}
{title2=$^4\mathrm{He}$}

Helium-4 is the stable helium isotope with two protons and two neutrons; its nucleus is an alpha particle. Its strong binding makes it the principal neutron-containing product of primordial <Big Bang nucleosynthesis>.

= Hydrogen
{parent=Chemical element}
{title2=$\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

= Metallic hydrogen
{parent=Hydrogen}
{wiki}

Metallic hydrogen is a high-pressure conducting state of <hydrogen>. Its fluid form in the deep interiors of <gas giants> supplies a conducting medium for a <planetary dynamo>, unlike the iron-alloy conducting cores of <terrestrial planets>.

= Deuterium
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$^2\mathrm H$}
{wiki}

This stable <hydrogen> isotope has one <proton> and one <neutron>. In the <Proton–proton chain> it is produced by the slow weak proton-pair reaction and is rapidly consumed by <proton> capture to make <helium-3>. Its short nuclear lifetime inside a stellar core allows a <deuterium quasi-equilibrium in proton-proton burning> approximation.

= Hydrogen anion
{parent=Hydrogen}
{title2=$\mathrm H^-$}
{wiki}

= Negative hydrogen ion
{synonym}

A negative hydrogen ion is a <hydrogen> nucleus with two bound electrons. Its weakly bound extra electron permits absorption at visible and infrared wavelengths in cool stellar atmospheres.

= Molecular nitrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{N}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Molecular hydrogen
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2$}
{parent=Chemical substance}

= Chemical compound
{parent=Chemical substance}
{wiki}

= Benzene
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_6$}

<Benzene> has a planar ring of six <carbon> <chemical atoms>, each bearing one <hydrogen>. Its $\pi$ <electron density> is distributed over the ring; the alternating <covalent bonds> in one <resonance structure> do not describe alternating actual <bond lengths>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/15-2-structure-and-stability-of-benzene[McMurry: benzene structure]{ref}

= Phenol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm C_6\mathrm H_5\mathrm{OH}$}

<Phenol> is <benzene> with one ring <hydrogen> replaced by a <hydroxy group>. That O–H group supplies the <hydrogen bond donor> in the experiment under <hydrogen bond acceptor>. https://openstax.org/books/organic-chemistry/pages/17-1-naming-alcohols-and-phenols[McMurry: phenol naming and structures]{ref}

= Sodium chloride
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{NaCl}$}

<Sodium chloride> contains <sodium> <cations> $\mathrm{Na}^+$ and <chlorine> <anions> $\mathrm{Cl}^-$ in a $1:1$ ratio; its <formula unit> has zero net <electric charge>.

= Glycerol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_3\mathrm{H}_8\mathrm{O}_3$}

<Glycerol> has three <carbon> <chemical atoms> and three $\mathrm{OH}$ groups. It supplies the scaffold of <triglycerides> and <glycerophospholipids>, but is not itself a long <hydrocarbon> chain. See https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/3-3-lipids[Biology 2e: glycerol]{ref}.

= Hydrocarbon
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

A <hydrocarbon> contains only <carbon> and <hydrogen>. A <fatty acid> also contains <oxygen>; calling its long chain a <hydrocarbon> region does not make the whole <fatty acid> a <hydrocarbon>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02889[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrocarbons]{ref}.

= Ester
{parent=Chemical compound}

The <carboxylic acid>-derived <ester> linkages in a <triglyceride> or typical <glycerophospholipid> have the form $\mathrm{R{-}C(=O){-}O{-}R'}$. Here the <acyl group> is attached through <oxygen> to the <glycerol>-derived part. See https://iupac.qmul.ac.uk/BlueBook/P6.html[IUPAC Blue Book, P-65.6: esters]{ref}.

= Choline
{parent=Chemical compound}

= Disulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Thiol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{R-SH}$}
{wiki}

A <thiol> is a <chemical compound> of the form $\mathrm{R-SH}$, with an organic group $R$ attached to <sulfur>. The <conjugate base> is $\mathrm{R-S^-}$; <cysteine> and <mercaptoethanol> supply examples of <thiol> groups. Two <thiol> groups can undergo <oxidation> to form a <disulfide bond>. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/T06359[IUPAC Gold Book: thiols]{ref}

= Ellman's reagent
{c}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= DTNB
{c}
{synonym}

<Ellman's reagent> is 5,5'-dithiobis(2-nitrobenzoic acid), abbreviated <DTNB>, a <disulfide> used to quantify accessible <thiols>. Reaction releases the colored TNB species measured by <absorbance> at <wavelength> $412\,\mathrm{nm}$. The <disulfide bond> experiment above explains why a <thiol> measurement can become an indirect bridge count only after controlled <reduction>. https://assets.thermofisher.com/TFS-Assets/LSG/manuals/MAN0011216_Ellmans_Reag_UG.pdf[Pierce Biotechnology: identity and use of Ellman's reagent]{ref}

= Urea
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$}
{wiki}

Urea is the <chemical compound> with <molecular formula> $\mathrm{CH}_4\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$ and structure $(\mathrm{NH}_2)_2\mathrm{CO}$. See the https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C57136&Mask=20[NIST Chemistry WebBook, urea: formula and structure]{ref}. Concentrated urea is used to promote <protein denaturation> in the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>.

= 2-Mercaptoethanol
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$}
{wiki}

= Mercaptoethanol
{synonym}

2-Mercaptoethanol is the <chemical compound> $\mathrm{HOCH}_2\mathrm{CH}_2\mathrm{SH}$; see the https://webbook.nist.gov/cgi/cbook.cgi?ID=C60242&Units=SI[NIST Chemistry WebBook, 2-mercaptoethanol: structure and names]{ref}. In the <ribonuclease A refolding experiment>, it breaks <protein disulfide bonds>, while the <peptide bonds> of the <protein backbone> remain intact. This separates a change in crosslinks from cutting the <polypeptide chain>.

= Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

<Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate> is a <cofactor> used in <metabolism>. Its oxidized and reduced forms, <NADP+> and <NADPH>, carry <electrons> between coupled <chemical reactions>. In <oxygenic photosynthesis>, <light-dependent reactions> produce <NADPH> for the <Calvin cycle>, which returns <NADP+> for reuse. https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/8-3-using-light-energy-to-make-organic-molecules[OpenStax Biology 2e, 8.3: recycling NADP+ and NADPH]{ref}.

= NADP+
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADP}^{+}$}

= Oxidized NADP
{synonym}

<NADP+> is the oxidized form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It can accept <electrons> to form <NADPH>; the <light-dependent reactions> supply this conversion during <oxygenic photosynthesis>.

= NADPH
{c}
{parent=Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate}
{title2=$\mathrm{NADPH}$}

<NADPH> is the reduced form of <nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide phosphate>. It carries <electrons> used in synthesis; in the <Calvin cycle> it becomes the oxidized form <NADP+>, available for reuse by <light-dependent reactions>. <NADPH> and <ATP> serve different roles even though both connect <light> capture to <carbon fixation>.

= Chloromethane
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_3\mathrm{Cl}$}
{wiki}

= Methyl chloride
{synonym}

An organochlorine gas with biological as well as possible nonbiological sources. Its specialized biological production is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example.

= Dimethyl sulfide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$(\mathrm{CH}_3)_2\mathrm S$}
{wiki}

= DMS
{c}
{synonym}

An organic sulfur gas emitted through biological processing, including marine production associated with sulfur-containing metabolites. It is a <secondary metabolic byproduct> example and a possible <atmospheric biosignature gas> whose detectability depends on production and photochemical destruction.

= Ozone
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm O_3$}
{wiki}

A triatomic form of <oxygen>, produced photochemically from <molecular oxygen>. The <ozone layer> absorbs ultraviolet radiation; ozone can trace atmospheric oxygen without being directly emitted by metabolism.

= Nitrous oxide
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm N_2\mathrm O$}
{wiki}

A nitrogen oxide produced substantially by microbial nitrogen cycling on modern <Earth>. Its atmospheric abundance and lifetime make it a candidate <atmospheric biosignature gas>, with context-dependent abiotic alternatives.

= Phosphine
{parent=Chemical compound}
{title2=$\mathrm{PH}_3$}
{wiki}

Phosphine is $\mathrm{PH}_3$. Its abundance in giant-planet observable atmospheres can depend on transport from deeper layers faster than local chemical destruction.

= Vanadium(II) oxide
{title2=$\mathrm{VO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Vanadium monoxide
{synonym}

= Titanium monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{TiO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Acetylene
{title2=$\mathrm{C}_2\mathrm{H}_2$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Hydrogen cyanide
{title2=$\mathrm{HCN}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Carbon dioxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}_2$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= CO2
{c}
{synonym}

= Carbon monoxide
{title2=$\mathrm{CO}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Ammonia
{title2=$\mathrm{NH}_3$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methane
{title2=$\mathrm{CH}_4$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Methanogenesis
{parent=Methane}
{wiki}

Methanogenesis is biological production of <methane> by anaerobic microorganisms. Waterlogged soil and lake sediments can favour it. Warming or thaw changes substrate availability, but production is distinct from atmospheric emission because oxidation and transport intervene.

= Water
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}$}
{parent=Chemical compound}
{wiki}

= Self-ionization of water
{parent=Water}
{wiki}

Two <water> <molecules> can exchange a <proton>, producing <hydronium> and <hydroxide> <ions>: $2\mathrm H_2\mathrm O\rightleftharpoons\mathrm H_3\mathrm O^++\mathrm{OH}^-$. Even pure <water> therefore supplies <ions>; extreme <dilution> eventually invalidates the <strong acid> assumption that it supplies all the <hydronium>.

= Ice
{parent=Water}
{title2=$\mathrm{H}_2\mathrm{O}(s)$}
{wiki}

= Snowflake
{parent=Ice}
{wiki}

A snowflake is an <ice> crystal or an aggregate of <ice> crystals grown in atmospheric conditions. Its morphology depends on vapour supersaturation, transport, <temperature> and crystal anisotropy. Not every snowflake is dendritic.

= Dendritic growth of a snowflake
{parent=Snowflake}

An exposed tip receives vapour more efficiently than a sheltered surface and can amplify its lead, producing <crystal dendrites> and side branches. The planar thermal <solidification> mechanism is an analogy, not a complete vapour-growth model. The <hexagonal ice crystal anisotropy> selects six preferred directions; an isotropic linear instability alone does not select six arms.

= Steady state
{disambiguate=chemistry}
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

A <steady state (chemistry)> has time-independent concentrations because formation and removal rates balance for the species considered. Nonzero reaction fluxes can continue, so a steady state need not be <chemical equilibrium>. A <quasi-steady-state approximation> imposes this balance approximately for a fast intermediate while slower species evolve.

= Physical chemistry
{parent=Chemistry}
{wiki}

= Chemical polarity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

<Chemical polarity> concerns unequal distribution of <electron density> in a <chemical bond> or <molecule>. A <polar molecule> has a permanent <electric dipole moment>; the effects of individual bonds must be combined with their spatial arrangement. In a symmetric linear <carbon dioxide> <molecule>, opposing bond-dipole contributions cancel. In <water>, its <bent molecular geometry> prevents that cancellation, giving a permanent <electric dipole moment>.https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/7-6-molecular-structure-and-polarity[Chemistry 2e, 7.6: geometry and molecular polarity]{ref}

= Polar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

A <polar molecule> has a nonzero permanent <electric dipole moment> because of its internal <electric charge> distribution. It can still have zero total <electric charge>. <Water> is polar; a <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>.

= Nonpolar molecule
{parent=Chemical polarity}

= Nonpolar
{synonym}

A <nonpolar molecule> has no permanent <electric dipole moment>. This distinguishes its charge distribution from the separated partial charges of a polar <molecule>.

= Hydrophilicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophilic
{synonym}

<Hydrophilicity> describes affinity for <water>. Regions with <chemical polarity> or <electric charge> can interact favorably with <water>, including through <hydrogen bonds> where suitable groups are present. <Hydrophilic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the <water> on either side of a <lipid bilayer>. Such affinity does not mean forming <covalent bonds> with every neighboring substance, nor does it require a net negative <electric charge>. See https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK26883/[Alberts et al.: the chemical components of a cell]{ref}.

= Amphiphile
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Amphiphilic
{synonym}

= Amphipathic
{synonym}

An <amphiphile> is a <molecule> containing distinct <hydrophilic> and <hydrophobic> regions. <Amphiphilic> and <amphipathic> describe this combination. Membrane-forming <phospholipids> are examples; their two regions give different parts of one <molecule> different affinities for <water>. The word describes a structure, not a guarantee of a particular aggregate shape. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/A00303[IUPAC Gold Book: amphiphilic]{ref}.

= Hydrophobicity
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{wiki}

= Hydrophobic
{synonym}

<Hydrophobicity> describes unfavorable mixing with <water>, as illustrated by long <hydrocarbon> regions. The <hydrophobic> regions of membrane-forming <phospholipids> face the interior of a <lipid bilayer>. This organization is explained by the <hydrophobic effect>, rather than by imagining that chains actively repel <water>. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction]{ref}.

= Hydrophobic effect
{parent=Hydrophobicity}
{wiki}

The <hydrophobic effect> is the solvent-mediated tendency of <nonpolar> regions to associate in <water>. Exposing a <hydrocarbon> region changes how surrounding <water> <molecules> can arrange and interact; reducing that exposure can lower the total <free energy>. Both <water> and <lipids> enter the accounting. The effect is not an additional <covalent bond> between chains. See https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/H02907[IUPAC Gold Book: hydrophobic interaction]{ref}.

At fixed <temperature> and <pressure>, compare two candidate organizations by the change in <Gibbs free energy>, $\Delta G=\Delta H-T\Delta S$, where $H$ is <enthalpy>, $T$ is absolute <temperature>, and $S$ is <entropy>. An organization with lower $G$ is favored in this comparison. Reducing exposed <hydrocarbon> area is one contribution; changes in chain packing and the <entropy> of the <lipids> also matter. This explains why <hydrophobicity> alone cannot predict every aggregate shape. See <Chemistry 2e by Paul Flowers and others/16.4 Free Energy>.

= Absorbance
{parent=Physical chemistry}
{title2=$A$}
{wiki}

<Absorbance> is the <dimensionless> quantity $A=\log_{10}(P_0/P)$, where $P_0$ and $P$ are reference and transmitted radiant <powers> at the chosen <wavelength>. For example, $A=1$ means one tenth of the reference <power> is transmitted. Under the <Beer-Lambert law>, <absorbance> can determine an absorbing species' <concentration>; attenuation by cloudiness or unrelated colored <solutes> needs separate controls. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00626[IUPAC Gold Book: absorbance and its concentration relation]{ref}

= Beer-Lambert law
{c}
{parent=Absorbance}
{title2=$A=\varepsilon lc$}
{wiki=Beer–Lambert_law}

The <Beer-Lambert law> relates <absorbance> $A$ to an absorbing species' <concentration> $c$ and path length $l$ by $A=\varepsilon lc$ in a homogeneous medium using sufficiently narrow-band light. The coefficient $\varepsilon$ depends on the species, <wavelength> and <chemical solution> conditions. This makes <Ellman's reagent> useful for quantitative <thiol> assays once its released colored species has been calibrated. https://goldbook.iupac.org/terms/view/B00626[IUPAC Gold Book: Beer-Lambert law and validity conditions]{ref}

